window.SMS_DOC_INDEX=[{"title":"Simple Media System","cat":"Start","url":"index.html","text":"Simple Media System for PlayStation 2: current downloads, supported media, storage, converter and open playback issues."},{"title":"The maintained SMS fork","cat":"Start","url":"index.html#whats-new","text":"SMS was created by Eugene Plotnikov and developed by the PS2 homebrew community. This storage-focused fork adds MX4SIO, ATA/exFAT, i.LINK and MMCE paths, a folder-served UDPFS network drive, on-screen network configuration, USB/Bluetooth DualShock 3/4 input and updated artwork. You need a PS2 that can already launch homebrew; a hard disk or network adapter is optional. The October development work adds folder shuffle, converter multi-audio output and text-subtitle burn-in, and fixes reproduced parser, streaming and frame-lifetime defects. Known issues distinguishes implemented fixes from the reports still awaiting affected-console tests. Poster-grid browsing remains a feature request."},{"title":"Choose your next step","cat":"Start","url":"index.html#sections","text":"Simple Media System for PlayStation 2: current downloads, supported media, storage, converter and open playback issues."},{"title":"First playback","cat":"Start","url":"index.html#first-playback","text":"Install, enable a source and open a file."},{"title":"Prepare media","cat":"Start","url":"index.html#prepare-media","text":"Presets, tracks, subtitles and output validation."},{"title":"Choose storage","cat":"Start","url":"index.html#choose-storage","text":"Device names, setup and driver exclusions."},{"title":"Keep your settings","cat":"Start","url":"index.html#keep-your-settings","text":"Boot-folder config, network files and fallback paths."},{"title":"Check current issues","cat":"Start","url":"index.html#check-current-issues","text":"Open reports, tested scope and useful reproductions."},{"title":"Browse the wiki","cat":"Start","url":"index.html#browse-the-wiki","text":"The complete guide and reference map."},{"title":"Formats and limits","cat":"Start","url":"index.html#playback-spec","text":"Media Current routes Video MPEG-4 Part 2 / Xvid / DivX in AVI; mp4v in MP4; MPEG-1/2 in MPEG program streams. Format details . Music MP2/MP3/MPA, AAC/M4A, AC-3, FLAC, Ogg Vorbis and WMA v1/v2. Audio constraints . Pictures and text Baseline JPEG, M3U playlists and external SRT/SUB/TXT subtitles. Existing AVI DXSB bitmap subtitles are a separate path. Video dimensions At most 1024 pixels on either axis. This is an acceptance check, not a real-time playback guarantee. Files Keep each file below 4 GiB on every source, including exFAT. Tracks Eight stream-table slots; MP4 and MPEG-PS retain at most seven streams to reserve a terminator. The converter allows video plus six audio tracks. Unsupported video H.264/AVC, H.265/HEVC, VP9, AV1 and WMV. An MP4 extension alone does not identify the video codec. Earlier project documentation records successful standard-definition fixture playback. Those are useful starting profiles; generating the test-media pack in CI does not retest a console. High bitrate, resolution, audio workload and source throughput still matter."},{"title":"Start with a small, known file","cat":"Start","url":"index.html#at-a-glance","text":"Download and launch the standard ELF with your homebrew loader. Use a supported existing FAT32/exFAT volume, copy a small MP3 or conservative video onto it, and enable that source in Device settings . Pick the source, press Down to enter its files and Cross to open a file. Start opens settings from the browser; Triangle stops playback. If it fails, keep the file unchanged and follow the reporting checklist . Read the controls , settings reference and language guide when you are ready to customize. Credits and preserved historical manuals retain the project's lineage."},{"title":"Getting Started","cat":"Start","url":"getting-started.html","text":"Play your first SMS file: install, enable storage, navigate the device browser and open supported media."},{"title":"The SMS screen","cat":"Start","url":"getting-started.html#the-screen","text":"The SMS browser is split into three stacked areas, each drawn inside its own rounded rectangle. Whichever area you are currently working in is highlighted. Learn these three and the whole interface makes sense."},{"title":"1 · Device menu — top row","cat":"Start","url":"getting-started.html#1-device-menu-top-row","text":"A horizontal row of device icons across the top: one icon for each storage source SMS has detected (USB, MX4SIO, the internal HDD, MMCE, a network share, a CD/DVD, and so on). This is where you choose which device to browse."},{"title":"2 · File list — middle","cat":"Start","url":"getting-started.html#2-file-list-middle","text":"The file browser for the device you picked: the folders and playable files on it. Directories, files and device roots use different icons so you can tell them apart at a glance. This is where you find and open your media."},{"title":"3 · Status line — bottom","cat":"Start","url":"getting-started.html#3-status-line-bottom","text":"A thin bar at the bottom. The current path (where you are in the filesystem) is shown on the left, and a clock sits on the right. Watch the path here to keep track of how deep into a folder tree you have gone. ℹ How SMS browses SMS reads the raw filesystem of a device and plays media wherever it happens to sit. There is no mandatory folder layout to follow and nothing special to name your files — drop a .avi or .mp3 anywhere on the stick and SMS will list it. Only supported media files (and folders) are shown in the browser."},{"title":"Navigating with the pad","cat":"Start","url":"getting-started.html#navigating","text":"Everything in the browser is done with the controller. The table below covers the buttons you need. The device menu and the file list each respond a little differently, so both are noted. Button In the device menu In the file list Left / Right Move between device icons Jump back up to the device menu Down Enter the file list of the selected device Move down one item Up — Move up one item Cross ✕ Select the highlighted device and load its file list Open the selected folder, or play the selected file Triangle △ — Go up one level (folder → parent → back to the device list) L1 / L2 — Page-scroll a long list up / down Select + Circle ○ Power the PS2 off from the browser In short: Left/Right picks a device, Down drops into its files, Up/Down (and L1/L2 for whole pages) moves through the list, Cross opens or plays, and Triangle backs out one level at a time."},{"title":"Play your first file","cat":"Start","url":"getting-started.html#first-file","text":"The quickest path to a working playback is a USB stick, because almost every PS2 has a front USB port and nothing needs configuring first. Here it is end to end."},{"title":"1 · Format the stick","cat":"Start","url":"getting-started.html#1-format-the-stick","text":"Format a USB flash drive as FAT32 or exFAT on your computer. Either works."},{"title":"2 · Copy media onto it","cat":"Start","url":"getting-started.html#2-copy-media-onto-it","text":"Copy a supported file to the stick — a .avi (DivX / Xvid / MPEG-4 Part 2), .mp4 ( mp4v , not H.264/H.265), .mpg video, or an .mp3 song is a safe first test. You can put it in any folder, or just at the top level. See Video Playback and Audio & Playlists for the full format lists."},{"title":"3 · Plug in and boot SMS","cat":"Start","url":"getting-started.html#3-plug-in-and-boot-sms","text":"Insert the stick into the PS2's USB port and launch SMS."},{"title":"4 · Enable USB","cat":"Start","url":"getting-started.html#4-enable-usb","text":"On a clean install SMS defaults optional Autostart flags to off — you turn on what you need. Open the settings menu and either turn on Autostart USB (so USB starts every boot from now on) or use the Start USB now item to bring it up immediately without rebooting. A USB device icon then appears in the device menu. See USB and the Settings Reference for more."},{"title":"5 · Pick USB in the device menu","cat":"Start","url":"getting-started.html#5-pick-usb-in-the-device-menu","text":"Use Left/Right along the top row to highlight the USB device (it appears as a massN: device — mass0: if it's the only mass-storage device connected), press Cross to select it, then Down to enter its file list."},{"title":"6 · Browse to your file","cat":"Start","url":"getting-started.html#6-browse-to-your-file","text":"Use Up/Down (and L1/L2 to page through a long list) to move through the files and folders. Press Cross to open a folder; press Triangle to go back up if you take a wrong turn. The status line at the bottom shows where you are."},{"title":"7 · Press Cross to play","cat":"Start","url":"getting-started.html#7-press-cross-to-play","text":"Highlight your file and press Cross . SMS loads it and playback begins. That's it — you're watching a movie (or listening to music) on a stock PS2. Nothing shows up in the list? If the file list is empty after entering the device, the file may not be in a format SMS recognises, or USB may not have started. Double-check the device icon is present in the top row, and confirm the file's extension is one SMS supports. The Troubleshooting page walks through the common causes."},{"title":"Playback controls","cat":"Start","url":"getting-started.html#playback-controls","text":"Once a file is playing, a small set of buttons controls it. These are the essentials to remember: Button During playback Select Pause Start Resume Triangle △ Stop and return to the file browser So: Select to pause, Start to carry on, and Triangle to stop and go back to browsing. Video playback has more on offer — seeking, subtitles, aspect-ratio modes and an in-player menu — which are covered on the Video Playback and Subtitles pages."},{"title":"Powering off","cat":"Start","url":"getting-started.html#power-off","text":"When you're done, you can turn the console off straight from SMS. In the file browser, press Select + Circle together and the PS2 powers down. You don't need to exit to the PS2 browser first."},{"title":"Where to go next","cat":"Start","url":"getting-started.html#next","text":"You've got the interface and a first file playing. From here, pick the path that matches your setup. Devices Overview Every storage source SMS reads MX4SIO Play from a microSD card Internal HDD APA & exFAT drives MMCE SD2PSX / MemCard PRO2 SMB Network Stream from a PC share UDPFS Browse a PC folder Settings Reference Autostart toggles & options Video Playback Formats & player controls Subtitles SRT / SUB over video Troubleshooting When something won't play Why is nothing enabled when I first boot SMS? By design. On a clean install SMS defaults optional Autostart choices to off — you switch on only the devices you actually use, either permanently with the per-device Autostart toggles in settings, or on demand with the Start \u003cdevice> now items. This keeps startup fast and avoids probing hardware you don't have. Your choices are saved with the rest of your settings, so once you've turned USB (or any device) on, it comes back the next time you boot. See the Settings Reference for the full list of toggles. Do my files need to be in a special folder? No. SMS is a media player, not a game loader — it browses the raw filesystem of the device and plays media files wherever they sit. There is no required folder tree, no cover-art directory and no special naming scheme. Put your videos, music and pictures wherever is convenient and browse to them with the pad. Boot-source exception: optional Autostart flags default off, but SMS may start/remount the device holding its ELF/config during startup. A source already visible after first boot does not mean another Autostart choice was saved. See configuration discovery ."},{"title":"Install & First Boot","cat":"Start","url":"install.html","text":"Install and update SMS using standard, packed or diagnostic ELFs, with the correct build identity and settings location."},{"title":"What you need","cat":"Start","url":"install.html#what-you-need","text":"SMS is homebrew: it is an unsigned program, so your console needs to be able to run unsigned programs. It does not modify your PS2, install anything to the system, or require a modchip — it just needs a way in. A PS2 that can run homebrew. Any of the usual routes works — a softmod such as FreeMcBoot on a memory card, FreeDVDBoot from a burned disc, or any other exploit/loader that lets you launch an ELF. If you already run homebrew on your PS2, you are ready. The SMS build — the SMS.elf file itself. See Getting SMS below. A media device to play from. The simplest is a FAT32 or exFAT USB flash drive ; if you want to use MX4SIO, the internal HDD, i.LINK, MMCE, SMB or UDPFS instead, each is covered under Devices . On a very early / fat PS2 you can also just burn media to CD/DVD. A controller to drive the menus: DualShock 2, or DualShock 3/4 over USB or Bluetooth. A memory card is optional throughout: settings follow the boot device, and with no card fitted SMS writes IPCONFIG.DAT next to the ELF too. ℹ DualShock 3 / 4 Wired DS3/DS4 support uses USB and is loaded automatically. For Bluetooth, connect a compatible USB Bluetooth dongle, plug the controller into USB, choose Device settings → Pair Bluetooth controller , then unplug the cable and power the controller on. These USB controller paths do not contend with MX4SIO on SIO2. Homebrew access is up to you This guide assumes your console can already launch homebrew. Setting up FreeMcBoot, FreeDVDBoot or another exploit is a separate, well-documented topic that depends on your exact console model and region — follow a current guide for your PS2. SMS itself asks nothing special of your console beyond \"can run an ELF.\""},{"title":"Getting SMS","cat":"Start","url":"install.html#getting-sms","text":"You need one file: the compiled SMS.elf . Grab it from the project's builds — the Releases & Changelog page tracks the available downloads. If you would rather build the ELF yourself from the source tree, the Building from Source page walks through the Docker toolchain and make . The IOP driver modules SMS needs (USB, MX4SIO, HDD, MMCE, network and the rest) are embedded inside the ELF , so a single file is genuinely all you copy — there are no loose .irx files to place beside it. ℹ One file, many names You can rename the ELF to suit your loader (some setups expect BOOT.ELF on a memory card, for example). The program is the same regardless of the filename."},{"title":"Running the ELF","cat":"Start","url":"install.html#running-it","text":"SMS launches like any other PS2 homebrew ELF. Use whichever method your console is already set up for — the following are the common ones, not an exhaustive list. If your loader can point at an ELF, it can launch SMS."},{"title":"From a memory card via FreeMcBoot / OSDSYS","cat":"Start","url":"install.html#from-a-memory-card-via-freemcboot-osdsys","text":"If your memory card runs FreeMcBoot , copy SMS.elf to the card (via a memory-card manager on the PS2, or an FTP/USB transfer tool) and add it to your FreeMcBoot / OSDSYS launch menu, or drop it where your menu already looks for ELFs. Then pick it from the boot menu at power-on. This is the most convenient setup — SMS is available every time you turn the console on, no disc required."},{"title":"With wLaunchELF (uLaunchELF) off USB or a memory card","cat":"Start","url":"install.html#with-wlaunchelf-ulaunchelf-off-usb-or-a-memory-c","text":"wLaunchELF (uLaunchELF / uLE) is the community file-manager-and-launcher most homebrew users already have. Copy SMS.elf to a USB drive or memory card, open uLaunchELF, browse to the file ( mass: for USB, mc0: / mc1: for a memory card) and press the launch/confirm button on it. uLaunchELF loads the ELF and SMS starts. There are legacy walk-through screenshots of this flow in the project's image set."},{"title":"Launched by another loader or as an auto-boot ELF","cat":"Start","url":"install.html#launched-by-another-loader-or-as-an-auto-boot-el","text":"Any launcher that can run an arbitrary ELF can start SMS — some people wire it into their boot menu so it runs automatically, or launch it from another homebrew front-end. As long as the loader hands control to SMS.elf , SMS behaves the same. Where SMS then saves its settings depends on where it was booted from — see Next steps ."},{"title":"From a burned disc (older consoles)","cat":"Start","url":"install.html#from-a-burned-disc-older-consoles","text":"On consoles set up for it, SMS can also be run from optical media (for example via FreeDVDBoot or a boot disc), which is handy on an early PS2 with no other storage. Preparing a bootable disc is a console-specific process; once it boots, the SMS experience is identical. ℹ Legacy install screenshots The project keeps older step-by-step images (uLaunchELF, CD burning, FTP transfer) under docs/images/ — files named installation-*.png . They predate this fork's UI refresh but still illustrate the general launch-from-USB and copy-to-card flows."},{"title":"First boot — what you see","cat":"Start","url":"install.html#first-boot","text":"When SMS starts you land on its desktop : a full-screen background with three bordered areas. The DEVICE menu across the top — a row of device icons (USB, MX4SIO, HDD, MMCE, network and so on). The FILE menu in the middle — the file browser / list for whatever device is selected. The STATUS line along the bottom — the current path on the left and a clock on the right. The active area is highlighted; each area sits inside its own rounded rectangle. You move between the device row and the file list with the D-pad — full navigation is covered in Getting Started and the Devices Overview . On a clean install, optional Autostart choices are off Out of the box SMS starts with no storage devices running — the device row will look sparse until you turn something on. This is deliberate: you enable exactly the devices you use. Open the settings menu and flip the matching Autostart toggle (USB, MX4SIO, HDD, i.LINK, MMCE, network or UDPFS), or use its Start … item to bring it up on the spot without a reboot. Once a device is started it appears in the top row and you can browse into it. Full details are on the Settings Reference . Three resource pairs — choose one from each MX4SIO and MMCE share SIO2; the internal HDD's APA/PFS and exFAT/BDM paths share ATA; and SMB/host and UDPFS load different stacks for the one Ethernet controller. Whichever starts first owns the shared resource for that boot. See Devices Overview for the specifics."},{"title":"Preparing a media device","cat":"Start","url":"install.html#prepare-device","text":"SMS is a media player that browses the raw filesystem of whatever device you point it at and plays media files wherever they sit. There is no required folder structure — no special \"movies\" or \"cover-art\" tree to build. You just put files on a drive and browse to them."},{"title":"Format the drive FAT32 or exFAT","cat":"Start","url":"install.html#format-the-drive-fat32-or-exfat","text":"A standard FAT32 or exFAT USB flash drive or microSD works. Format it on your PC the normal way; no special partitioning is needed for USB or MX4SIO. (The internal APA HDD is the exception — that path uses the PS2's own partition scheme; see Internal HDD .)"},{"title":"Copy your media anywhere on it","cat":"Start","url":"install.html#copy-your-media-anywhere-on-it","text":"Drop your videos, music and pictures onto the drive in whatever folders you like. SMS recognises files by extension — AVI/DivX/Xvid, mp4v MP4 and MPG/MPEG for video; MP2/MP3, AAC, AC3, FLAC, OGG and WMA for audio; JPEG for pictures; plus M3U playlists and external SRT/SUB subtitles. The full list is on the Video and Audio pages."},{"title":"Plug it in and start the device in SMS","cat":"Start","url":"install.html#plug-it-in-and-start-the-device-in-sms","text":"Connect the drive, then start the matching device from the SMS device menu or its Autostart toggle. Your files appear in the browser, ready to open with Cross . That is it — no import step, no database. ℹ Device names you'll see BDM mass-storage devices (USB, MX4SIO, exFAT HDD, i.LINK) all appear as massN: where N is 0, 1, 2… MMCE units show as mmce0: / mmce1: , the internal APA HDD as hdd0: , UDPFS as udpfs: , and optical discs via cdfs . You rarely need to type these — you pick devices by their icon — but they show up in the status line's path."},{"title":"Next steps","cat":"Start","url":"install.html#next-steps","text":"SMS is running and a device is prepared — now go play something and tune the setup: Getting Started Play your first file, end to end Devices Overview Every storage source explained Settings Reference Autostart toggles & options MX4SIO microSD playback — the headline Network Setup On-screen IP, SMB & UDPFS Releases Download the latest ELF Where do my settings get saved? Use Save settings after changes. On a remountable launch source, SMS.cfg normally lives beside the ELF even when a memory card is fitted; SMS.smb follows its resolved directory. See Config Files & Paths for card-slot, network-file and fallback rules. Do I have to build my media into an OPL-style folder tree? No. SMS is not OPL — there are no ISOs, no VMC , no ul.* files and no coverart folders. It plays whatever media files it finds, wherever they are on the drive. Just copy your files on and browse to them. Boot-source exception: optional Autostart flags default off, but SMS may start/remount the device holding its ELF/config during startup. A source already visible after first boot does not mean another Autostart choice was saved. See configuration discovery ."},{"title":"Downloads & Releases","cat":"Start","url":"releases.html","text":"SMS rolling versus stable downloads, player and converter packages, build manifests, hashes and synthetic test media."},{"title":"Choose your build","cat":"Start","url":"releases.html#getting-a-build","text":"Channel Use it for Identity Rolling development Current fixes, features and issue retests. Updated by successful master publication. The release notes' commit and the tester bundle's build-info.json . v3.0.0-rev1 stable tag The fixed July 2026 release. It predates the October fixes and converter additions. Tag/source 95b803ba07d1c55aa644acbd633936eb0a1e6ad6 ; published 2026-07-26 UTC. Actions / PR artifacts Testing a particular unmerged candidate. Exact run, artifact and manifest head/source SHA. A rolling download cannot test an unmerged PR. The latest stable release link excludes prereleases, so it can still lead to July while rolling contains much newer work."},{"title":"Rolling download contents","cat":"Start","url":"releases.html#whats-in-a-release","text":"File Purpose Simple-Media-System.zip Standard, packed and DIAG ELFs, build manifest and SMB component notices. Simple-Media-System.elf Standard player; use this for ordinary playback and first comparisons. Simple-Media-System-packed.elf The same player in a self-extracting executable. Use the standard ELF if your loader has trouble with packed files. Simple-Media-System-DIAG.elf On-screen startup/probe labels for reporting freezes. Record the number and label. Simple-Media-System-languages.zip Language variants matching this build's string table, including Spanish. See installation . SMS-Media-Converter.exe Windows 10/11 converter. SMS-Media-Converter-Win7.exe Python 3.8 / Qt5 build for Windows 7. Packaging CI runs on a current Windows runner; it is not live Win7 acceptance. SMS-Media-Converter-Linux.zip Linux source/application package; follow the included Python/PySide instructions. FFmpeg/FFprobe are separate tools. Embedded IOP modules are carried inside the player ELF; normal installation does not require copying loose IRX files. Keep matching translations with each build you compare."},{"title":"What the current development line contains","cat":"Start","url":"releases.html#this-build","text":"PR #30 merged on 2026-10-27 as 4a6959a . It includes MPEG-PS/MOV probing and progress fixes, stream failure recovery, MPEG RGB-frame ownership and shutdown cleanup, folder shuffle, Spanish integration and converter controls. See the open acceptance work before treating a reported issue as resolved."},{"title":"Verify the file you actually downloaded","cat":"Start","url":"releases.html#release-hashes","text":"Rolling assets are replaced over time. Read build-info.json inside the tester ZIP and keep it with your test report; it contains source/head commits, the Actions run and ELF SHA-256 hashes. Compare a downloaded ELF with those hashes, rather than a hash copied from an older guide. sha256sum Simple-Media-System.elf # PowerShell: Get-FileHash .\\Simple-Media-System.elf -Algorithm SHA256 The July stable release retains its own asset hashes on its release page. A source-code archive from a mutable rolling tag is not a substitute for the tester bundle's build identity."},{"title":"What the validation establishes","cat":"Start","url":"releases.html#whats-new","text":"The merged work passed host regression tests, target builds and controlled emulator probes. Those checks expose specific parser, I/O and frame-lifetime defects. They do not establish affected-console color, USB throughput, exact-file MP4/MPEG playback, skin restart, shuffle or audio-switching acceptance. All nine reported issues were still open at this audit."},{"title":"Synthetic test-media pack","cat":"Start","url":"releases.html#test-media","text":"The test-media prerelease is regenerated on master from tools/gen-test-media.sh . It includes short codec fixtures and the MP4 matrix with a README. Generation is a host operation. Record your console, storage source, build and actual result when playing it."},{"title":"Historical changelog","cat":"Start","url":"releases.html#historical-changelog","text":"SMS has a long history. It was created by Eugene Plotnikov as, in his own words, \"the simplest DivX player able to play good-resolution movies at good frame rate on the unmodded PS2 without any extra equipment,\" and was developed intensively across 2005–2009 before the ps2homebrew community carried it forward. The high-level lineage below is drawn from the project's own CHANGELOG.md — refer to that file in the repository for the exhaustive, entry-by-entry history. Era What arrived Mid–2005 — foundations The core DivX/XviD/MPEG-4 player using the PS2's IPU for colour conversion; CDDAFS (playing data off a burned audio CD on an unmodded console); early A/V synchronisation and V-Blank sync; internal HDD support via APA/PFS partitions; a heavily PS2-optimised FFmpeg-derived core. Summer 2005 — the GUI The three-area GUI that SMS still uses — device menu, file browser and status line — with pause / resume / stop, the file browser, and screen-alignment adjustments; USB mass-storage support; AC3 audio; config saved to the memory card. Late 2005 — network & discs Network (host:) playback; ISO CD/DVD and later DVD-Video disc reading on unmodded consoles; more codecs; skin/background-image support; a proper SMS menu and internationalised, scalable fonts. Late 2005 / early 2006 — subtitles & playlists External SRT / SUB subtitles with adjustable size and colour, plus manual A/V and subtitle sync; multi-audio-track support; the object-oriented desktop rewrite; early MP3 / M3U playback and \"play all in folder\". 2006 — more formats & input Remote-control support; MPEG-1/2 program-stream video; DTS and OGG Vorbis audio; on-disc file management (copy/delete on the internal HDD); pan-scan and display tuning; HD/DTV video-mode groundwork. Late 2006 — SMB The SMB/CIFS network client, so SMS could browse Windows / Samba shares over the network adapter; faster network transfers; UTF-8 subtitle support. 2007 — HD video & WMA 720p / 1080i and VESA/DTV video modes; a new floating-point MPEG audio decoder; WMA (ASF) and raw PCM audio; multiple SMB servers; assorted decoder and sync refinements. 2008 — audio breadth & pictures AAC / M4A and FLAC audio; a baseline JPEG viewer with slidesh"},{"title":"Legacy tools & downloads","cat":"Start","url":"releases.html#legacy-downloads","text":"Companion tools and files for classic SMS, preserved from the original ps2-home.com forum posts so they don't vanish. They are third-party works by their original authors , kept here purely for archival — we didn't write them and can't vouch for the Windows binaries. Third-party, as-is The .exe tools below are old Windows binaries (2005–2014) from the PS2 homebrew community, with no stated license; we have not run or vetted them, and antivirus may flag old packed executables — use at your own discretion. Credit belongs to their original authors. Some archives are .rar (open with 7-Zip / WinRAR). Download What it is Author / source SMS manual (PDF) The classic Simple Media System user manual. SMS community · ps2-home.com Language pack — SMS_lng09 20 community translations ( .lng ): BG, CA, CZ, DE, EN, ES, FR, HU, ID, IT, NL, PL, PT, PT-BR, RU, SE, SK, TR, UA. Vintage 2009 — likely missing newer menu strings, so treat as a reference/starting point. Translators credited in the pack's README (EN by Eugene Plotnikov) pic2smi Windows tool: converts a picture into an SMS .smi skin / background. ps2-home.com smspal Windows tool: edit / generate an SMS palette ( SMS.pal ). ps2-home.com Rad HOST Client 1.8a Windows PC tool: streams media to SMS over its host: connection. ps2-home.com"},{"title":"Where to next","cat":"Start","url":"releases.html#next","text":"Install & First Boot Get the ELF running MX4SIO The headline feature Building from Source Compile it yourself Credits & Sources Who built what"},{"title":"Video Playback","cat":"Playing media","url":"video.html","text":"SMS video containers, codecs, source limits, previously reported profiles, controls, conversion guidance and open regressions."},{"title":"Supported video formats","cat":"Playing media","url":"video.html#formats","text":"SMS recognises a video by its container and codec, not merely its extension. The heavy lifting is done by a set of FFmpeg-derived decoders that have been hand-optimised for the PS2's Emotion Engine. The table below lists what plays and the file extensions SMS looks for. Container Supported video codec Extensions AVI MPEG-4 Part 2 / ASP (DivX, Xvid and Microsoft MPEG-4 v3 tags) .avi .divx .xvid MP4 / ISO-BMFF MPEG-4 Part 2 ( mp4v ) .mp4 MPEG program stream MPEG-1 / MPEG-2 .mpg .mpeg Modern video codecs are not supported H.264/AVC, H.265/HEVC, VP9 and AV1 are not decoded. An MP4 carrying one of those video codecs may still play its AAC soundtrack, which looks like successful audio-only playback, but that does not mean the video codec is supported. WMV video is also not registered; .wma is a standalone Windows Media audio route. Audio inside a video (the soundtrack) is decoded by the same engine that plays standalone music files, so a movie can carry an MP3, AAC, AC3, or other supported audio track. For the full audio picture — including playlists and standalone music — see Audio & Playlists ."},{"title":"Exact implementation limits","cat":"Playing media","url":"video.html#implementation-limits","text":"These are hard limits visible in the current source, not estimates: Property Limit What it means Frame dimensions Width ≤ 1024 and height ≤ 1024 SMS rejects the file if either dimension is greater than 1024. MPEG-1/2 frame rate 23.976, 24, 25, 29.97, 30, 50, 59.94 or 60 fps The MPEG sequence-header rate table, including MPEG-2 extensions. Compressed video packet/sample 2,097,088 bytes The 2 MiB packet ring minus its mandatory 64-byte allocation overhead. Streams/tracks Eight table slots; MP4/MPEG-PS retain at most seven usable streams The container-wide stream table includes video, audio and subtitle tracks. Individual file size smaller than 4 GiB File sizes, positions and seeks use 32-bit addressing. 1024×1024 is an acceptance ceiling, not a speed guarantee The player has no universal maximum bitrate or generic MPEG-4 frame-rate rejection. A file that passes the dimension check can still be too demanding for real-time playback. Work varies with frame rate, B-frames, QPel/GMC, interlacing, motion complexity, audio decoding, device throughput and fragmentation. Large frames may also switch automatically from 32/24-bit to dithered 16-bit GS textures to fit video memory."},{"title":"Previously reported console profiles","cat":"Playing media","url":"video.html#hardware-tested","text":"Earlier project documentation records these fixtures as having played on a real PS2, including transport checks. This audit did not rerun that hardware matrix or qualify the October changes on the affected consoles. The generation script defines the inputs; CI generation alone is not a playback result. Container / codec Resolution / rate Encoded profile Result AVI / Xvid + MP3 640×480, 30 fps 1.5 Mbit/s video; no B-frames; GOP 30; 128 kbit/s MP3 Passed MPEG-2 PS + MP2 640×480, 30 fps 2.5 Mbit/s video; GOP 15; 192 kbit/s MP2 Passed MPEG-1 PS + MP2 640×480, 30 fps 1.15 Mbit/s video; GOP 15; 192 kbit/s MP2 Passed MP4 / mp4v + AAC 640×480, 30 fps 1.5 Mbit/s video; no B-frames; GOP 30; 48 kHz stereo AAC at 128 kbit/s Passed MP4 / mp4v + AAC 320×240, 30 fps 600 kbit/s video; no B-frames; GOP 30; 48 kHz stereo AAC at 128 kbit/s Passed MP4 / H.264 or H.265 + supported audio Tested negative cases Unsupported video codec No video, as expected; supported audio can still play This table is the real-time confirmed baseline , not the full accepted range. Higher dimensions up to the 1024-pixel-per-axis source limit remain valid inputs but have not been qualified as universally smooth."},{"title":"Playing a video","cat":"Playing media","url":"video.html#playing","text":"Opening a movie is a three-key affair: pick the device, walk into the folder, press play."},{"title":"Pick the device","cat":"Playing media","url":"video.html#pick-the-device","text":"SMS opens on the device menu — the top row of device icons. Use Left / Right to highlight the device your movie lives on (a massN: device for USB / MX4SIO / HDD-exFAT / i.LINK, an mmce0: card, the internal hdd0: , a CD/DVD, smb: or udpfs: ). If the device you want is not listed, it may not be started yet — see Devices Overview for the Autostart toggles and the \"Start \u003cdevice> now\" option."},{"title":"Enter the file list","cat":"Playing media","url":"video.html#enter-the-file-list","text":"Press Down to drop into the file menu (the middle browser). Move with Up / Down , page through long folders with L1 / L2 , open a folder with ✕ , and step back up one level with △ (folder → parent → device menu). The status line at the bottom shows the current path on the left and a clock on the right."},{"title":"Press play","cat":"Playing media","url":"video.html#press-play","text":"Highlight the video file and press ✕ . SMS reads the file straight from the device and starts playback. When the movie ends — or when you stop it — you land back in the browser exactly where you were. ℹ Playing from MX4SIO This fork's headline feature is smooth video playback from an MX4SIO microSD adapter, which earlier SMS builds could not do reliably. It works exactly like any other device: pick the MX4SIO device, browse to your movie, press ✕ . See the MX4SIO page for setup."},{"title":"Player controls","cat":"Playing media","url":"video.html#controls","text":"While a video is playing, the pad drives the player directly: Button Action Select Pause. Press it again — or Start — to resume. Start Resume from pause; during normal playback it opens the in-player menu. △ Triangle Stop playback and return to the file browser Left / Right Seek backward / forward Up / Down Volume up / down ✕ Cross Show or hide the on-screen time display □ Square Cycle the aspect / pan&scan mode — eight in all. The chosen mode is remembered in your settings. L1 / R1 Pan the picture left / right (useful once you have zoomed with Square) L1 + R1 Reset pan and zoom back to default ○ Circle Cycle the sync adjusters: A/V sync → subtitle sync (only when a subtitle is loaded) → off. L2 / R2 With an adjuster selected by Circle, shift it by −10 / +10 ms — the fix for audio or subtitles that drift out of step. L2 + R2 Reset the selected sync adjuster to zero Cross, Circle, L2 and R2 are video-only. During audio-only playback those buttons do nothing, and Left/Right seek within an ordinary track; M3U transport can move between playlist entries. If the movie has an external subtitle file alongside it, SMS displays it over the picture — see Subtitles for how subtitle files are matched and styled. ℹ Videos do not chain When a video ends you go back to the file browser. This is deliberate: the file after a video is usually unrelated, so auto-advancing would be a surprise. Only audio continues into the next track of the folder — see Audio & Playlists . To watch several videos back to back, highlight the folder and choose Play all → Video ."},{"title":"Play a video folder in order or shuffled","cat":"Playing media","url":"video.html#folder-playback","text":"Highlight a folder and press ○ Circle to open Play all , then choose Video to play its videos back to back. For shuffled playback, first enable Randomize playlist under Player settings → MP3 settings . This setting also controls video folders, despite living in the MP3 sub-menu. Turn it off to keep the folder's normal play order."},{"title":"How SMS decodes video","cat":"Playing media","url":"video.html#decoding","text":"SMS earns its \"good video on a stock PS2\" reputation by splitting the work between the Emotion Engine and a dedicated piece of PS2 hardware — the IPU (Image Processing Unit). Decoders derived from FFmpeg. SMS's video and audio decoders started life as parts of the FFmpeg project and were then heavily reworked for the PS2 — moving to a packed image format so the decoder produces macroblocks in the IPU's native layout, and making aggressive use of the scratchpad and DMA. The original author measured roughly a 300% speed-up in video decoding versus stock FFmpeg from these changes. The IPU does the colour conversion. Turning decoded frames into a displayable picture (the colour-space conversion, YUV → RGB) is handed to the IPU, which runs it as an interrupt-driven process in parallel with the main CPU still decoding the next frames. Because that step is offloaded, the Emotion Engine is free to spend its cycles decoding — which is exactly why a plain, unmodified PS2 can play good-resolution movies at a good frame rate with no HDD and no extra hardware. Performance depends on the file The PS2 is a fixed console, so how smoothly a movie plays comes down to how demanding the file is. Resolution, frame rate and coding tools affect decoder work; bitrate also affects storage demand but is not a complete measure of decode complexity. A demanding encode can push the console past real-time, especially from a slower or fragmented device. Start from a hardware-confirmed profile when making a new encode."},{"title":"Encoding tips","cat":"Playing media","url":"video.html#encoding","text":"If you are making files for SMS rather than playing existing ones, a few choices make the difference between flawless playback and a slideshow. You do not have to do any of this by hand. The Media Converter is a desktop app that applies these settings for you and only offers formats SMS can play — the presets below are what it produces. Use it unless you specifically want to drive FFmpeg yourself. Start from a tested profile. 640×480 at 30 fps with 1.5 Mbit/s MPEG-4 Part 2 video is confirmed in both AVI/Xvid + MP3 and MP4/ mp4v + AAC. Lower dimensions or bitrate provide more headroom. Use a supported codec, not just a familiar extension. For MP4, select MPEG-4 Part 2 ( mp4v ), not the much more common H.264 or H.265 encoders. Avoid expensive tools when compatibility matters most. B-frames, QPel and GMC are implemented but add work. The hardware-confirmed fixtures use no B-frames. Defragment on slower devices. On a device with limited read throughput, a heavily fragmented file forces the console to seek constantly, which can starve the decoder. Copying the file back onto a freshly-formatted card or drive so it lands contiguously gives the smoothest reads, which helps most on the slowest media. Add subtitles as a separate file. Use external text subtitles when you want to switch or restyle them; put a matching .srt / .sub next to the movie. Converter burn-in is available when permanent text is preferred — details on the Subtitles page. The exact command used for the confirmed 640×480 MP4 profile is: ffmpeg -i INPUT.mp4 -c:v mpeg4 -vtag mp4v -b:v 1500k -bf 0 -g 30 \\ -s 640x480 -c:a aac -b:a 128k -ar 48000 -ac 2 \\ -movflags +faststart OUTPUT.mp4"},{"title":"Community 960×544 MPEG-2 profile","cat":"Playing media","url":"video.html#community-mpeg2","text":"tktagmedia reported a successful 44-minute MX4SIO playback using the higher-resolution MPEG-2/MP2 approach. The preserved Windows drag-and-drop script scales to 960×540, pads to 960×544, forces a 16:9 display aspect, uses two passes at 3 Mbps (3.4 Mbps maxrate), GOP 15, two B-frames and 224 kbps/48 kHz stereo MP2. This is a reported profile, not a universal default or a confirmed fix for the open USB/color reports. The script does not cap the source frame rate at 30 fps, and its forced aspect is unsuitable for every source. Start with a short sample and compare the same file/settings on your console. A 60 ms V/A -00:060 workaround is not a universal six-frame NTSC correction; use the sync controls only as needed."},{"title":"Limitations","cat":"Playing media","url":"video.html#limitations","text":"SMS is a stock-console player, and a few historic limits come with that. Knowing them up front saves a lot of \"why won't this play\" head-scratching. Codec support is deliberately narrow. There are no H.264, H.265, VP9, AV1 or WMV video decoders. Interlaced is supported, but not part of the current fixture baseline. The MPEG-4 decoder contains interlaced field/DCT paths, and MPEG-2 supports frame- and field-encoded interlaced video. The published test profiles above are progressive. The source ceiling and the real-time ceiling are different. SMS accepts each dimension through 1024, but no single bitrate or resolution below that is guaranteed for every codec-tool and storage combination. Large files remain 32-bit addressed. exFAT removes FAT32's filesystem limit, but the SMS file context still limits an individual file to less than 4 GiB. Why can a stock PS2 play \"good-resolution movies\" at all? Because SMS never asks the Emotion Engine to do the whole job. It offloads colour-space conversion to the PS2's IPU as an interrupt-driven, parallel process, and it re-shaped the FFmpeg decoder so its output lands in exactly the format the IPU wants — cutting the traffic between CPU and memory and freeing the main processor to keep decoding. That parallelism, plus scratchpad-and-DMA optimisation, is what let the original author benchmark peak frame rates well above real-time on standard-definition DivX. It is the same architecture this fork still relies on — the fork's work includes parser, streaming and frame-lifetime changes as well as access to more devices. See the current issue map for the limits of validation."},{"title":"Where to next","cat":"Playing media","url":"video.html#next","text":"Subtitles SRT / SUB over your video Audio & Playlists Soundtracks, music, M3U MX4SIO Play movies from microSD USB The easiest source Devices Overview Start the device you need Troubleshooting When a file won't play Code/fixture references: decoder registration , stream table , fixture definitions . See current acceptance gaps ."},{"title":"Audio & Playlists","cat":"Playing media","url":"audio.html","text":"SMS audio formats, sample/channel constraints, M3U playlists, folder playback, shuffle and repeat behavior."},{"title":"Supported audio formats","cat":"Playing media","url":"audio.html#formats","text":"SMS recognises audio files by their extension and hands them to the matching decoder. These are the audio formats and file extensions this build plays: Format Extensions Notes MPEG Audio Layer II / III .mp2 .mp3 .mpa MP2 and MP3 use the shared MPEG-audio decoder. AAC Main / LC and HE-AAC/SBR .aac .m4a .mp4 .aac is raw ADTS; .m4a is the audio-only ISO-BMFF route. An audio-only .mp4 falls back to the same reader. AC3 (Dolby Digital) .ac3 The same audio format SMS decodes for DVD-style video soundtracks. FLAC .flac Mono or stereo; decoded to 16-bit PCM output. Ogg Vorbis .ogg Mono or stereo; decoded to 16-bit PCM output. WMA v1 / v2 .wma Windows Media Audio carried in an ASF container. This is audio, not WMV video. Supported movie/container tracks can additionally use DTS and 16-bit little- or big-endian PCM . Those codecs are registered for embedded audio, but the browser does not expose standalone .dts or .wav routes."},{"title":"Exact implementation limits","cat":"Playing media","url":"audio.html#implementation-limits","text":"Property Current behavior / limit Compressed audio packet/sample 524,224 bytes maximum (512 KiB packet ring minus the mandatory 64-byte allocation overhead) AAC object types AAC Main and AAC-LC, with SBR/HE-AAC extension processing AAC core sample rates 8, 11.025, 12, 16, 22.05, 24, 32, 44.1, 48, 64, 88.2 and 96 kHz AAC channels Mono/stereo output; accepted multichannel AAC is downmixed to stereo FLAC and Ogg Vorbis channels Mono or stereo only FLAC, Ogg and AAC decoded output 16-bit PCM Container tracks Eight table slots; MP4 and MPEG-PS retain at most seven streams including video/subtitles Individual file size Smaller than 4 GiB because file sizes, positions and seeks are 32-bit"},{"title":"Previously reported audio matrix","cat":"Playing media","url":"audio.html#hardware-tested","text":"Earlier project documentation records successful playback of these 20-second audio fixtures. This is a historical reported baseline, not a new test of the current merge. The matrix uses a 44.1 kHz mono tone unless otherwise noted: Route Fixture profile Result MP3 192 kbit/s Passed Ogg Vorbis Quality 5 Passed WMA v2 128 kbit/s Passed FLAC Lossless mono fixture Passed AC-3 192 kbit/s Passed AAC in M4A 128 kbit/s Passed Raw ADTS AAC 128 kbit/s Passed MP2 192 kbit/s Passed Audio-only MP4 / AAC 48 kHz stereo, 128 kbit/s Passed as music with the visualiser AAC Main, HE-AAC/SBR, WMA v1, DTS, embedded PCM and multichannel downmix are source-supported paths but were not separate cases in this hardware fixture matrix. Match the extension to the contents SMS chooses the decoder from the file's extension. A file that has been renamed to the wrong extension (say, an AAC stream saved as .mp3 ) may fail to play. Keep the extension honest — .flac for FLAC, .ogg for Vorbis, and so on — and if a track refuses to open, double-check that its real format matches its name."},{"title":"Playing audio","cat":"Playing media","url":"audio.html#playing","text":"Playing a song is exactly like opening any other file — browse to it and press Cross."},{"title":"Pick the device","cat":"Playing media","url":"audio.html#pick-the-device","text":"In the device menu (the top row of device icons), press Left/Right to highlight the device your music is on, then press Down to drop into the file list."},{"title":"Find the track","cat":"Playing media","url":"audio.html#find-the-track","text":"In the file list , move with Up/Down; use L1 / L2 to page-scroll long folders and Triangle to step up a level. The status line at the bottom shows your current path on the left."},{"title":"Press Cross to play","cat":"Playing media","url":"audio.html#press-cross-to-play","text":"Highlight the audio file and press ✕ . SMS opens the audio player and starts playback."},{"title":"The audio player screen","cat":"Playing media","url":"audio.html#player-screen","text":"While a track plays, SMS shows its audio player rather than a video picture. From here the pad controls playback (see Controls below). When one track finishes, SMS moves on to the next entry if there is a queue — either the rest of an M3U playlist or the other music in the same folder — otherwise playback ends and you return to the browser. Can SMS play every song in a folder automatically? Yes. SMS has long had a \"play all the music in this folder\" behaviour, so once one track finishes it can roll on to the next file in the same directory rather than stopping after a single song. Simply pressing Cross on a track is enough — it plays on through the rest of the folder's music, skipping non-audio files, and stops when you press Triangle . To play a folder without picking a track first, highlight the folder and choose Play all → Audio . Two related options live in Settings → Player settings → MP3 settings : Randomize playlist and Repeat mode . They behave differently from what the names suggest. Repeat mode repeats the item you are playing , not the folder — so with an .m3u playlist it loops the whole playlist, but during folder playback it loops the current track and never advances. Turn it off if you want a folder to play through."},{"title":"Playlists (M3U)","cat":"Playing media","url":"audio.html#playlists","text":"An M3U file ( .m3u ) is a plain-text list of tracks to play, in order. Instead of opening songs one at a time, you open the .m3u and SMS queues every track it lists and plays them back to back. SMS treats .m3u as a first-class media file: it appears in the browser like any other, and pressing ✕ on it starts the whole list."},{"title":"What goes in an .m3u file","cat":"Playing media","url":"audio.html#m3u-format","text":"An M3U is just one file path per line. SMS understands both the bare form (one path per line) and the extended #EXTM3U / #EXTINF form that carries a duration and a display title for each entry. A minimal example: #EXTM3U #EXTINF:213,Opening Track Music/track01.mp3 #EXTINF:187,Second Track Music/track02.flac Music/track03.ogg A few practical notes on how SMS reads them: Relative paths work — a path is taken relative to the folder the playlist lives in, so keeping the .m3u alongside its tracks is the simplest layout. Slashes are flexible — Windows-style back-slashes in a path are accepted and treated as forward slashes. Mixed formats are fine — a single playlist can mix MP3, FLAC, OGG and the other supported audio formats; SMS picks the right decoder for each entry as it reaches it. Titles are optional — with #EXTINF lines SMS uses the supplied title; without them it falls back to the track's filename. ℹ Shuffle a playlist When the player's randomize option is on, SMS plays a playlist's tracks in a shuffled order rather than the order written in the file. Turn it off to hear the list exactly as it is laid out. See the Settings reference for where this lives."},{"title":"Controls during playback","cat":"Playing media","url":"audio.html#controls","text":"Audio uses the same transport controls as video, so if you already know one you know the other: Button Action Select Pause Start Resume △ Triangle Stop and return to the file browser Up / Down Volume up / down Left / Right Seek backward / forward within the track Stopping a playlist with Triangle drops you back to the browser; there is no need to let the whole queue finish."},{"title":"Audio inside video","cat":"Playing media","url":"audio.html#soundtracks","text":"The same decoders power the soundtrack of a movie. When you play an AVI, MP4 or MPEG file, SMS decodes its embedded audio stream — commonly MP3, MP2, AAC or AC3 — right alongside the picture. If the video codec itself is unsupported (for example H.264 or H.265) the supported audio track may still play by itself. So \"audio format support\" and \"a video's soundtrack\" are two sides of the same engine; for the video side of things, see Video Playback ."},{"title":"Tips","cat":"Playing media","url":"audio.html#tips","text":"Name files clearly. The browser shows filenames, so descriptive names ( 01 - Artist - Title.mp3 ) make navigating an album far easier than cryptic ones. Keep a playlist with its tracks. Because relative paths are resolved against the playlist's own folder, storing the .m3u in the same directory as the songs is the most portable arrangement — copy the folder to another device and it still works. Use the right extension. Since SMS selects the decoder by extension, an honest extension is the single most reliable way to make sure a track opens on the first try. Lossless files increase storage demand. FLAC files are large; playing them from a suitable device such as MX4SIO microSD or the internal HDD is smoother than from a slow source. See Devices Overview . ℹ Where to go next For the moving-picture side, read Video Playback . To pick where your music lives, start at Devices Overview and the per-device pages. Every playback and player option is catalogued in the Settings reference . Code/fixture references: decoder registration , stream table , fixture definitions . See current acceptance gaps ."},{"title":"Pictures","cat":"Playing media","url":"pictures.html","text":"Baseline JPEG viewing in SMS, image-folder playback, rotation, sizing and current viewer limits."},{"title":"Overview","cat":"Playing media","url":"pictures.html#overview","text":"Alongside video and audio, SMS reads JPEG still images. JPEG decoding, rescaling and GS upload consume time and memory; large images can take longer to open. The viewer works the same way no matter where the file lives — a burned disc, USB stick, MX4SIO microSD, the internal HDD, i.LINK, MMCE, an SMB share or the UDPFS network drive. If you can browse to the file, you can view it. ℹ What SMS shows The viewer handles standard baseline JPEG photos — the everyday .jpg / .jpeg files that cameras and phones produce. It is a picture viewer , not an editor: it opens and displays images, and lets you page through, rotate, and rescale them on screen."},{"title":"Viewing a picture","cat":"Playing media","url":"pictures.html#viewing","text":"Getting a photo on screen is the same three-area workflow you use for any file in SMS."},{"title":"Pick the device","cat":"Playing media","url":"pictures.html#pick-the-device","text":"In the device menu (the top row of icons), use Left/Right to highlight the device that holds your photos, then press Down to drop into the file list."},{"title":"Browse to the folder","cat":"Playing media","url":"pictures.html#browse-to-the-folder","text":"In the file list use Up/Down to move (L1/L2 page-scroll long lists), Cross to open a folder, and Triangle to go up one level. Photos can sit anywhere on the device — SMS browses the raw filesystem, so there is no special \"pictures\" folder you have to use."},{"title":"Open the JPEG","cat":"Playing media","url":"pictures.html#open-the-jpeg","text":"Highlight a .jpg or .jpeg file and press Cross . SMS decodes the image and displays it full-screen, with the file's path shown briefly along the bottom. If a picture won't open If SMS can't decode a file, it shows the filename with an error indication instead of the photo. Progressive or unusual JPEG variants, or files that aren't really JPEGs, are the usual cause — re-save the image as a standard (baseline) JPEG and try again. You can still step to the next picture with Right ."},{"title":"Controls in the viewer","cat":"Playing media","url":"pictures.html#controls","text":"Once a photo is on screen, the pad controls the viewer directly (these also respond to the equivalent buttons on a PS2 DVD remote): Button What it does Right (or Cross ) Next picture in the set. At the last image it wraps back to the first. Only when a folder was opened with Play all → Images; a single photo opened on its own has nothing to step to. Left Previous picture. At the first image it wraps to the last. Same condition as above. Square Toggle how the image is sized on screen — switches between fit-to-screen and actual (unscaled) view. Circle Rotate the picture. R1 Rotate the picture the other way. Triangle Leave the viewer and return to the file browser. ℹ Stepping through a folder Opening a single .jpg with Cross shows just that one picture — Left and Right have nothing to step to. To page through a folder, highlight the folder itself and choose Play all → Images ; Left and Right then walk the whole set in order, wrapping at each end."},{"title":"Tips","cat":"Playing media","url":"pictures.html#tips","text":"Use standard baseline JPEG files (the normal output of cameras, phones, and most photo software). That is what the viewer is built for. The PS2 outputs a standard-definition-style picture, so there is nothing to gain from enormous multi-megapixel files — SMS fits the image to the screen anyway. Reasonable, screen-sized JPEGs open fastest and use the least memory. Keep the photos you want to flip through together in one folder . Because Left/Right step through the current folder, a tidy folder becomes an easy, browsable set of pictures. Faster storage means faster loading. Reading large photos from a quick device (USB, MX4SIO microSD, the internal HDD) feels snappier than over a slow disc or a busy network share. Can SMS play a slideshow, or zoom into a photo? The viewer's confirmed on-screen actions are stepping between images (Left/Right), rotating (Circle / R1), switching between fit-to-screen and actual size (Square), and returning to the browser (Triangle). This documentation only describes controls that are verified in the build, so if you're looking for an automatic timed slideshow or a free zoom-and-pan, treat those as not part of the picture viewer here. ℹ Related pages Looking for moving pictures instead? See Video Playback . For where your files can live and how to turn devices on, see the Devices Overview and the Settings Reference ."},{"title":"Subtitles","cat":"Playing media","url":"subtitles.html","text":"External SRT/SUB/TXT text, AVI DXSB bitmap subtitles, font/encoding settings and converter burn-in distinctions."},{"title":"Supported subtitle formats","cat":"Playing media","url":"subtitles.html#formats","text":"SMS recognises three file extensions for subtitles and reads two underlying text formats: Extension Format SMS reads it as Timing style .srt SubRip (SRT) Real time, HH:MM:SS,mmm --> HH:MM:SS,mmm .sub MicroDVD / time-code \"SUB\" Frame-based {start}{end} , or time [start][end] / H:M:S: .txt Same reader as .sub Frame- or time-based, as above In practice, SRT is the format to prefer : it is the most common subtitle format, it is easy to find and edit, and its cues carry exact times rather than frame counts, so they line up regardless of the video's frame rate. Use a .sub / .txt file when that is what you already have. SRT example SubRip time-coded cues SUB example Frame / time-code lines"},{"title":"SubRip (.srt)","cat":"Playing media","url":"subtitles.html#srt-detail","text":"An SRT file is a numbered list of cues. Each cue is a sequence number, a start-and-end time line using --> , and one or more lines of text, followed by a blank line: 1 00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:15,500 This is the first subtitle. 2 00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:18,200 And this one has two lines. SMS also understands simple \u003cb> , \u003ci> and \u003cu> tags around a line — bold, italic and underline — and shows those lines in their own colours (see Subtitle settings below)."},{"title":"MicroDVD-style (.sub / .txt)","cat":"Playing media","url":"subtitles.html#sub-detail","text":"The .sub / .txt reader accepts frame-based cues where the numbers in braces are the start and end frame , with a pipe | separating lines: {300}{375}This is the first subtitle. {400}{455}And this one has|two lines. Frame-timed SUB files depend on frame rate Because brace-style .sub cues are counted in frames, SMS converts them to real time using the video's frame rate. If your .sub was authored for a different frame rate than your video, the lines will drift out of sync as the movie plays. SRT (real-time) files do not have this problem — if timing wanders on a SUB file, look for an SRT of the same subtitles instead."},{"title":"Adding subtitles to a video","cat":"Playing media","url":"subtitles.html#adding","text":"SMS finds a subtitle automatically by name-matching : it takes the video's file name, drops the extension, and looks in the same folder for a file with the same base name and a .srt , .sub or .txt extension. If it finds one, it loads it when the video starts."},{"title":"Match the file names","cat":"Playing media","url":"subtitles.html#match-the-file-names","text":"Rename the subtitle so its base name is identical to the video. For Movie.avi the subtitle should be Movie.srt (or Movie.sub / Movie.txt ). The two names must agree up to the dot; only the extension differs."},{"title":"Put them in the same folder","cat":"Playing media","url":"subtitles.html#put-them-in-the-same-folder","text":"Copy the subtitle onto the same device and into the same directory as the video, so they sit side by side in the file list. SMS looks for the sidecar file in that same folder — it does not search elsewhere."},{"title":"Leave \"Autoload subtitles\" on","cat":"Playing media","url":"subtitles.html#leave-autoload-subtitles-on","text":"Automatic loading is controlled by the Autoload subtitles option in Settings → Player settings (press Start in the browser to open the SMS menu), which is on by default . As long as it is on and the names match, the subtitle loads with no extra steps."},{"title":"Play the video","cat":"Playing media","url":"subtitles.html#play-the-video","text":"Open the video as usual. SMS shows a brief Loading subtitles progress step while it reads the file, then the text appears over the picture at the cue times. If the subtitle file has a problem, SMS reports a subtitle error with a line number instead — see Tips & troubleshooting . Soft subtitles rendered over video during playback. ℹ Picking a subtitle by hand If a subtitle is not named to match the video, SMS can still use it — but you have to ask for it. Highlight the video and press Circle instead of Cross : SMS opens a small browser so you can pick the subtitle file yourself. It is never offered automatically; opening with Cross only ever tries the name-matching above."},{"title":"Subtitle settings","cat":"Playing media","url":"subtitles.html#settings","text":"Subtitle appearance is controlled from Settings → Player settings — press Start in the browser to open the SMS menu, then choose Player settings… . Every option below is saved to your configuration , so once you set it to taste it sticks for future videos. While you adjust size and offset, SMS shows a live sample line so you can see the effect immediately. The subtitle settings, with a live sample line for size and position. Option What it does Autoload subtitles On/off. When on, SMS name-matches a sidecar .srt / .sub / .txt and loads it when the video starts. On by default. Opaque subtitles On/off. Draws a solid dark panel behind the text so it stays readable over bright or busy scenes, rather than letting the text sit directly on the picture. Subtitle color The colour of normal subtitle text, chosen from the palette. Subtitle bold color The colour used for lines wrapped in an SRT \u003cb> (bold) tag. Subtitle italic color The colour used for lines wrapped in an SRT \u003ci> (italic) tag. Subtitle underline color The colour used for lines wrapped in an SRT \u003cu> (underline) tag. Subtitle alignment Cycles through four placements for where lines sit horizontally: center , left , right and center-right . Subtitle offset How far up from the bottom of the screen the subtitle block sits. Raise it to lift the text above an overscan edge or a TV's own on-screen bar. Subtitle font width Horizontal size of the subtitle glyphs — makes the lettering narrower or wider. Subtitle font height Vertical size of the subtitle glyphs — makes the lettering shorter or taller. Subtitle MBCS Selects a multi-byte (MBCS) font for languages whose characters need one, so non-Latin subtitles render correctly. See Character encoding . ℹ Size is set as two separate numbers Font width and height are adjusted independently, so you can, for example, keep the letters short but stretch them wider, or the reverse. The settings menu previews the size on the sample line as you change it. Nudge them up a little if the text is hard to read from the couch, or down if long lines are wrapping too much."},{"title":"Tips & troubleshooting","cat":"Playing media","url":"subtitles.html#tips","text":"External SRT/SUB/TXT text, AVI DXSB bitmap subtitles, font/encoding settings and converter burn-in distinctions."},{"title":"Character encoding","cat":"Playing media","url":"subtitles.html#encoding","text":"SMS reads subtitle text as either UTF-8 or a single code page: UTF-8 is detected automatically when the file begins with a UTF-8 byte-order mark. Saving your SRT as \"UTF-8 with BOM\" from a text editor is the most dependable way to get accented and non-Latin characters right. Files without a UTF-8 mark are interpreted through the current code page, so plain ASCII always works, and other single-byte text works when the matching code page is in use. For scripts that need a multi-byte font (for example CJK), turn on Subtitle MBCS and select an installed multi-byte font; otherwise those characters cannot be drawn. If a subtitle shows boxes, wrong letters or gibberish where accented or non-Latin characters should be, it is almost always an encoding mismatch — re-save the file as UTF-8 (with BOM), or match the code page / MBCS font to the language."},{"title":"Timing","cat":"Playing media","url":"subtitles.html#timing","text":"Prefer SRT for correct sync. SRT cues carry absolute times, so they line up on any video. Frame-based .sub files are tied to a frame rate and can drift if that rate does not match the movie. Match the release. A subtitle written for one cut or frame rate of a film will not necessarily fit another version of the same film. If lines run consistently early or late, you likely have the wrong subtitle for that particular video file. Watch for wrapping. Very long single lines are wrapped to fit the screen. If wrapping looks awkward, reducing the font width a little often lets a line fit on one row."},{"title":"Subtitle errors","cat":"Playing media","url":"subtitles.html#errors","text":"If SMS cannot make sense of the file, it shows a subtitle error naming the problem (a format or sequence issue) and the line number where it gave up, then returns you to playback. This usually points at a malformed cue — a mistyped time line, a missing sequence number, or a file that is not actually SRT/SUB text. Open the file in a plain text editor, fix the flagged area, and try again. Why does my .sub file drift but the .srt is fine? Brace-style .sub cues ( {start}{end} ) are measured in frames , not seconds. SMS turns those frame numbers into real times using the video's frame rate. If the file was authored against a different frame rate than your copy of the video, every cue lands slightly off, and the error grows over the length of the movie. An SRT file stores real times directly, so it is immune to this. When a SUB file drifts, the simplest fix is to find and use an SRT of the same subtitles. Video Playback Formats, controls, the player Settings Reference Every menu & option Troubleshooting When something won't play"},{"title":"Embedded bitmap subtitles and converter burn-in","cat":"Playing media","url":"subtitles.html#embedded-and-burned","text":"The AVI decoder registers DXSB bitmap subtitles. That existing reader does not imply support for arbitrary embedded text tracks in MP4 or other containers. The converter can render one external or embedded text subtitle into the pixels. Burn-in cannot be switched off in SMS; external sidecars remain the flexible text option. See converter subtitle controls ."},{"title":"Media Converter","cat":"Playing media","url":"converter.html","text":"SMS Media Converter presets, multi-audio output, text-subtitle burn-in, source SD sizing and PASS/WARN/FAIL limits."},{"title":"Prepare a file for SMS","cat":"Playing media","url":"converter.html#why","text":"Install FFmpeg and FFprobe from a trusted distribution, or let the application's setup helper locate them. A Windows executable does not guarantee they are installed. Builds need the selected encoders; text burn-in also requires the subtitles/libass filter. Add a source file, choose an output folder and start with a conservative preset. Keep a short sample for your first console test. Use Advanced settings only for deliberate changes. The settings badge tells you whether settings differ from the preset; it is not a hardware certification badge. Read the conversion/validation result and retain the log if anything fails. Test the output in SMS before converting a large library. H.264, H.265, VP9 and AV1 are not SMS video decoders. An MP4 container may hold either supported mp4v or unsupported modern video; conversion changes the codec, not just the filename."},{"title":"Preset choices","cat":"Playing media","url":"converter.html#presets","text":"Preset family Default target Tradeoff Xvid-compatible AVI 640×480, 30 fps; 1.5 Mbps video + 128 kbps MP3. Software MPEG-4 decode with hardware-assisted color conversion. A useful starting profile; content and transport can still cause stutter. MPEG-2 / MPEG-1 PS 640×480, 30 fps; 2.5 Mbps MPEG-2 or 1.15 Mbps MPEG-1 + 192 kbps MP2. IPU-assisted MPEG path. Compare it with AVI on your setup; neither guarantees smoother playback for every source. MPEG-4 MP4 640×480 / 1.5 Mbps, or 320×240 / 600 kbps; 30 fps + 128 kbps AAC. MPEG-4 Part 2, with the MP4 index front-loaded using +faststart . See the remaining MP4 report . High Quality — Fast Storage 10 Mbps Xvid, GOP 6, 48 kHz stereo PCM16; keeps source SD dimensions. High storage demand; console acceptance is pending. Stereo PCM adds 1.536 Mbps before container overhead. MP3 / Ogg Vorbis / AAC music 192 kbps, 48 kHz stereo. Lossy audio output. Video and embedded cover art are dropped. FLAC music FLAC with the source sample rate/channel count retained when possible. Lossless compression does not restore a lossy source or guarantee unchanged samples after format conversion. SMS's FLAC path requires mono/stereo and outputs 16-bit PCM; read validation results for your input. Ordinary video presets use GOP 50 and zero B-frames unless changed; the high-quality preset uses GOP 6. The generated historical test fixtures use different GOP values, so a fixture result is not proof of every converter setting."},{"title":"Tracks, subtitles and advanced controls","cat":"Playing media","url":"converter.html#advanced","text":"SMS Media Converter presets, multi-audio output, text-subtitle burn-in, source SD sizing and PASS/WARN/FAIL limits."},{"title":"One audio track or all source audio","cat":"Playing media","url":"converter.html#audio-tracks","text":"Source audio track selects a zero-based source index. Keep all source audio tracks encodes all selected source audio to the configured audio codec in AVI, MPEG-PS or MP4. At most six audio tracks plus video are allowed; excess tracks produce a settings error. Tracks are re-encoded, not copied bit for bit. Language/title metadata are preserved where supported by the container. The player has its own stream limits and codec/container mappings. Validate actual audio switching in SMS; a desktop muxer accepting a track does not prove that the PS2 reader can use it."},{"title":"Text-subtitle burn-in","cat":"Playing media","url":"converter.html#burn-in","text":"Select an external subtitle or a zero-based embedded text subtitle index. None disables embedded selection; Clear removes the selected input. One subtitle is rendered into the video pixels. It cannot subsequently be disabled or restyled in SMS. Bitmap subtitle burn-in and switchable embedded-text output are not implemented by this control. For switchable text, retain a separate matching SRT/SUB/TXT sidecar and use SMS's subtitle reader . The existing AVI DXSB bitmap-subtitle reader is a different player feature."},{"title":"Keep source SD dimensions","cat":"Playing media","url":"converter.html#source-sd","text":"Keep source SD resolution retains even source dimensions up to 720×576. Larger sources use the selected target; it is enabled by default only for the high-quality preset. Check display aspect and pixel aspect on your TV. Choosing this option alone does not establish hardware playback speed."},{"title":"Two-pass encoding","cat":"Playing media","url":"converter.html#encoding-passes","text":"The app implements two-pass video encoding: analysis followed by an encode using the recorded statistics. It can improve bitrate allocation, at the cost of another processing pass. It does not cap every instantaneous packet or eliminate source/transport bottlenecks. More than two passes is not exposed by this application."},{"title":"Keyframe interval and B-frames","cat":"Playing media","url":"converter.html#keyframes","text":"GOP/keyframe interval controls how far the player may need to decode from an earlier keyframe after a seek. At 30 fps, GOP 50 is about 1.67 seconds. B-frames are a separate prediction tool. Start with zero B-frames when isolating decode load; do not substitute one setting for the other."},{"title":"Understand PASS, WARN and FAIL","cat":"Playing media","url":"converter.html#notes","text":"The validator checks codecs/container mappings, dimensions, frame-rate constraints, track counts, file size and observed packet sizes across audio/video streams. Video packets must fit 2,097,088 bytes and audio packets 524,224 bytes. It stops an output approaching the 32-bit file limit; keep each file below 4 GiB. PASS : the inspected output passed the host checks. It is not a console playback result. WARN : an issue needs attention or inspection was incomplete. The packet scan is bounded for memory/time; an incomplete scan keeps the output and cannot certify unobserved packets. FAIL : a checked constraint failed, such as an observed oversized packet. Read the reason before retrying. Invalid input selections report a settings error before encoding. A real application exception can produce a crash log. Keep the preset, changed settings, source/output codec information and exact build when reporting it; do not treat every successful conversion as proof that an open player regression is fixed."},{"title":"Windows 7 package","cat":"Playing media","url":"converter.html#windows7","text":"The Win7 executable uses Python 3.8, PySide2/Qt5 and a compatible PyInstaller bootloader. The modern package uses Qt6. Current-runner tests verify packaging and logic, not operation on a live Windows 7 installation. The Windows FFmpeg fallback is digest-pinned; the pin must be refreshed before its archive retention expires. See developer notes ."},{"title":"Controls","cat":"Playing media","url":"controls.html","text":"SMS browser, video, audio and picture controls; sync adjustment and documentation keyboard shortcuts."},{"title":"Browser","cat":"Playing media","url":"controls.html#browser","text":"Button Action D-pad Left / Right Choose a device; from the file list return to device selection. D-pad Down Enter the selected device's file list; move down within it. D-pad Up Move up in the file list. Cross Open a folder or start the selected media file. Triangle Parent folder / back to the device list. L1 / L2 Page up / down in a long list. Start Open SMS settings. Circle on a folder Play all menu: Audio, Video or Images. Randomize playlist also affects video folders. Circle on video Choose an external subtitle manually. Select + Circle Power off from the browser."},{"title":"Video and audio","cat":"Playing media","url":"controls.html#playback","text":"Button Action Select Pause / timeline controls. Start Resume; during normal playback open the player menu. Triangle Stop and return to the browser. Left / Right Rewind / fast-forward; within a normal audio track, seek. Playlist transport can change entries. Up / Down Volume. Cross (video) Toggle on-screen timer; exit fast-forward/rewind preview. Square (video) Cycle pan-scan/aspect modes. L1 / R1 (video) Pan left / right; together reset pan/zoom. Circle (video) Select V/A audio/video or S/V subtitle/video sync adjustment. L2 / R2 (video) Decrease/increase the selected sync value by 10 ms; together reset it. Do not apply a fixed offset to every NTSC file. One community workaround used V/A -00:060 , a 60 ms setting, not a universal six-frame correction. Compare your actual file with the offset reset first. Use the in-player menu for available audio/subtitle selections. Multiple audio tracks in a converted file still need a playback test in SMS."},{"title":"Picture viewer","cat":"Playing media","url":"controls.html#pictures","text":"Cross or Right advances and Left goes back when a folder was opened with Play all → Images . A separately opened JPEG has no folder queue to advance through. Square toggles fit/actual size, Circle and R1 rotate, and Triangle returns to the browser."},{"title":"Documentation keyboard shortcuts","cat":"Playing media","url":"controls.html#keyboard","text":"On this website, / focuses search when you are not typing in a field. Up/Down chooses a result, Enter opens it and Escape closes results. On narrow screens the navigation button opens the guide list. Optional cursor orbs can be disabled and default off for reduced-motion/touch users."},{"title":"Devices Overview","cat":"Storage & network","url":"devices.html","text":"SMS storage namespaces, startup choices, driver exclusions and links to USB, MX4SIO, HDD, MMCE, i.LINK and network setup."},{"title":"The device bar","cat":"Storage & network","url":"devices.html#device-bar","text":"The SMS screen is split into three stacked areas, each drawn inside a rounded rectangle: Device menu — the top row of icons , one per source that is currently available. This is the device bar. File menu — the middle area: the file/folder browser for whichever device is selected. Status line — the bottom strip: the current path on the left and a clock on the right. Only the active area is highlighted. You move around the device bar with Left/Right , and press Down to drop into the file list for the highlighted device; Left/Right from the top of the file list takes you back up to the bar. A device only earns an icon on the bar once it has been started and something is present. Startup may already remount the launch source to read its settings; otherwise start the source you need (see Autostart vs Start now below). ℹ Navigating a device Inside the file list: Up/Down move the cursor, L1/L2 page-scroll, Cross opens a folder or plays a file, and Triangle goes up one level (folder → parent → back out to the device bar). In the browser, Select + Circle powers the console off. See the Getting Started guide for the full control map."},{"title":"How devices are named","cat":"Storage & network","url":"devices.html#naming","text":"Each source mounts under a short device name, the same style of prefix PS2 homebrew uses everywhere. Knowing the name helps you read the status line and understand which device you're on. Mount name What it is mass0: , mass1: , … BDM mass storage. USB, MX4SIO microSD, ATA/exFAT internal HDD and i.LINK drives all share this one namespace. The number ( N = 0, 1, 2…) is assigned in the order the volumes are discovered, not by which kind of device it is — so mass0: might be USB on one boot and your MX4SIO card on another, depending on what started first. mmce0: , mmce1: MMCE cards. An SD2PSX or MemCard PRO2 in memory-card slot 1 or slot 2. MMCE is not a BDM mass device, so it keeps its own name rather than joining the massN: pool. hdd0: Internal HDD (APA/PFS). The classic Sony hard-drive path, where SMS reads media from a PFS partition on the drive's APA table. cdfs CD/DVD data disc. A burned or pressed data disc (including DVD-Video), read through the console's optical drive. cdda Audio CD. Red-book audio tracks from a music CD. smb: SMB share. A folder shared from a Windows PC or a Samba server, reached over the network adapter. udpfs: UDPFS network drive. A PC folder served by PS2-Servers UDPFSD and discovered automatically over Ethernet. This is file-level UDPFS, not an emulated disk or UDPBD image. host: ps2link host. Files served from a PC over ps2link — a developer/testing path, not something you use for everyday playback. The massN: number can move Because BDM volumes are numbered in discovery order, a device's number is not fixed forever. If you rely on the icons on the device bar you'll never have to think about it — each running BDM device shows its own icon (USB, MX4SIO, ATA/HDD, i.LINK) so you pick the source visually, and SMS browses the volume behind that icon regardless of its massN: number."},{"title":"Autostart vs Start now","cat":"Storage & network","url":"devices.html#autostart","text":"SMS doesn't blindly poll every bus on boot. Instead, each device has an Autostart toggle in the settings menu, and — for anything not currently running — a Start \u003cdevice> now item that brings it up on demand without a reboot."},{"title":"Autostart \u003cdevice>","cat":"Storage & network","url":"devices.html#autostart-device","text":"A per-device on/off toggle. When on, SMS brings that source up automatically every time it boots. There is one toggle for each optional source: Autostart USB , Autostart i.LINK , Autostart MX4SIO , Autostart MMCE , Autostart HDD (the classic APA/PFS path) and Autostart HDD BDM (the ATA/exFAT path). Networking is a single choice presented as three rows — Autostart HOST (ps2dev) , Autostart SMB and Autostart UDPFS — and picking one clears the other two."},{"title":"Start \u003cdevice> now","cat":"Storage & network","url":"devices.html#start-device-now","text":"When a device is not already running, a matching \"Start \u003cdevice> now\" item appears so you can spin it up for this session without turning on Autostart or rebooting — handy for a one-off (plug in a drive, start it, browse it)."},{"title":"Clean config = optional Autostart off","cat":"Storage & network","url":"devices.html#clean-config-optional-autostart-off","text":"Out of the box, no optional Autostart flag is enabled . You decide what to enable. This keeps boot fast and avoids probing hardware you don't have. Clearing the config returns you to this state (see Where settings live ). ℹ Just want to try a device? Use Start now . It doesn't change your saved config, so the device won't come up again on the next boot unless you also flip its Autostart toggle. Once you know a source is one you use every session, turn on Autostart for it. Full details of every toggle live on the Settings Reference ."},{"title":"Mutual exclusions","cat":"Storage & network","url":"devices.html#exclusions","text":"Two storage pairs share a physical bus, and the two network implementations share the console's single Ethernet controller. Only one member of each pair can own its resource in a boot. MX4SIO ⇄ MMCE — the memory-card / SIO2 bus An MX4SIO microSD adapter and an MMCE device (SD2PSX / MemCard PRO2) both plug into the memory-card slot and talk over the console's SIO2 bus. Only one can be active at a time — if MX4SIO is running, starting MMCE will decline, and vice versa. To use one, make sure the other isn't autostarted; turn off the other's Autostart (or reboot) and start the one you want. APA HDD ⇄ ATA/exFAT HDD — the internal ATA bus The classic internal HDD path (APA/PFS, hdd0: ) and the modern ATA/exFAT path (which appears as a BDM massN: device) both drive the console's internal ATA bus. They can't run together — whichever starts first owns the bus , and the other backs off. Pick the one that matches how your internal drive is formatted: APA/PFS for a classic Sony-partitioned drive, ATA/exFAT for a drive formatted exFAT. SMB / host ⇄ UDPFS — one Ethernet stack per boot The ordinary SMS network path (SMB or developer host: ) and UDPFS load different drivers for the same EMAC3 Ethernet controller. Whichever network personality starts first owns the NIC until the next boot. Pick one of Autostart HOST (ps2dev) , Autostart SMB or Autostart UDPFS — selecting one automatically clears the other two. If a different network mode is already running when you switch, SMS offers to restart the network in place (\"Network is already running. Restart it now to apply?\"); accepting resets the IOP and rebuilds the chosen stack without relaunching SMS, declining saves the choice for the next boot. Note that an in-place restart also drops any mounted storage drivers, so they need starting again."},{"title":"Playback sources at a glance","cat":"Storage & network","url":"devices.html#all-sources","text":"Every storage backend SMS can read, side by side. Click a device to open its full page. Device Bus / how it connects Filesystem Mount name Notes USB USB port (BDM mass) FAT32 / exFAT massN: The everyday, no-extras source. Works on any PS2 with a USB port. MX4SIO Memory-card slot / SIO2 (BDM mass) FAT32 / exFAT massN: microSD in an MX4SIO adapter. Playback works in this fork — the headline feature. Excludes MMCE. Internal HDD — APA Internal ATA (APA/PFS) PFS on an APA partition hdd0: Classic Sony hard-drive path; SMS lists the drive's PFS partitions and mounts the one you pick. Excludes the ATA/exFAT path. Internal HDD — ATA/exFAT Internal ATA (BDM mass) exFAT massN: Modern exFAT internal drive over ATA — added in this fork. Excludes the APA path. i.LINK i.LINK / IEEE 1394 (BDM mass) FAT32 / exFAT massN: A FireWire drive on models with an i.LINK port. Added in this fork. MMCE Memory-card slot / SIO2 (non-BDM) Handled by the MMCE device mmce0: / mmce1: SD2PSX or MemCard PRO2. Its own device, not massN: . Excludes MX4SIO. SMB network Network adapter (Ethernet) SMB / CIFS share smb: A Windows or Samba shared folder, set up fully on-screen. Needs a network adapter. UDPFS network drive Network adapter (Ethernet) PC folder via UDPFS / UDPRDMA udpfs: Automatic LAN discovery of a PS2-Servers UDPFSD folder. File-level service, not UDPBD. Excludes SMB/host for that boot. CD / DVD Optical drive ISO 9660 (incl. DVD-Video) cdfs A burned or pressed data disc, read through the console's own drive. Audio CD (CDDA) Optical drive Red-book audio cdda Music CD tracks played directly. That's the ten playback sources. Another name, host: , exists for serving files from a PC over ps2link — it's a developer/testing path, not an everyday media source."},{"title":"Which source should I use?","cat":"Storage & network","url":"devices.html#which","text":"Just get going USB — any PS2, no extras Tiny & cheap MX4SIO microSD adapter Big library, internal Internal HDD (APA or exFAT) Stream from a PC SMB network share Fast PC folder UDPFS network drive FireWire drive i.LINK storage SD2PSX / PRO2 MMCE memory-card device Off a disc CD / DVD / audio CD Turn devices on Autostart & Start-now"},{"title":"Where settings live","cat":"Storage & network","url":"devices.html#config","text":"Use Save settings after changes. On a remountable launch source, SMS.cfg normally lives beside the ELF even when a memory card is fitted; SMS.smb follows its resolved directory. See Config Files & Paths for card-slot, network-file and fallback rules. ℹ Back to a clean slate Clearing the config resets SMS to defaults — meaning optional Autostart choices are off ; a remounted boot source may still appear in the device bar. See the Settings Reference for exactly what's stored and how to reset it. Why do USB, MX4SIO, ATA and i.LINK all share massN: ? Under the hood, those four are handled by the PS2 homebrew BDM (Block Device Manager) layer: each provides a block device, and a shared FAT/exFAT filesystem driver mounts the volumes it finds as mass0: , mass1: , and so on. Because they all present the same kind of block device, SMS treats them uniformly — the same browse-and-play code path works for a USB stick, an MX4SIO card, an exFAT internal drive or a FireWire disk. The device bar still shows a distinct icon for each so you always know which physical source you're looking at, even though they share a naming scheme. Do I need a hard drive or network adapter to use SMS? No. SMS runs on a bone-stock PS2 and can play straight from USB, MX4SIO, MMCE or a burned disc. The internal-HDD, SMB and UDPFS sources are there if you have the relevant hardware — they're optional extras, not requirements. Boot-source exception: optional Autostart flags default off, but SMS may start/remount the device holding its ELF/config during startup. A source already visible after first boot does not mean another Autostart choice was saved. See configuration discovery ."},{"title":"USB","cat":"Storage & network","url":"usb.html","text":"USB mass storage in SMS: FAT32/exFAT, device startup, file-size limits and the open USB stutter report."},{"title":"Overview","cat":"Storage & network","url":"usb.html#overview","text":"A USB mass-storage device — a flash drive/stick, or a self-powered USB hard disk — connects to the PS2's USB port. SMS loads its USB driver on the IOP and mounts the drive through BDM (the Block Device Manager), the same mounting layer used by MX4SIO, the exFAT internal HDD and i.LINK. Because they all share that layer, USB drives take device names in the common massN: namespace: the first mounted BDM volume is mass0: , the next mass1: , and so on. Once mounted, the drive appears as a device icon in the device menu (the top row of the SMS screen). Select it and press Down to drop into the file list and browse. SMS plays media directly off the drive — video, audio and pictures alike. Which massN: is my USB stick? The number is assigned in mount order, not per port, so it depends on which BDM devices are running and the order they came up. If you also run MX4SIO, i.LINK or an exFAT HDD, your USB drive may not always be mass0: . The active device's name is shown on the status line at the bottom of the screen — just glance there to confirm which volume you are browsing."},{"title":"Compatibility","cat":"Storage & network","url":"usb.html#compatibility","text":"SMS reads USB drives formatted with a standard PC filesystem and a normal partition table. Requirement Details Filesystem FAT32 or exFAT Partition table MBR (master boot record) — the usual default for USB media Device type USB flash drive / stick, or a self- or externally-powered USB hard disk Media location Anywhere on the drive — SMS browses the raw filesystem, so any folder layout works Format the drive as a single MBR partition. FAT32 is the most universally compatible choice; use exFAT if you prefer that filesystem on a larger volume; each SMS media file must still be below 4 GiB. There is no coverart, ISO or special-folder requirement — SMS is a media player that reads your files where they are, not a game loader. ℹ A note on USB speed The PlayStation 2's USB hardware is USB 1.1, so raw throughput off any USB drive is modest by modern standards. It can handle conservative media profiles, but throughput and queue behavior depend on the file and build. The current USB stutter report remains open. Very high bitrate files may be the exception — if a demanding clip stutters, a lower-bitrate encode or a faster device such as MX4SIO or the internal HDD will help. Keeping files unfragmented (below) matters more than the drive's own speed rating."},{"title":"Preparing the drive","cat":"Storage & network","url":"usb.html#preparing","text":"USB mass storage in SMS: FAT32/exFAT, device startup, file-size limits and the open USB stutter report."},{"title":"Format as FAT32 or exFAT","cat":"Storage & network","url":"usb.html#format-as-fat32-or-exfat","text":"On your PC, format the USB drive as a single FAT32 or exFAT partition on an MBR partition table. If you need to reformat, back up the contents first: formatting erases the volume. An existing compatible filesystem does not need to be reformatted just to try SMS."},{"title":"Copy your media anywhere","cat":"Storage & network","url":"usb.html#copy-your-media-anywhere","text":"Drag your videos, music and pictures onto the drive. You can drop them at the root, or organise them into folders however you like — SMS shows whatever directory tree is on the drive. There is no mandatory folder structure: no DVD/ , CD/ or ART/ tree, no ISOs. Just files."},{"title":"Use supported formats","cat":"Storage & network","url":"usb.html#use-supported-formats","text":"Copy files SMS can play: video such as AVI (DivX/Xvid/MPEG-4 Part 2), MP4 (MPEG-4 Part 2 mp4v ) and MPEG program streams; audio such as MP2/MP3 , AAC , FLAC , OGG , WMA and AC3 ; and JPEG pictures. H.264/H.265 video is not supported even when stored in an MP4 file. External SRT/SUB subtitles and M3U playlists work too. The Video and Audio pages list every format in detail."},{"title":"Eject cleanly, then plug into the PS2","cat":"Storage & network","url":"usb.html#eject-cleanly-then-plug-into-the-ps2","text":"Safely eject the drive from your PC so all writes are flushed, then plug it into the PlayStation 2's USB port. Fat consoles have their ports on the front; slims have them at the top-front. Then boot SMS."},{"title":"Enabling USB in SMS","cat":"Storage & network","url":"usb.html#enabling","text":"On a clean install SMS leaves optional Autostart flags off — you turn on the devices you want. There are two ways to get USB running."},{"title":"Autostart USB (recommended)","cat":"Storage & network","url":"usb.html#autostart","text":"In the settings menu, turn on Autostart USB . From then on, SMS loads the USB driver at boot, so a plugged-in drive is detected and mounted automatically every time you start the player. This is the set-and-forget option if you use USB regularly."},{"title":"Start USB now (on demand)","cat":"Storage & network","url":"usb.html#start-now","text":"If USB is not currently running — for example you left Autostart off — SMS offers a Start USB support item so you can bring the device up on the spot, without rebooting. If USB is already running and you plug a drive in afterwards, that item is gone; use Refresh connections at the bottom of Device settings instead, which re-scans the mass slots for newly attached drives. Trigger it, give the driver a moment to load and enumerate the drive, and the USB device appears in the device menu. ℹ Pick USB in the device menu Once USB is running, use Left/Right in the device menu to move the highlight onto the USB ( massN: ) device, then press Down to enter its file list. The status line at the bottom shows the current device and path so you always know where you are."},{"title":"Browsing & playing","cat":"Storage & network","url":"usb.html#browsing","text":"Inside the file list, navigation is the same as anywhere in SMS: Input Action Up / Down Move the selection through the file list L1 / L2 Page-scroll through a long list Cross (✕) Open the highlighted folder, or start playing the highlighted file Triangle (△) Go up one level — folder → parent → back out to the device menu Left / Right Return to the device menu to switch devices Select a video, music track or picture with Cross and it plays. During video or audio playback, Select pauses, Start resumes, and Triangle stops and returns you to the browser. Subtitles and M3U playlists that live alongside your media on the USB drive are picked up automatically — see Subtitles and Audio & Playlists ."},{"title":"Tips & troubleshooting","cat":"Storage & network","url":"usb.html#tips","text":"Keep every media file below 4 GiB. exFAT can store larger files, but SMS still uses 32-bit file addressing. Split or re-encode an oversized movie; changing the filesystem does not remove that player limit. Keep files unfragmented. On a heavily-fragmented drive, playback can stutter as the PS2 chases scattered pieces of a file. The simplest fix is to copy everything off, reformat the drive, and copy it back so each file lands contiguously. On slow or old media this matters more than anything else. Avoid too many small fragments. A near-full drive that has had files added and deleted many times fragments faster; leaving some free space and doing occasional clean reformats keeps large videos playing smoothly. Confirm the device name on the status line. If you are unsure which massN: is your USB drive (because MX4SIO, i.LINK or an exFAT HDD are also running), read the bottom status line — it shows the current path, which starts with the active device. Nothing appears? Make sure USB is actually started (Autostart USB, or Start USB now), that the drive is FAT32/exFAT on an MBR table, and that it is fully seated in the port. Powered USB hard disks should have their own power connected. See Troubleshooting if a drive still won't show up. ℹ USB and the other BDM devices USB, MX4SIO, the exFAT (BDM) internal HDD and i.LINK all share the massN: namespace and can generally run side by side. The one pairing to know about elsewhere is that MX4SIO and MMCE both use the memory-card (SIO2) bus, so only one of those two can be active at a time — USB itself is unaffected. See the Devices Overview for the full picture. Why does my USB drive show up as massN: and not \"usb0:\"? This build mounts USB through BDM (the Block Device Manager), a shared block-device layer that also handles MX4SIO, the exFAT internal HDD and i.LINK. BDM presents every attached block volume under one unified massN: namespace, numbered in the order the volumes are discovered. So the name reflects mount order across all BDM devices , not the USB port or the drive brand. Whichever volume comes up first is mass0: ; the next is mass1: , and so on. When in doubt, the status line at the bottom of the screen always t"},{"title":"MX4SIO (microSD)","cat":"Storage & network","url":"mx4sio.html","text":"MX4SIO microSD playback in SMS: setup, mass device naming, driver constraints and file-size/performance limits."},{"title":"What MX4SIO is","cat":"Storage & network","url":"mx4sio.html#what-is-mx4sio","text":"An MX4SIO adapter takes a microSD card and presents it to the PS2 through the memory-card slot, communicating over the console's SIO2 controller bus (the same bus the pad and memory card use). To SMS the card looks like any other BDM (Block Device Manager) mass-storage volume: it is mounted from the FAT/exFAT layer and surfaces as massN: , exactly the abstraction USB, exFAT-over-ATA and i.LINK all share. That means SMS does not need a special \"MX4SIO file browser\" — it simply browses the card's raw filesystem and plays whatever media files it finds, wherever they sit. There is no mandatory folder tree to build. In the device menu the card appears with the same massN: naming as the other mass devices, where N is 0 , 1 , 2 … depending on what else is mounted. Point the browser at the card, walk into your folders, and open a file."},{"title":"Why it is the headline feature","cat":"Storage & network","url":"mx4sio.html#why-notable","text":"Plenty of PS2 tools can mount an MX4SIO card and list its directories — but that is the easy part. A media player is different: it streams large reads continuously while the rest of the console is busy decoding audio, driving the GUI and polling the pad. Under exactly those conditions, earlier attempts to use MX4SIO in SMS would mount the card and show the file list, then stall the moment you tried to actually play a file . This build fixes that. Video and audio now play smoothly from a microSD in an MX4SIO adapter — the same experience you get from USB or the internal HDD. To make it a first-class citizen, MX4SIO also gets: Its own device icon in the top device bar, visually distinct from the USB icon, so you always know which card you are browsing. An Autostart MX4SIO toggle in the settings menu, so it can come up automatically at boot if you want it to. A Start MX4SIO support item, always present in Device settings, so you can bring the card online on demand without rebooting. MX4SIO and MMCE cannot both run MX4SIO and MMCE both use the SIO2 / memory-card bus, so SMS can only drive one of them at a time . Turn on Autostart for the one you actually use, and leave the other off. See MX4SIO vs MMCE below."},{"title":"Compatibility","cat":"Storage & network","url":"mx4sio.html#compatibility","text":"SMS reads the card through the standard FAT/exFAT layer, so a microSD formatted as FAT32 or exFAT will mount. Use FAT32 or exFAT supported by your device/firmware. exFAT filesystem support does not remove SMS's below-4-GiB limit for each media file. Either way, the media files themselves must be a format SMS can play — see Video , Audio and Pictures for the full list (MPEG-4 Part 2 AVI, mp4v MP4, MPEG-1/2 program streams, MP2/MP3, AAC, AC3, FLAC, OGG, WMA, JPEG, and so on). ℹ Hardware note Playback was confirmed on real PS2 hardware with a FAT32-formatted microSD in an MX4SIO adapter — a 640×480 XviD + MP3 AVI and a standalone MP3 both play smoothly. As with any SD adapter, cheap or worn cards can misbehave; if a particular card gives trouble, try a different, known-good one."},{"title":"Preparing the card","cat":"Storage & network","url":"mx4sio.html#prepare-card","text":"MX4SIO microSD playback in SMS: setup, mass device naming, driver constraints and file-size/performance limits."},{"title":"Format the microSD","cat":"Storage & network","url":"mx4sio.html#format-the-microsd","text":"Use a compatible FAT32 or exFAT volume. Keep each SMS media file below 4 GiB on either filesystem. Back up the contents before reformatting; an already compatible volume can be used as it is."},{"title":"Copy your media on","cat":"Storage & network","url":"mx4sio.html#copy-your-media-on","text":"Copy your video, audio, picture and playlist files onto the card from your PC. You can organise them into folders however you like — SMS browses the raw filesystem, so there is no required directory layout. Keep external subtitle files ( .srt / .sub ) next to the video they belong to; see Subtitles ."},{"title":"Seat the adapter","cat":"Storage & network","url":"mx4sio.html#seat-the-adapter","text":"With the card loaded, insert the MX4SIO adapter into one of the PS2's memory-card slots. Remember that this is the same slot family MMCE devices and the memory card use — plan around the one-device-at-a-time rule below."},{"title":"Boot SMS and enable MX4SIO","cat":"Storage & network","url":"mx4sio.html#boot-sms-and-enable-mx4sio","text":"Boot the PS2 into SMS, then turn MX4SIO on (next section). On a clean install optional Autostart choices are off, so you choose what to bring up."},{"title":"Enabling MX4SIO","cat":"Storage & network","url":"mx4sio.html#enabling","text":"On a fresh install SMS leaves optional Autostart flags off — you switch on the devices you want. There are two ways to bring MX4SIO up."},{"title":"Autostart MX4SIO","cat":"Storage & network","url":"mx4sio.html#autostart","text":"In the settings menu, turn on Autostart MX4SIO . From then on SMS loads the MX4SIO driver at boot and the card is ready to browse as soon as the desktop appears. This is the option to use if MX4SIO is your everyday media source. Your choice is saved with the rest of your settings (see Settings ), so it sticks across reboots."},{"title":"Start MX4SIO now","cat":"Storage & network","url":"mx4sio.html#start-now","text":"Device settings always lists Start MX4SIO support , so you can bring the card online on demand — no reboot needed. It is shown even when MX4SIO is already up (a harmless no-op) and even when MMCE holds the SIO2 bus, in which case it reports an error explaining why MX4SIO cannot start. This is handy when you didn't autostart it but decided you want it this session, or when you have just seated the adapter."},{"title":"Pick it in the device menu","cat":"Storage & network","url":"mx4sio.html#pick-in-menu","text":"Once MX4SIO is running, the card shows up in the device menu (the top row of device icons) with its own icon, separate from USB. Use Left / Right to land on it, then press Down to drop into the file list and start browsing. From there it behaves like any SMS browser: Up / Down to move, L1 / L2 to page-scroll, Cross to open a file or folder, and Triangle to step up a level (file list → parent → back to the device menu). ℹ Telling MX4SIO apart from USB USB and MX4SIO both live in the massN: namespace, so under the hood they are the same kind of device. SMS keeps them visually distinct in the device bar by giving MX4SIO its own icon — so even with a USB stick and an MX4SIO card mounted at the same time, you can see at a glance which one you are on."},{"title":"MX4SIO vs MMCE","cat":"Storage & network","url":"mx4sio.html#vs-mmce","text":"This is the one gotcha worth internalising. Both MX4SIO and MMCE devices physically occupy a memory-card slot and talk over the SIO2 bus — so SMS can only drive one of them at a time . They are not interchangeable, either: they use different drivers and mount under different names. MX4SIO MMCE (SD2PSX / MemCard PRO2) Card type microSD in an SD/SPI adapter SD card in an SD2PSX or MemCard PRO2 How SMS sees it BDM mass storage — massN: MMCE device — mmce0: / mmce1: Shares the slot with USB / HDD / i.LINK (the massN: pool) Its own mmce namespace Enable via Autostart MX4SIO / Start MX4SIO now Autostart MMCE / Start MMCE now Device icon Its own icon, distinct from USB Its own icon Rule of thumb: turn on Autostart for the adapter you actually own, and leave the other off. If your device is an SD2PSX or MemCard PRO2, use the MMCE page. If it's a plain SD-over-SIO2 adapter, you're in the right place. The other exclusion — HDD paths Separately from the SIO2 rule, note that the two internal-HDD access paths — classic APA/PFS and exFAT over ATA (BDM) — both own the internal ATA bus, so only one of those can run at once either. That's unrelated to MX4SIO, but if you're enabling several devices it's worth knowing. See Internal HDD ."},{"title":"How it works (at a glance)","cat":"Storage & network","url":"mx4sio.html#how-it-works","text":"You don't need to know any of this to use MX4SIO — but in short: a continuously-streaming media player asks a lot more of an SD-over-SIO2 card than a lightweight file manager does, because it reads constantly while also decoding audio and driving the GUI. This build carries the MX4SIO driver and access-path hardening needed to keep playback reliable under exactly those demands. It is entirely automatic — there is nothing to enable, tune, or configure. ℹ Nothing to configure The reliability work is all under the hood; the deeper engineering details live in the project's developer notes, not here. To use MX4SIO you simply format the card, copy your media, enable MX4SIO, and play."},{"title":"Notes & tips","cat":"Storage & network","url":"mx4sio.html#tips","text":"MX4SIO reads any format SMS can play — it is a storage device, not a format. Point the browser at your files and open them like on any other device. You can browse into any folder structure; there is no mandatory layout. Keep .srt / .sub subtitle files beside their video. Enable either MX4SIO or MMCE, never both — they share the SIO2 slot bus. Prefer FAT32 for maximum compatibility; use exFAT if supported by your setup. Keep individual SMS media files below 4 GiB on either filesystem. If a specific card acts up, try another known-good microSD before assuming a software fault. Your Autostart MX4SIO choice is saved with your settings and persists across reboots — see Settings for where the config lives. Why is MX4SIO reliable here when it can be finicky elsewhere? An SD-over-SIO2 card behaves differently in a continuously-streaming media player than in a lightweight file manager that reads a little and then sits idle. This build carries the MX4SIO driver and access-path hardening needed for the media-player case, so playback stays smooth even while SMS is busy decoding audio and drawing the GUI. To the user it simply works — the reliability work is entirely under the hood. Do I have to build a folder tree like OPL's DVD/CD/ART? No. SMS is a media player, not a game loader — it browses the raw filesystem of the card and plays media files wherever they are. There is no OPL-style mandatory folder structure and no ISO/coverart convention to follow. Copy your files on, organise them however you like, and browse to them. File-size and test scope: keep every SMS media file below 4 GiB, including on exFAT. Existing project reports do not qualify every drive/adapter or the current development build. See known issues and keep the same file/build/device when comparing results."},{"title":"Internal HDD","cat":"Storage & network","url":"hdd.html","text":"SMS internal HDD access through APA/PFS or ATA/exFAT, driver exclusions, preparation and source limits."},{"title":"Two ways to use the HDD","cat":"Storage & network","url":"hdd.html#two-ways","text":"SMS is a media player, not a game loader — so the HDD is just another place to keep your video, music and pictures. The catch is that the internal drive can be formatted in one of two schemes, and SMS reaches each one through a different driver stack: APA / PFS The PS2's own partition scheme exFAT via ATA A plain drive as massN: They are mutually exclusive : both paths want to own the internal ATA bus, so whichever one starts first takes it and the other quietly steps aside. You choose which by picking the matching Autostart toggle — see Choosing one below. How it appears APA / PFS exFAT via ATA (BDM) Format on the disk Sony's APA partition scheme + PFS filesystem Plain exFAT partition What SMS browses Any PFS partition on the drive — you pick one from a list The whole exFAT volume, any folders Device name in SMS Internal HDD (APA) massN: (shared BDM mass namespace) Made with HDD-OSD / OPL-era APA & PFS tools Any PC — format exFAT, copy files Enable with Autostart HDD (the APA/PFS row) Autostart HDD BDM"},{"title":"APA / PFS — the PS2's native HDD format","cat":"Storage & network","url":"hdd.html#apa","text":"The APA partition scheme and PFS filesystem are the PS2's own native HDD formats — the same ones used by Sony's HDD-OSD (the \"PlayStation BB Navigator\" era) and by the classic homebrew HDD tools. On this kind of drive, SMS lists the PFS partitions it finds on the APA table as a folder-like list. Pick one with Cross to mount it and browse the media inside; SMS remembers the partition you last used and re-enters it automatically on the next boot. There is no required partition name. ℹ No special partition name needed Older guides tell you to create a partition called SMS_Media . That has not been necessary for a long time and this build does not look for it — SMS shows you every PFS partition on the drive and mounts the one you choose. Any partition made with a standard APA/PFS tool works, whatever it is called. If you already have an SMS_Media partition it will simply appear in the list like any other."},{"title":"Preparing an APA drive","cat":"Storage & network","url":"hdd.html#apa-prepare","text":"SMS doesn't format drives or create partitions itself — you do that ahead of time with the usual PS2 HDD tools (HDD-OSD, or a homebrew APA/PFS partition manager such as uLaunchELF). The short version:"},{"title":"Format the drive as APA","cat":"Storage & network","url":"hdd.html#format-the-drive-as-apa","text":"Initialise the internal drive with the standard PS2 APA scheme, the same way you would for any HDD-OSD / OPL-era setup. This is a one-time step done from a HDD tool, not from SMS."},{"title":"Create a PFS media partition","cat":"Storage & network","url":"hdd.html#create-a-pfs-media-partition","text":"Make a PFS partition to hold your media — call it whatever you like. Copy your video, audio and picture files into it (folders inside are fine — SMS browses the raw tree)."},{"title":"Enable the APA path in SMS","cat":"Storage & network","url":"hdd.html#enable-the-apa-path-in-sms","text":"Turn on Autostart HDD APA in the settings (or use Start HDD APA now — see Enabling & tips ). SMS starts the HDD driver stack and lists the drive's PFS partitions."},{"title":"Browse and play","cat":"Storage & network","url":"hdd.html#browse-and-play","text":"Pick the internal-HDD icon in the device menu and press Down into the list. Choose your partition with Cross to mount it, then open any file with Cross as usual — SMS remembers the partition and re-enters it on the next boot. It behaves exactly like every other SMS source — the raw filesystem, no mandatory folder layout. APA drives are ~2 TB and older-drive friendly The APA scheme addresses drives up to about 2 TB . If you have a larger drive — or you'd simply rather copy files from a PC without any PS2-side tools — use the exFAT route below instead."},{"title":"exFAT via ATA","cat":"Storage & network","url":"hdd.html#exfat","text":"The modern alternative skips APA entirely. Format the internal drive as plain exFAT on your PC, drop your media anywhere on it, and let SMS read the drive through its ATA block driver . Because that driver plugs into the same Block Device Manager (BDM) that handles USB, MX4SIO and i.LINK, the internal drive simply appears as another massN: device in SMS — no PS2-specific formatting, no partition juggling. ℹ One shared massN: namespace USB, MX4SIO, the exFAT internal HDD and i.LINK all mount into the same massN: pool ( N = 0, 1, 2…). The number is assigned as devices come up, so a drive is mass0: if it mounts first, mass1: if a USB stick got there ahead of it, and so on. You browse it by picking its device icon in the top row, not by memorising the number."},{"title":"Preparing an exFAT drive","cat":"Storage & network","url":"hdd.html#exfat-prepare","text":"SMS internal HDD access through APA/PFS or ATA/exFAT, driver exclusions, preparation and source limits."},{"title":"Format exFAT on your PC","cat":"Storage & network","url":"hdd.html#format-exfat-on-your-pc","text":"Connect the drive to a computer (or format it in-bay with a PC-side tool) and format it as exFAT . Leave the allocation-unit / cluster size at the default for the drive's capacity rather than choosing a custom value."},{"title":"Copy your media","cat":"Storage & network","url":"hdd.html#copy-your-media","text":"Drag your video, audio and picture files onto the drive — into any folders you like. SMS browses the raw filesystem, so there is no required folder tree (no OPL-style DVD/ / CD/ layout)."},{"title":"Fit the drive and enable the BDM path","cat":"Storage & network","url":"hdd.html#fit-the-drive-and-enable-the-bdm-path","text":"Install the drive in the console's HDD bay, then turn on Autostart HDD BDM in SMS (or Start HDD BDM now ). SMS brings up the ATA driver and the exFAT volume mounts as massN: ."},{"title":"Browse and play","cat":"Storage & network","url":"hdd.html#browse-and-play-x","text":"Select the internal-HDD icon in the device menu and open files just like any other massN: device. Copy large files one at a time The PS2 reads far better from files that aren't fragmented. When you copy a batch to an exFAT drive, add big video files individually rather than dumping the whole folder in one go — many PC copy operations interleave a large batch, fragmenting every file. Sequential copies keep playback smooth."},{"title":"Choosing one — they're mutually exclusive","cat":"Storage & network","url":"hdd.html#choosing","text":"Here's the important constraint: the APA/PFS path and the exFAT/ATA path both own the internal ATA bus , so only one can be active at a time. SMS enforces this with a simple \"first come, first served\" rule — whichever backend starts first claims the bus, and the other declines to start for that session. ℹ First to start owns the bus If the APA HDD path comes up first, the exFAT/ATA path won't start (and vice versa). This isn't an error — it's SMS keeping two drivers that both drive the same internal disk from stepping on each other. To switch schemes, disable the one you don't want (or don't autostart it) and reboot so the other can claim the bus cleanly. Practically, that means you decide up front which scheme your drive is formatted for and enable the matching autostart: Your drive is… Turn on Leave off APA-formatted (any PFS partition) Autostart HDD APA Autostart HDD BDM exFAT-formatted (a plain drive) Autostart HDD BDM Autostart HDD APA Autostart HDD BDM is the ATA/exFAT path — \"BDM\" because the exFAT drive is read through the same Block Device Manager as your other mass devices. Autostart HDD APA is the classic native-partition path. Don't enable both Turning on both autostarts doesn't get you both drives — it just means whichever one happens to initialise first wins the bus, and the result depends on start order. Pick the one that matches how your drive is formatted and leave the other off. Can the HDD run alongside USB, MX4SIO, MMCE and SMB? Yes — the internal-HDD bus contention is only between the two HDD schemes . Whichever HDD path you choose can run happily at the same time as USB, i.LINK and SMB. Note two other exclusions elsewhere in SMS: MX4SIO and MMCE share the memory-card (SIO2) bus, so only one of those two runs at once; and the exFAT/ATA HDD is a massN: device like USB and MX4SIO, so they simply each take their own slot in that shared namespace."},{"title":"Enabling & tips","cat":"Storage & network","url":"hdd.html#enabling","text":"On a clean install optional Autostart choices are off — you turn on exactly what you need. The relevant controls live in the settings menu: Autostart HDD APA — brings up the classic APA/PFS path at boot and lists the drive's PFS partitions. Autostart HDD BDM — brings up the exFAT drive over ATA at boot as a massN: device. Start HDD … now — when a device isn't already running, SMS also offers an on-demand start item, so you can bring the HDD up without a reboot. Handy for testing which scheme your drive actually mounts. ℹ If a start stalls, watch the status line Starting the internal HDD spins the drive up and probes it, which takes a moment. SMS shows the current stage on the status line as it goes; if a start ever hangs on an unusual drive layout, the last message on screen tells you exactly which stage it reached. Because the HDD isn't set to auto-start on a clean install, a failed manual start won't stop SMS from booting next time — just reboot and try the other scheme, or fall back to USB/MX4SIO."},{"title":"Practical tips","cat":"Storage & network","url":"hdd.html#tips","text":"Match the toggle to the format. An exFAT drive will not mount under Autostart HDD APA, and an APA/PFS partition won't appear under Autostart HDD BDM. If a drive doesn't show up, you almost certainly enabled the wrong path for how it's formatted. Big drive? Use exFAT. APA tops out around 2 TB; exFAT lets you use larger drives and skip PS2-side tooling entirely — just format on a PC and copy files. Big library already on APA? Keep it. If your drive is already APA-formatted, the APA path reads your existing PFS partitions directly with no conversion — whatever they are named. The drive is optional hardware. Everything above assumes a Network Adapter and an internal drive are fitted. Without them, SMS is perfectly happy on USB , MX4SIO , MMCE or a network share ."},{"title":"Related pages","cat":"Storage & network","url":"hdd.html#related","text":"Devices Overview How every source fits together USB The other big massN: device i.LINK FireWire storage, also BDM mass Settings Reference Every autostart toggle File-size and test scope: keep every SMS media file below 4 GiB, including on exFAT. Existing project reports do not qualify every drive/adapter or the current development build. See known issues and keep the same file/build/device when comparing results."},{"title":"i.LINK (IEEE 1394 / FireWire)","cat":"Storage & network","url":"ilink.html","text":"SMS i.LINK/FireWire SBP-2 storage, required physical connector, setup, mass naming and device test limits."},{"title":"What is i.LINK?","cat":"Storage & network","url":"ilink.html#what-is-ilink","text":"i.LINK is Sony's branding for the IEEE 1394 serial bus — the same standard sold elsewhere as FireWire (Apple) or Lynx (Texas Instruments). Many PS2 models expose a 4-pin i.LINK port on the front or side of the console. It was originally intended for connecting the PS2 to other PS2s and to digital video gear; in practice, on homebrew, it is a handy way to attach an external hard drive or SSD enclosure that speaks FireWire. When SMS starts its i.LINK support, it loads the IOP driver modules that walk the FireWire bus, find any attached storage, log in to it and expose its partitions to the console's block-device layer. From that point on the drive is just another BDM mass device — the same generic storage plumbing that USB sticks, MX4SIO microSD cards and exFAT internal HDDs all ride on. That is why an i.LINK drive appears as massN: and behaves identically to a USB drive once it is mounted: SMS does not treat i.LINK as a special \"kind\" of media, only as another source of massN: volumes. Not every PS2 has i.LINK Later fat models and slimline PS2s can lack the i.LINK port; do not assume that a fat case guarantees one. If your console has no FireWire connector, this feature simply has nothing to attach to — use USB , MX4SIO or the internal HDD instead. Check the front and sides of your console for a small 4-pin i.LINK-labelled socket before buying a cable."},{"title":"Compatibility","cat":"Storage & network","url":"ilink.html#compatibility","text":"i.LINK storage in SMS is for external FireWire drives : a hard disk or SSD in an enclosure (or a dock) that connects over IEEE 1394. The drive is read through the same filesystem layer as every other BDM mass device, so the same formatting rules apply. Property What works Connection i.LINK / IEEE 1394 / FireWire, into the PS2's i.LINK port Device type External hard drive or SSD enclosure that implements SBP-2 storage Filesystem FAT32 or exFAT Appears as massN: (shares the BDM namespace with USB, MX4SIO and ATA/exFAT HDD) Role in SMS A browsable media source — play video, audio and pictures directly from it ℹ A modest note on tested drives i.LINK support is confirmed working on real hardware in this fork, mounting an exFAT drive over FireWire. That said, the FireWire enclosure market is wide and varied, and only a small number of drives have actually been tried. If your particular enclosure does not come up, it may be an enclosure/bridge-chip quirk rather than a problem with your media — try a different enclosure or fall back to USB ."},{"title":"Preparing the drive","cat":"Storage & network","url":"ilink.html#preparing","text":"Set the drive up on your PC the same way you would prepare a USB drive for SMS, then copy your media onto it."},{"title":"Format FAT32 or exFAT","cat":"Storage & network","url":"ilink.html#format-fat32-or-exfat","text":"On your computer, format the FireWire drive as FAT32 or exFAT . exFAT is the easy choice for large drives and large files; FAT32 is fine for smaller drives."},{"title":"Copy your media on","cat":"Storage & network","url":"ilink.html#copy-your-media-on","text":"Drop your video, audio and picture files onto the drive. SMS browses the raw filesystem — there is no mandatory folder layout to follow. Put your files wherever you like (a simple folder such as /Movies or /Music just keeps things tidy) and SMS will let you navigate to them."},{"title":"Eject, connect, and power the drive","cat":"Storage & network","url":"ilink.html#eject-connect-and-power-the-drive","text":"Safely eject the drive from your PC, then plug it into the PS2's i.LINK port with a FireWire cable. If your enclosure needs external power, make sure it is powered and the disk is spun up before (or as) SMS looks for it. ℹ Which formats will play Any file SMS understands will play from an i.LINK drive: video (DivX / XviD / MPEG-4 Part 2 AVI, mp4v MP4, MPEG-1/2 program streams), audio (MP2/MP3, AAC, AC3, FLAC, OGG Vorbis, WMA) and JPEG pictures, plus M3U playlists and external SRT / SUB subtitles. See Video Playback and Audio & Playlists for the full detail."},{"title":"Enabling i.LINK in SMS","cat":"Storage & network","url":"ilink.html#enabling","text":"On a clean install optional Autostart choices are off — you turn on the devices you want. i.LINK is off until you enable it, in one of two ways."},{"title":"Turn on Autostart i.LINK (persistent)","cat":"Storage & network","url":"ilink.html#turn-on-autostart-i-link-persistent","text":"In the settings menu, enable the Autostart i.LINK toggle. From then on, every time SMS boots it will bring the FireWire bus up and look for an attached drive automatically. This is the \"set it and forget it\" option if you keep a drive on the i.LINK port. Your choice is saved with the rest of your settings (see Settings )."},{"title":"Or use \"Start i.LINK now\" (on demand)","cat":"Storage & network","url":"ilink.html#or-use-start-i-link-now-on-demand","text":"If i.LINK is not already running, a Start i.LINK now item is available so you can bring it up on the spot — no reboot needed. Handy when you only occasionally attach a FireWire drive and would rather not auto-start it every boot."},{"title":"Give it a couple of seconds","cat":"Storage & network","url":"ilink.html#give-it-a-couple-of-seconds","text":"FireWire discovery is not instant. After i.LINK starts, the driver needs a short moment — on the order of a couple of seconds — to discover the drive on the bus and log in to it before it appears in the device menu. If it does not show up immediately, wait a beat; this delay is normal. Have the drive connected and ready first Because discovery happens shortly after i.LINK starts, plug the drive in and make sure it is powered and spun up before you enable Autostart i.LINK or press Start i.LINK now. A drive that is still spinning up, or connected after the scan, may be missed until the next start."},{"title":"Browse & play","cat":"Storage & network","url":"ilink.html#browse-and-play","text":"Once the drive is discovered it appears in the top device menu as a massN: volume. From there it behaves exactly like any other storage device in SMS:"},{"title":"Pick the device","cat":"Storage & network","url":"ilink.html#pick-the-device","text":"In the device menu at the top, use Left / Right to highlight your i.LINK volume (a massN: entry), then press Down to drop into the file list."},{"title":"Navigate to your file","cat":"Storage & network","url":"ilink.html#navigate-to-your-file","text":"In the file list, move with Up / Down , page through long lists with L1 / L2 , open a folder or start a file with Cross , and go up one level with Triangle . The bottom status line shows your current path on the left and a clock on the right."},{"title":"Play","cat":"Storage & network","url":"ilink.html#play","text":"Open a video, track or picture and it plays straight from the FireWire drive. During playback, Select pauses, Start resumes, and Triangle stops and returns you to the browser. ℹ Which massN: is my i.LINK drive? USB, MX4SIO, i.LINK and exFAT/ATA HDD all share the single massN: numbering pool, and the number a device gets depends on what started and in what order. If you have several BDM devices connected at once, the simplest way to tell them apart is to open each massN: entry and recognise its contents. With only the i.LINK drive attached it will typically be mass0: ."},{"title":"Notes & good to know","cat":"Storage & network","url":"ilink.html#notes","text":"Its own bus. i.LINK runs over the FireWire (IEEE 1394) bus, independent of USB and of the SIO2 / memory-card bus. It happily works alongside USB and MX4SIO at the same time — there is no conflict between them. Confirmed on hardware. i.LINK storage has been tested and works on a real PS2 in this fork, mounting an exFAT drive over FireWire next to the other working devices. A couple of seconds to appear. The short discovery/login delay after starting i.LINK is expected — give the bus a moment before assuming the drive is not there. FAT32 or exFAT only. Keep the drive formatted FAT32 or exFAT; other filesystems are not read. No special folder tree. SMS reads the raw filesystem and plays media wherever it sits — there is no required directory layout to build. Why does i.LINK take a moment to show up, when USB is instant? FireWire storage is reached over a discovery-and-login handshake: after the bus comes up, the driver has to enumerate the devices on it, find the storage node, and log in to it before its partitions can be mounted. That handshake takes a short while — on the order of a couple of seconds — whereas USB tends to enumerate faster. The practical upshot is simply to have the drive connected, powered and spun up before you start i.LINK, and to wait a beat for the massN: entry to appear rather than assuming nothing happened. Can I use i.LINK and the internal HDD together? Yes. i.LINK is an external FireWire device on its own bus, so it does not clash with the internal HDD's ATA bus. The one exclusivity that lives on the internal ATA bus is between the classic APA/PFS HDD path and the exFAT/ATA (BDM) HDD path — you pick one of those two for the internal drive (see Internal HDD ). i.LINK sits outside that entirely and can run at the same time as either. ℹ Related pages USB and MX4SIO are the other BDM mass devices and behave the same way once mounted. For the internal drive (APA and exFAT) see Internal HDD . For the full list of per-device Autostart toggles, see the Settings Reference . For a bird's-eye view of every storage backend, see the Devices Overview . The driver uses SBP-2/SCSI over IEEE 1394, not PS2 ATA commands. Historical project documentation re"},{"title":"MMCE (SD2PSX / MemCard PRO2)","cat":"Storage & network","url":"mmce.html","text":"SMS MMCE support for SD2PSX and MemCard PRO2: firmware/card setup, slot names and SIO2 driver exclusions."},{"title":"What is MMCE?","cat":"Storage & network","url":"mmce.html#what-is-mmce","text":"MMCE stands for Memory Card Mass-storage Emulation . Devices like the SD2PSX and the MemCard PRO2 plug into a PS2 memory-card slot and, as well as pretending to be a memory card, can present the full contents of their SD card to homebrew over a small command protocol. That protocol was designed by bbsan2k , and it is the same protocol the wider PS2 homebrew scene uses to reach these adapters. When SMS starts MMCE support, it loads the IOP driver that talks to the adapter and then checks the memory-card slots for a device that answers. Any card it finds is added to the device menu as a browsable volume: mmce0: for the first slot, mmce1: for the second. From that point on MMCE is just another source of files — SMS reads the raw folders on the SD card and plays whatever media sits there, with the same menus, the same subtitle handling and the same playback controls as every other device. ℹ Unlike a game loader, SMS just browses the card SMS is a media player , not a game loader. There is no mandatory folder layout — no DVD/ , CD/ or artwork folders, no disc images. Drop your files wherever you like on the SD card and navigate to them in the file list. If you keep your movies in a Movies folder and your music in Music , that is exactly what you will see."},{"title":"MMCE vs MX4SIO","cat":"Storage & network","url":"mmce.html#mmce-vs-mx4sio","text":"Both MMCE adapters and MX4SIO put an SD card in the memory-card slot, so it is easy to confuse them — but they are two completely different things, driven two different ways, and they cannot run at the same time . MMCE (SD2PSX / MemCard PRO2) MX4SIO Hardware SD2PSX or MemCard PRO2 adapter MX4SIO (a.k.a. SIO2SD) microSD adapter How SMS reads it The bbsan2k MMCE protocol The MX4SIO block-device ( BDM ) driver Appears as mmce0: / mmce1: massN: (shared with USB, ATA, i.LINK) Enable with Autostart MMCE Autostart MX4SIO Only one memory-card-slot SD device at a time MMCE and MX4SIO both use the console's SIO2 / memory-card-slot bus . SMS will only bring up one of them, so if MX4SIO is already running, MMCE will not start until you turn MX4SIO off. Decide which adapter you are using and enable just that one. Your other storage — USB, the internal HDD, i.LINK, SMB — is unaffected and can run alongside either. Put simply: if your adapter is an SD2PSX or a MemCard PRO2 , use MMCE (this page). If it is an MX4SIO microSD adapter, use the MX4SIO page instead. The device you own decides which one to turn on."},{"title":"Preparing the card","cat":"Storage & network","url":"mmce.html#preparing","text":"Getting media onto an MMCE card is the same job as preparing any SD card — do it on your PC, then load it into the adapter."},{"title":"Format the SD card","cat":"Storage & network","url":"mmce.html#format-the-sd-card","text":"Format the SD card as FAT32 or exFAT . FAT32 is the most universally compatible; exFAT is another option where supported by the adapter firmware. Keep each SMS media file below 4 GiB on either filesystem."},{"title":"Copy your media on","cat":"Storage & network","url":"mmce.html#copy-your-media-on","text":"Copy your video, audio and picture files onto the card in whatever folder structure you like — SMS reads the raw filesystem, so there is no required layout. Keep any external subtitle file ( .srt / .sub ) next to its video with a matching name so SMS pairs them automatically. See Video and Audio for the exact formats SMS plays."},{"title":"Load the card into the adapter","cat":"Storage & network","url":"mmce.html#load-the-card-into-the-adapter","text":"Insert the SD card into your SD2PSX or MemCard PRO2 , then seat the adapter in a PlayStation 2 memory-card slot. Slot 1 becomes mmce0: and slot 2 becomes mmce1: . ℹ Card as memory card and media source An SD2PSX or MemCard PRO2 can still act as your virtual memory card at the same time as SMS reads media from its SD card — the two roles co-exist. SMS reads media and can write its own configuration/assets in the exposed filesystem; normal playback does not edit the adapter's saved-game memory-card images."},{"title":"Enabling MMCE in SMS","cat":"Storage & network","url":"mmce.html#enabling","text":"On a fresh install optional Autostart choices are off — you switch on the devices you actually use. MMCE is controlled from the settings menu, right alongside the other per-device Autostart toggles."},{"title":"Turn off MX4SIO first (if it is on)","cat":"Storage & network","url":"mmce.html#turn-off-mx4sio-first-if-it-is-on","text":"Because MMCE and MX4SIO share the memory-card bus, make sure Autostart MX4SIO is off before you try MMCE. If MX4SIO is already running, SMS will decline to start MMCE."},{"title":"Enable Autostart MMCE","cat":"Storage & network","url":"mmce.html#enable-autostart-mmce","text":"Open the settings menu and switch on Autostart MMCE in the device list. With it on, SMS loads the MMCE driver and scans the memory-card slots each time it boots, mounting any card it finds."},{"title":"Or start it on demand","cat":"Storage & network","url":"mmce.html#or-start-it-on-demand","text":"Don't want to reboot? Start MMCE support is always listed in Device settings — choose it to load the driver and scan the slots immediately. It is deliberately shown even when MMCE is already up (harmless no-op) and even when MX4SIO holds the SIO2 bus, in which case it reports an error telling you why MMCE cannot start. — the device shows up in the device menu without a restart."},{"title":"Save your settings","cat":"Storage & network","url":"mmce.html#save-your-settings","text":"Use Save settings after changes. On a remountable launch source, SMS.cfg normally lives beside the ELF even when a memory card is fitted; SMS.smb follows its resolved directory. See Config Files & Paths for card-slot, network-file and fallback rules. MMCE will not start while MX4SIO is running This is the single most common surprise. If you turn on Autostart MMCE but nothing appears, check that Autostart MX4SIO is off. They cannot share the bus, so whichever one you leave enabled is the one you get."},{"title":"Browsing & playing","cat":"Storage & network","url":"mmce.html#browsing","text":"Once MMCE is up, it behaves like any other device in the three-part SMS interface — a device menu across the top, the file list in the middle, and the status line (current path on the left, clock on the right) along the bottom."},{"title":"Pick the device","cat":"Storage & network","url":"mmce.html#pick-the-device","text":"In the device menu (top row), press Left / Right to highlight your MMCE device — the SD2PSX / MemCard PRO2 icon — then press Down to drop into its file list."},{"title":"Navigate the card","cat":"Storage & network","url":"mmce.html#navigate-the-card","text":"In the file list, use Up / Down to move and L1 / L2 to page through long folders. Press Cross to open a folder or start a file, and Triangle to go up one level (folder → parent → back to the device menu). Left / Right returns you to the device menu."},{"title":"Play","cat":"Storage & network","url":"mmce.html#play","text":"Select a video, track or picture and press Cross to play it. During playback, Select pauses, Start resumes, and Triangle stops and returns you to the browser. Subtitles, M3U playlists and everything else work exactly as they do on other devices."},{"title":"Notes & tips","cat":"Storage & network","url":"mmce.html#notes","text":"Slot 1 mounts as mmce0: and slot 2 mounts as mmce1: . If you have two MMCE adapters, both can appear at once — each as its own device. MMCE and MX4SIO are mutually exclusive — both use the memory-card (SIO2) bus, so enable only one. USB, the internal HDD, i.LINK and SMB are unaffected and can run alongside your MMCE card. There is no required folder layout . SMS reads the raw SD card and plays media from wherever you put it — this is a media player, not a game loader, so there are no disc images or artwork folders to set up. Format the SD card FAT32 or exFAT . Keep each SMS media file below 4 GiB even on exFAT. Keep external subtitles ( .srt / .sub ) next to the matching video so SMS pairs them automatically. See Subtitles . If the device doesn't appear, confirm MX4SIO Autostart is off, the card is seated, and MMCE Autostart (or Start MMCE now ) has been used. See Troubleshooting . What is the MMCE protocol, and how is it different from MX4SIO? MMCE (Memory Card Mass-storage Emulation) is a protocol created by bbsan2k for SD-card adapters that plug into the PS2's memory-card slot. The adapter presents its SD card to a dedicated IOP driver over a small custom command set that SMS speaks directly. The SD2PSX and MemCard PRO2 both implement it, and SMS mounts what they expose as mmce0: / mmce1: . MX4SIO is a different SD-in-memory-card adapter. It is driven as a block device (BDM) and mounts into the massN: namespace shared with USB, ATA and i.LINK — not as an mmceN: device. The two are entirely distinct: each needs its own driver, and because they share the console's memory-card bus, only one can be active at a time. Why can't MMCE and MX4SIO run together? Both adapters sit in the PlayStation 2 memory-card slot and talk to the console over the same SIO2 hardware path. The two drivers cannot own that bus simultaneously, so SMS brings up only one of them: if MX4SIO is already started, an attempt to start MMCE is refused, and vice versa. This is a constraint of SMS's current SIO2 driver ownership, not a claim that the physical slots can never host both adapter types — pick the device you are using and enable just that one. All of your other storage (USB, internal HDD"},{"title":"CD / DVD / CDDA","cat":"Storage & network","url":"cd-dvd.html","text":"SMS optical media: CDDAFS, DVD-Video, data discs and audio CD paths, with preserved historical guidance."},{"title":"The disc types SMS understands","cat":"Storage & network","url":"cd-dvd.html#disc-types","text":"SMS reads four kinds of optical media, each with its own history and its own use. All of them work on a stock console; none require a chip. Disc type What it is What you get CDDAFS Data hidden on an audio-CD layout so a stock PS2 accepts a burned CD-R Browse a small filesystem of media files burned onto the disc — the original SMS trick DVD-Video A disc authored in the DVD-Video (UDF) layout, holding your media files Play video/audio files straight from a burned DVD on an unmodded PS2 Data CD / DVD (ISO) An ordinary ISO 9660 / UDF data disc Browse the disc like any other filesystem and open media files on it Audio CD (CDDA) A standard Red Book music CD Play the CD's audio tracks directly Whichever disc you insert, the flow is the same: SMS identifies it, shows a disc device, and lets you browse or play. The differences below are about how the disc was made , not how you use it once it's in the tray."},{"title":"CDDAFS — the burned-CD trick that started SMS","cat":"Storage & network","url":"cd-dvd.html#cddafs","text":"A stock PlayStation 2 refuses to boot or read a burned data CD-R — the console's copy protection treats it as an \"illegal\" disc. Eugene Plotnikov's answer, borrowing an idea from Drakonite's UMCDR, was CDDAFS : a way of storing ordinary data files on a disc that is physically laid out as an audio CD . Because the PS2 happily authenticates an audio-CD-shaped disc, a CDDAFS disc reads on (in Eugene's words) hopefully any PS2 — no modchip needed. This is the very first component of SMS, and the reason the project is a \"system\" and not just a player. ℹ What CDDAFS actually is In Eugene's own description, CDDAFS \"just stores data on a CD audio disk, which can be read on any PS2.\" The disc's audio tracks act as directories , and a control track at the start holds global disc information — a name, a description, even little icon bitmaps that a browser can decorate itself with. It is a tiny, purpose-built filesystem riding inside an audio-CD table of contents. SMS reads that structure directly and shows you the files inside."},{"title":"Building a CDDAFS disc (conceptually)","cat":"Storage & network","url":"cd-dvd.html#cddafs-build","text":"You do not author a CDDAFS disc on the PS2 — you build it on a PC and burn it. Eugene wrote a companion Windows program, CDXplorer , whose job is to take your files and produce a cue/bin pair describing the special audio-CD-with-data layout. You then burn that cue/bin to a CD-R with a burning program, using a Disc-At-Once mode (Eugene used Nero's \"Disc-At-Once / 96\" option). CDXplorer can also read an existing CDDAFS disc back, so you can copy your files off again. CDDAFS is a create-on-PC, play-on-PS2 workflow The CDDAFS filesystem is intentionally simple, and the tooling is old and Windows-only. Treat the exact cue/bin build as a PC task with CDXplorer (or a compatible CDDAFS builder), not something you do from the SMS menus. This page describes CDDAFS conceptually so you understand what the disc is and why it works — for the precise button-by-button burn, follow the tool you use to make the disc. CDXplorer, Eugene's PC companion tool, building a new CDDAFS layout before it writes the cue/bin pair you burn to CD-R. Burning a CDDAFS disc: your burning software opens the .cue that CDXplorer produced and writes it to a blank CD-R."},{"title":"Add your files on the PC","cat":"Storage & network","url":"cd-dvd.html#add-your-files-on-the-pc","text":"In CDXplorer (or a compatible CDDAFS tool), start a new disc and add the media files you want on it — for example a DivX/XviD movie and its soundtrack. The classic use is a single feature-length movie per disc."},{"title":"Produce the cue/bin","cat":"Storage & network","url":"cd-dvd.html#produce-the-cue-bin","text":"Have the tool generate the cue/bin pair that describes the CDDAFS (audio-CD) layout. This is the image you will burn — not a normal data ISO."},{"title":"Burn to CD-R with Disc-At-Once","cat":"Storage & network","url":"cd-dvd.html#burn-to-cd-r-with-disc-at-once","text":"Burn the cue/bin to a blank CD-R using a Disc-At-Once write mode. Because the disc masquerades as an audio CD, the PS2 accepts it. Burn slowly and use good media (see Notes )."},{"title":"Play it in SMS","cat":"Storage & network","url":"cd-dvd.html#play-it-in-sms","text":"Put the disc in the PS2, launch SMS, wait for the disc device to appear, drop into the file list, and open your movie. It plays straight off the burned CD-R on a stock console. How does a CDDAFS disc lay its data out? (the historical detail) From Eugene's README: track 0 is a control track holding a GUID, a disc name and description, a small disc picture, and a table of 32×32 icon images, followed by a directory table. Each remaining audio track is a directory containing file entries — a name, an icon index, and the file's start offset and size — followed by the file data itself. There is one level of directories (historically capped at 99), and the number of files is limited only by the disc's capacity. Eugene notes fitting an ~800 MB movie onto an ordinary 700 MB disc without trouble, since the audio-CD layout foregoes the extra error-correction overhead of a data disc. This is deep background — you never touch these structures by hand; the PC tool writes them and SMS reads them."},{"title":"DVD-Video — playing files from a burned DVD","cat":"Storage & network","url":"cd-dvd.html#dvd-video","text":"SMS can also read media files from a DVD-Video disc on an unmodified console. Support for reading .avi (and other) files from DVD-Video discs was added with a cdvd.irx driver that understands the UDF filesystem DVD-Video uses. In practice this means you can author a burned DVD in the DVD-Video layout, drop your media files into it, and browse them in SMS exactly like any other device — with the big advantage that a DVD holds far more than a CD-R, so you are not limited to a single small movie per disc. ℹ Why \"DVD-Video\" and not a plain data DVD The DVD-Video layout is the burned-disc form a stock PS2 is happiest reading, which is why SMS's DVD path targets it. When you make the disc on your PC, choose your burning program's DVD-Video compilation type (rather than a plain DVD-ROM data project) and place your media files into it before burning. Authoring the disc on the PC: pick the DVD-Video compilation type in your burning software, then add your media files and burn to a blank DVD-R. Once burned, the DVD behaves like a data source in SMS: wait for the disc device to appear, enter the file list, and open a file. Video decoding uses the PS2's IPU for colour conversion, the same as from any other device — so DivX / XviD / MPEG-4 AVIs, MPEG program streams and the other supported formats all play from disc. Soft subtitles sitting alongside a video on the disc are picked up too."},{"title":"Data CD / DVD (ISO)","cat":"Storage & network","url":"cd-dvd.html#data-iso","text":"Beyond the two burned-disc tricks above, SMS also reads ordinary ISO 9660 / UDF data discs — the standard data-disc format. On consoles able to read them, an ISO data CD or DVD simply appears as a disc device you can browse, with your media files inside. This is handy when you already have media on a normal data disc, or when your setup can read data discs directly. The browsing and playback experience is identical to CDDAFS and DVD-Video once the disc is recognised — the only difference is how the disc was mastered."},{"title":"Audio CD (CDDA)","cat":"Storage & network","url":"cd-dvd.html#audio-cd","text":"SMS also plays plain Audio CDs — ordinary Red Book music CDs. Insert a music CD and SMS reads its table of contents, mounts it as an audio disc, and lists its tracks for playback. This is separate from CDDAFS: a CDDAFS disc only looks like an audio CD to sneak data past the copy protection, whereas a genuine Audio CD holds real audio tracks that SMS plays as music. ℹ Audio CD vs data disc — SMS decides for you When a disc goes in, SMS inspects it and routes it correctly on its own: a real music CD becomes an Audio CD you play track by track, while a data disc (CDDAFS, ISO, or DVD-Video) becomes a filesystem you browse. You do not pick a mode — you just insert the disc and browse or play whatever SMS shows."},{"title":"Notes — burning quality, compatibility & what boots","cat":"Storage & network","url":"cd-dvd.html#notes","text":"SMS optical media: CDDAFS, DVD-Video, data discs and audio CD paths, with preserved historical guidance."},{"title":"Burning quality & media","cat":"Storage & network","url":"cd-dvd.html#quality","text":"Burn slowly, on good media. A stock PS2 optical drive — especially an older or well-used one — is far happier reading a carefully burned disc. Use quality blank CD-R / DVD-R media and a conservative write speed. CDDAFS needs Disc-At-Once. Because a CDDAFS disc is written as an audio CD, burn its cue/bin with a Disc-At-Once write mode, not Track-At-Once. Eugene developed and tested with Nero's Disc-At-Once/96. One movie per CD-R is the classic case. A CDDAFS CD-R is small; if you want more media (or larger files) per disc, a DVD-Video disc holds far more. For libraries of media, a rewritable storage device such as USB or MX4SIO is far more convenient than juggling discs."},{"title":"Disc & console compatibility","cat":"Storage & network","url":"cd-dvd.html#compat","text":"Optical playback depends on your drive and how you boot Every PS2 optical drive is a little different, and older drives read burned media less reliably as they age. Beyond the drive itself, how your console is set up to boot homebrew affects what discs it will start. A stock console typically needs a boot method (such as a memory-card exploit) to launch SMS in the first place; solutions like a modchip or a disc-boot exploit (e.g. FreeDVDBoot) change what the console will boot directly from a disc. Those boot mechanics are outside SMS itself — once SMS is running, this page describes what it does with whatever disc you insert. If a burned disc will not read, re-burn slower on fresh media before assuming SMS is at fault."},{"title":"Disc-related settings","cat":"Storage & network","url":"cd-dvd.html#disc-settings","text":"SMS's Device settings include a couple of options that affect the optical drive: Option What it does Disable CD/DVD Turns off SMS's automatic CD/DVD detection, so SMS leaves the optical drive alone. Handy if probing the drive is slow or you never use discs. CD/DVD speed Controls the optical read speed (low / medium / high). A lower speed can help a marginal drive read a burned disc; a higher speed is faster when your media and drive are healthy. SMS also stops the disc spinning while a movie is paused or while you scroll the file list, and stops the drive at startup — small touches that keep the drive quieter and cooler. See the Settings Reference for where these live in the menus."},{"title":"Optical audio out (a bonus)","cat":"Storage & network","url":"cd-dvd.html#passthrough","text":"If your PS2 is connected to a receiver over its S/PDIF optical output, SMS can pass compressed Dolby Digital (AC3) and DTS audio through to that receiver rather than decoding it on the console — useful for films with surround soundtracks played from disc. This is unrelated to CD burning; it is just what SMS can do with a film's audio once it is playing."},{"title":"Which disc type should I use?","cat":"Storage & network","url":"cd-dvd.html#which-disc","text":"CDDAFS Burned CD-R, stock PS2, one movie DVD-Video Burned DVD, more media per disc Audio CD Play a normal music CD Prefer no discs? USB, MX4SIO & more are easier Optical media is SMS's roots and still works on a bone-stock console — but if you have a USB stick, an MX4SIO microSD adapter, an internal HDD or a network share, those hold far more and never wear out a drive. See the Devices Overview for the full picture. A short history: why SMS is a \"system\", not just a player SMS was created by Eugene Plotnikov (2001–2005) to answer one question: could an unmodified PS2 play a good-resolution DivX movie with sound, straight off a burned disc, with no extra hardware? To make that possible he needed three pieces — hence \"system\": CDDAFS (the audio-CD data trick so a burned disc would read), CDXplorer (the PC tool that builds the disc), and the player itself (a heavily PS2-optimised port of parts of FFmpeg, using the console's IPU for colour conversion). The community later added USB, HDD, network, a GUI browser, a skin engine and many more formats — and this fork adds MX4SIO, exFAT HDD, i.LINK, MMCE and on-screen network setup — but the optical-disc heart described on this page is where it all began. See Credits & Sources ."},{"title":"Network Setup","cat":"Storage & network","url":"network.html","text":"SMS static IP, HOST/SMB/UDPFS choices, explicit restart boundaries and folder-served UDPFS setup."},{"title":"Requirements","cat":"Storage & network","url":"network.html#requirements","text":"A fat PS2 with a Network Adapter , or a slim PS2 with built-in Ethernet. A wired Ethernet connection to the same LAN as the server PC. The PS2 has no built-in Wi-Fi. For SMB: a Windows, Samba or NAS host offering an SMB1/CIFS share. For UDPFS: PS2-Servers running its UDPFSD folder server."},{"title":"Set the PS2's static IP on-screen","cat":"Storage & network","url":"network.html#ip-config","text":"Open Device settings → Network settings → Edit IPCONFIG.DAT . Enter the PS2's own IP address, network mask and gateway one octet at a time, then choose Save/Update IPCONFIG.DAT . With a memory card present SMS writes mc0:/SYS-CONF/IPCONFIG.DAT , the same place the PS2 browser and other software expect it. With no memory card fitted it writes IPCONFIG.DAT beside SMS.ELF instead, and reads it back from there on the next boot — so a console with no card can still be given an address. UDPFS requires a valid static PS2 IP; SMB uses the same network configuration."},{"title":"Choose an address","cat":"Storage & network","url":"network.html#choose-an-address","text":"Give the PS2 an unused static address on the same subnet as the server, such as 192.168.1.20 when the PC is 192.168.1.10 ."},{"title":"Enter mask and gateway","cat":"Storage & network","url":"network.html#enter-mask-and-gateway","text":"For a typical home LAN the mask is 255.255.255.0 ; use your router's address as the gateway. Match your actual network."},{"title":"Save","cat":"Storage & network","url":"network.html#save","text":"Choose Save/Update IPCONFIG.DAT . With a memory card fitted the file goes to the card, where the PS2 browser and other titles expect it; with no card fitted SMS writes it beside the ELF instead, and reads it back from there next boot — so a card-less console can still be networked."},{"title":"Choosing a network mode","cat":"Storage & network","url":"network.html#modes","text":"SMS can use the network in three different ways, and the console can only do one of them per boot . They all drive the same single network adapter, and an IOP module cannot be unloaded once it has claimed it — so the choice is settled the moment one starts. Because of that, the three autostart entries in Device settings behave as one setting rather than three switches: Mode What it is for Autostart HOST host: access from a PC running ps2client — mainly a development convenience. Autostart SMB A Windows / Samba / NAS file share. See SMB . Autostart UDPFS A folder served over UDP from a PC. See UDPFS below. Turning one on turns the other two off automatically. Selecting the one that is already lit switches networking off. Previously two could be enabled at the same time, and the second one simply failed with no message — that is no longer possible. If you change the mode while a network stack is already running, SMS offers to restart the network there and then. Accepting reboots the console's I/O processor in place, which takes a moment and leaves SMS running; declining is safe — the choice is saved either way and applies next launch."},{"title":"SMB network shares","cat":"Storage & network","url":"network.html#smb","text":"The SMB browser stores a server address, share, user, password, client name and port. Add or edit the server under Device settings → Network settings → SMB server… using the on-screen keyboard; see SMB Network for the complete field reference. Start it with Start SMB support , or set Autostart SMB to have it come up on every boot. SMB1 is required SMS speaks SMB1/CIFS. Modern Windows disables SMB1 by default, so a Samba/NAS configuration that permits SMB1 is usually safer than enabling an obsolete protocol broadly on a general-purpose PC. Keep this service on a trusted LAN."},{"title":"UDPFS network drive","cat":"Storage & network","url":"network.html#udpfs","text":"UDPFS serves a real PC directory over the UDPFS/UDPRDMA protocol. It is a file-level service: the folder mounts directly as udpfs: . It is not UDPBD, does not require a disk image, and does not appear as massN: ."},{"title":"Configure the PS2 IP","cat":"Storage & network","url":"network.html#configure-the-ps2-ip","text":"Complete the static-IP steps above and save IPCONFIG.DAT ."},{"title":"Serve the folder","cat":"Storage & network","url":"network.html#serve-the-folder","text":"Run the UDPFSD server from PS2-Servers , select the folder to expose, and keep Modulo mode off . The PC and PS2 must be on the same LAN; SMS discovers the server by broadcast, so there is no server IP to enter in SMS."},{"title":"Start the drive","cat":"Storage & network","url":"network.html#start-the-drive","text":"Choose Start UDPFS network drive , or enable Autostart UDPFS and save settings. When discovery succeeds, browse the udpfs: device like any other source. Earlier project hardware report Earlier project documentation reports UDPFS boot, browsing, playback and clean exit on real PS2 hardware. The current documentation audit checked the source/configuration and did not repeat those console runs."},{"title":"Troubleshooting","cat":"Storage & network","url":"network.html#troubleshooting","text":"Check link and subnet. Confirm Ethernet link lights and put the PS2 and server on the same subnet. UDPFS says NOIP. Save the PS2's static IP through Edit IPCONFIG.DAT first. UDPFS discovery fails. Start UDPFSD before retrying, keep Modulo mode off, and allow UDP port 0xF5F6 (62966) through the PC firewall on the trusted LAN. The other network option disappeared. One network stack already owns the NIC. Reboot, then start only the path you intend to use. SMB reaches the host but cannot list or open shares. Check SMB1 availability and the configured credentials. Administrative shares ending in $ are intentionally hidden. SMB Network Share fields and login Settings Reference Autostart and Start-now controls Devices Overview All sources and exclusion rules"},{"title":"SMB Network Share","cat":"Storage & network","url":"smb.html","text":"SMS SMB1/CIFS shares: on-screen configuration, server compatibility, credentials, networking and troubleshooting."},{"title":"Overview","cat":"Storage & network","url":"smb.html#overview","text":"An SMB (Server Message Block) share is just a folder on another computer that has been made available over the local network. Windows calls this \"File Sharing\"; Linux and NAS boxes usually run Samba , an open-source implementation of the same protocol. SMS includes a small SMB1/CIFS client that connects to one of these shares over the PS2's Ethernet port and lets you walk its folders exactly as if it were a local disc — pick a movie and press play, and SMS streams it over the wire. On the device menu the share shows up as a network device alongside your other sources. Once connected, navigation, playback, subtitles and playlists all behave the same as they do for a local device — the only difference is that the bytes are arriving over Ethernet instead of off a disc or card. ℹ SMB needs the network up first The SMB client rides on top of the PS2's TCP/IP stack, so the console needs a working wired network connection — an IP address and a link to your router or switch — before it can reach a share. Configure that on the Network Setup page first; this page assumes the PS2 is already on your LAN. SMB/host and UDPFS require separate IOP sessions SMS's ordinary SMB/host stack and the UDPFS network drive use different drivers for the one Ethernet controller. Whichever starts first owns it until reboot. Enable Autostart SMB — picking it automatically clears Autostart HOST ( ps2dev ) and Autostart UDPFS , so the three can never fight."},{"title":"Requirements","cat":"Storage & network","url":"smb.html#requirements","text":"A PS2 network adapter and a wired Ethernet connection to the same LAN as your PC or NAS. SMS's network support is wired only — there is no built-in Wi-Fi. A PC, NAS, or other device on that LAN that is sharing a folder over SMB1 / CIFS . Because modern Windows and Samba ship with SMBv1 switched off, you generally have to enable the SMB1 feature on the server (or use a device/NAS that still serves it) — this is the single most common reason a share is invisible. The server's IP address on the LAN, the exact share name , and a set of credentials — a username and password, or a guest / anonymous login if the share allows it. The PS2 already on the network with an IP address (see Network Setup )."},{"title":"Setting up the share on your PC or NAS","cat":"Storage & network","url":"smb.html#create-share","text":"This part happens on the computer, not on the PS2. The goal is a shared folder that answers to SMB1 and that you can reach with a known username and password (or as a guest). The exact steps differ by operating system, but the shape is always the same:"},{"title":"Create and share a folder","cat":"Storage & network","url":"smb.html#create-and-share-a-folder","text":"Make a folder to hold your media — for example a \"Movies\" folder — and turn on file sharing for it. On Windows this is Properties → Sharing (or \"Give access to\"); on a NAS it is the box's Shared Folders / SMB service; on Linux it is a Samba [share] entry. Write down the exact share name you give it — SMS needs it spelled the same way, case included."},{"title":"Decide on access: user or guest","cat":"Storage & network","url":"smb.html#decide-on-access-user-or-guest","text":"Either grant a specific username access to the share and set a password for it, or (if your OS allows it and you don't mind) enable guest / anonymous access so no password is needed. Note down whichever you choose — you'll type it into SMS."},{"title":"Make sure SMB1 is being served","cat":"Storage & network","url":"smb.html#make-sure-smb1-is-being-served","text":"On a modern Windows PC, enable the \"SMB 1.0/CIFS File Sharing Support\" Windows feature; on a modern Samba server, allow the old protocol (an SMB1 -friendly min protocol in the Samba config). Many NAS units have a checkbox to allow SMBv1 as well. Without this step the share exists but SMS cannot speak to it."},{"title":"Find the server's IP address","cat":"Storage & network","url":"smb.html#find-the-server-s-ip-address","text":"Note the PC / NAS IP address on your LAN (for example 192.168.1.50 ). SMS connects by IP. Give the machine a fixed / reserved address if you can, so it doesn't change between sessions."},{"title":"Configuring the SMB share in SMS","cat":"Storage & network","url":"smb.html#configuring","text":"Everything below is done on the console with the pad. This fork replaces the old fiddly per-character picker with a real on-screen keyboard , so you type the server address, share and credentials directly — there is no config file to prepare on a PC beforehand. You add a share once; SMS remembers it. ℹ The on-screen keyboard When you open one of the text fields, SMS pops up a full on-screen keyboard: move the D-pad to a character and press Cross to type it, use Circle to backspace and Square for a space, and commit the field with Triangle (or the OK key). It covers digits, upper- and lower-case letters and the common symbols ( . _ - / @ : and more), so full server names, usernames and passwords are all typeable from the pad. Open the SMB / network configuration from SMS's settings, then choose Add server… to create a new entry (or Edit server… to change an existing one). The add/edit form walks top-to-bottom through these fields: Field What to enter IP address The server's LAN address, entered as four numbers. Each octet is set with an on-screen spinner rather than the keyboard — e.g. 192 · 168 · 1 · 50 . Server name A short name for the share/host. Keep it brief — SMS is happiest with short server names. Username The account allowed to access the share. Leave it as your guest login if the share permits anonymous access. Password The password for that account (blank / guest if the share is open). Client name The name the PS2 announces itself as to the server. A default is fine; you can set something recognisable if you like. Port The TCP port SMS connects to. A normal SMB server listens on 445 (or 139 for the older NetBIOS transport); leave the pre-filled default in place unless your server uses a non-standard port, and set it to 445 for a standard Windows / Samba share. Share (optional) The share name you noted on the server. You can leave this blank to browse the server's list of shares, or fill it in to jump straight into one named share. When the fields are filled in, choose the Save Settings item at the bottom of the form to store the entry. SMS keeps the server list in a separate SMS.smb file in the same directory as the general SMS.cfg , so it follows the"},{"title":"Connecting & browsing","cat":"Storage & network","url":"smb.html#connecting","text":"SMS SMB1/CIFS shares: on-screen configuration, server compatibility, credentials, networking and troubleshooting."},{"title":"Bring up the network","cat":"Storage & network","url":"smb.html#bring-up-the-network","text":"With the network configured (and Autostart SMB on, or started on demand), SMS connects to your LAN and then to the SMB server you saved."},{"title":"Select the network device","cat":"Storage & network","url":"smb.html#select-the-network-device","text":"In the top row Device menu , use Left / Right to land on the network / SMB device, then press Down to drop into the file list."},{"title":"Browse the share","cat":"Storage & network","url":"smb.html#browse-the-share","text":"The middle File menu now lists the contents of the share. Move with Up / Down (use L1 / L2 to page through long folders), press Cross to open a folder or start a file, and Triangle to go up one level. The current path shows in the status line at the bottom left."},{"title":"Play","cat":"Storage & network","url":"smb.html#play","text":"Pick a video, track or picture and press Cross . Playback, subtitles and playlists work just as they do from a local device — during playback Select pauses, Start resumes, and Triangle stops and returns you to the browser. Streaming over the network Playback quality depends on your LAN and the file's bitrate. A wired connection to a modern router handles typical SMS video fine, but very high-bitrate files may benefit from copying to a local device (USB, MX4SIO, HDD) instead. If a file stutters over SMB but plays cleanly from a local source, the network is the limiting factor."},{"title":"Troubleshooting","cat":"Storage & network","url":"smb.html#troubleshooting","text":"Symptom Most likely cause & fix The share never appears / SMS can't connect at all SMBv1 is disabled on the server — the number-one cause. Enable \"SMB 1.0/CIFS File Sharing Support\" on Windows, allow the old protocol in Samba, or use a device that still serves SMB1. Can't reach the server, but the LAN seems fine Check the IP address you typed matches the server's current address (it may have changed if it isn't reserved). Confirm the PS2 itself has a valid IP — see Network Setup . Connects, but access is refused Wrong credentials . Re-check the username and password, and confirm that account actually has permission to the share. If you meant to use guest access, make sure the share allows it. Server is unreachable / times out A firewall on the PC is often blocking SMB. Allow file sharing (ports 445 / 139 ) through the firewall, and make sure the sharing service is actually running. Right server, but the folder is empty or wrong The share name is misspelled or points at the wrong folder. Re-open Edit server… and correct the Share field (or clear it to browse the server's share list). ℹ A quick sanity check Before blaming SMS, confirm another computer on the same network can open the share with the same IP, username and password. If a second PC can't reach it either, the problem is on the server (usually SMBv1 or the firewall), not on the PS2."},{"title":"Limitation: SMB1 only","cat":"Storage & network","url":"smb.html#limitation","text":"SMS's network share support is built on the original SMB1 (CIFS) protocol. It does not speak SMB2 or SMB3 , the newer versions that modern operating systems prefer. That is why a stock, up-to-date Windows PC or NAS won't be visible until you re-enable the legacy SMB1 feature on it. Speaking the newer protocols would require a substantial rewrite of the client, so for now the rule of thumb is simple: if the server serves SMB1, SMS can use it; if it only serves SMB2/3, it can't. Why is SMB1 a security concern, and is enabling it safe? SMBv1 is an old protocol with known weaknesses, which is exactly why Microsoft and the Samba project switched it off by default. Do not expose it to the internet. If you choose to use it, isolate the service to a trusted LAN or a dedicated media host. If you would rather not enable SMB1, use the UDPFS network drive or a local source such as USB , MX4SIO or the internal HDD ."},{"title":"See also","cat":"Storage & network","url":"smb.html#see-also","text":"Network Setup Get the PS2 on your LAN first Settings Reference Autostart SMB & where config lives Devices Overview How all the sources compare Troubleshooting When something won't play Changing an already running network personality requires the explicit IOP restart prompt or relaunching SMS. An IOP restart also invalidates other mounted drivers; start the required storage again. See Network Setup ."},{"title":"Settings Reference","cat":"Customize","url":"settings.html","text":"SMS display, device, browser and player settings, language toggle, saved configuration and driver restart behavior."},{"title":"The settings menu at a glance","cat":"Customize","url":"settings.html#menu-map","text":"The top level of the SMS menu has these entries. The first four open the option sub-menus documented on this page; the last four are actions. Top-level entry What it opens / does Display settings… TV system, character set, language, image position, and an Advanced sub-menu for resolution & sync — see below . Device settings… Every storage/network Autostart toggle, the Start … support on-demand items, CD/DVD options and controller options — see below . Browser settings… Appearance of the file browser: background image, sound FX, sorting/filtering, panel colours, the reset-button action and Exit-to target — see below . Player settings… Everything about playback: subtitles, audio/SPDIF, volume, colour resolution, scroll bar, and an MP3 sub-menu — see below . Quick help An on-screen list of the button shortcuts (browser and player). Save settings Writes your configuration to storage — see Saving your settings . Shutdown console Powers the PS2 off. Exit SMS Leaves SMS and returns to the boot browser it was launched from. Labels come straight from the app Every option name and value on this page is taken from the SMS build's own on-screen strings, so it should match what you see on the TV. Wording can differ slightly if you load a translated language file, but the layout and behaviour are the same."},{"title":"Device settings — storage & network","cat":"Customize","url":"settings.html#device-settings","text":"This is the menu you care about most on this fork: it is where you turn storage devices on. Each device has an Autostart toggle (whether it starts automatically at boot), and — when the device is not currently running — a matching Start … support item appears at the bottom of the menu so you can bring it up right now without rebooting."},{"title":"Autostart toggles","cat":"Customize","url":"settings.html#autostart","text":"Each toggle shows on or off . On a clean install (no saved settings) every one of these is off — SMS auto-starts nothing , so you enable only the devices you actually use. Once you save , your choices persist across reboots. Option Device it starts Values Autostart HOST ( ps2dev ) The ps2dev host: stack (ps2client on a PC). One of three mutually exclusive network rows. on / off Autostart SMB The SMB1/CIFS share client. One of three mutually exclusive network rows. See Network Setup . on / off Autostart USB USB mass storage ( massN: ). See USB . on / off Autostart HDD APA The internal HDD via the classic APA/PFS path ( hdd0: ); SMS lists the drive's PFS partitions so you can pick one. See Internal HDD . on / off Autostart HDD BDM The internal HDD via ATA as a BDM massN: device — this is the exFAT path added by this fork. See Internal HDD . on / off Autostart MX4SIO The MX4SIO microSD adapter over SIO2 ( massN: ). The headline feature of this fork. See MX4SIO . on / off Autostart i.LINK An i.LINK (IEEE 1394 / FireWire) drive as a BDM massN: device. See i.LINK . on / off Autostart MMCE An MMCE device (SD2PSX / MemCard PRO2) in the memory-card slot ( mmce0: / mmce1: ). See MMCE . on / off Autostart UDPFS The folder-served UDPFS network drive ( udpfs: ). Mutually exclusive with SMB/host for that boot. See UDPFS . on / off Shared resources — choose one member of each pair MX4SIO and MMCE both use the SIO2 / memory-card-slot bus. Only one can be active at a time — enable the one you're using, not both. HDD APA and HDD BDM both own the internal ATA bus. Pick one; whichever starts first owns the bus. Use HDD APA for a classic APA/PFS drive, or HDD BDM for a modern exFAT drive. Network (SMB/host) and UDPFS load different stacks for the one Ethernet controller. Whichever starts first owns it until reboot. Enable one, not both. See Devices Overview for the full picture."},{"title":"\"Start … support\" — bring a device up now","cat":"Customize","url":"settings.html#start-now","text":"When a device isn't running yet, a matching item appears at the bottom of the Device menu. Selecting it starts that device immediately, so it shows up in the device row without a reboot. Once the device is up, its item disappears from the list. The wording matches each device: Start SMB server support (shown when an SMB server is configured) Start HOST ( ps2dev ) support Start USB support Start HDD support (APA) Start HDD BDM (ATA / exFAT) Start MX4SIO support Start i.LINK support Start MMCE support Start UDPFS network drive The Device menu also ends with a Refresh connections item, which re-probes the running devices — handy after hot-swapping a card or drive so its contents are re-read. ℹ Autostart vs. Start-now Autostart decides what comes up automatically at every boot (and is remembered once saved). Start … support is a one-shot \"turn it on for this session\" that doesn't change the Autostart toggle. A common flow: leave everything off, use Start MX4SIO support to test, then — once happy — flip Autostart MX4SIO on and save ."},{"title":"CD/DVD and controller options","cat":"Customize","url":"settings.html#cd-and-controller","text":"The rest of the Device menu covers the optical drive and controller behaviour, plus the network settings entry that links to the on-screen network setup. Option What it does Values Network settings… Opens Edit IPCONFIG.DAT (the PS2's static IP, mask and gateway), the Network protocol selector, SMB server… — where share entries are added, edited and deleted — and the link parameters: Operating mode , plus Duplex mode and Standard when the mode is manual. See Network Setup . opens sub-menu Controller port 2 Tells SMS what is plugged into controller port 2 so it reads it correctly. gamepad / remote control / none Pair Bluetooth controller Pairs a DualShock 3 or DualShock 4 to the connected USB Bluetooth dongle. The row appears only when the Bluetooth driver is available: connect the dongle, plug the controller into USB, run this command, then unplug the cable and power on the controller. action Disable CD/DVD Turns the optical (CD/DVD) source on or off. Disable it if you never use discs, so SMS doesn't wait on the drive. on / off CD/DVD speed Read speed for the optical drive. low / medium / high Directional buttons Chooses how the D-pad is read — as a standard gamepad or as a remote control. gamepad / remote control"},{"title":"Display settings — TV output & language","cat":"Customize","url":"settings.html#display-settings","text":"Everything about how SMS drives the screen, plus the interface language and character set. Most people set the TV system once and never touch the rest. Option What it does Values TV system Chooses the video standard SMS outputs. NTSC / PAL Character set The code page used when drawing text (menus, file names, subtitles) — pick the one that matches your language/filenames. cycles through the built-in character sets Language English or the installed external SMS.lng pack; this row does not list every translation in the download ZIP. See Languages & Fonts . English / installed external pack Adjust image left / right / up / down Nudge the whole picture to centre it on your TV. Four separate items, one per direction. press to nudge Advanced settings… Opens the advanced display sub-menu below (resolution, colour depth, sync parameters). Only needed for HD/VESA modes or fine tuning. opens sub-menu"},{"title":"Advanced display settings","cat":"Customize","url":"settings.html#advanced-display","text":"These control the raw output resolution and timing. They are for advanced users chasing a specific mode or fixing a picture that doesn't sync — the defaults are fine for standard NTSC/PAL. Changes here only take effect when you run Apply settings at the bottom of the sub-menu. Option What it does Display height The output framebuffer height in scanlines. Display width The output framebuffer width in pixels. Color resolution Framebuffer colour depth (also selectable from the Player menu). See Color resolution . Synch. parameter 1 (video) Fine video-sync timing for the current mode. Adjust only if the picture rolls or is off-centre. Synch. parameter 2 (GUI) The equivalent sync tuning for the on-screen GUI layer. Synch. parameter 3 (Audio) The equivalent sync tuning for audio timing. Apply settings Commits the advanced display changes to the live output so you can see the result. HD modes and the background The 720p/1080i modes deliberately use a gradient instead of the desktop wallpaper because of VRAM use. A gradient in those modes is not the saved-skin restart report. See HD background behavior and the open skin report ."},{"title":"Browser settings — the file browser's look","cat":"Customize","url":"settings.html#browser-settings","text":"These change how the file browser looks and behaves, and what SMS does on the reset button / on exit. Colour items open a small palette picker; the rest are toggles or values. Option What it does Values Skin (shown at the top) The active .smi skin. Shows the skin name, or none . See Themes & Skins . cycles installed skins / none Use background image Whether the desktop background image (skin/artwork) is drawn behind the browser. on / off Sound FX Enables the browser's navigation sound effects. on / off Sort filesystem objects Whether folders/files are shown sorted rather than in raw filesystem order. on / off Filter media files Whether the browser hides files SMS doesn't recognise as playable media, so the list shows only openable files. on / off Display HDL partitions Whether HD Loader-style partitions on the internal HDD are shown in the browser. on / off Hide system partitions Whether the PS2's system partitions on the HDD are hidden from the list. on / off Use exception handler Installs SMS's exception handler (a debugging/robustness aid). on / off Active border color Palette colour of the highlighted (active) panel's border. palette index Inactive border color Palette colour of the inactive panels' borders / info bar. palette index Text color Palette colour of the browser's file-list text. palette index Status line text color Palette colour of the bottom status line (path + clock). palette index Selection bar color Palette colour of the highlight bar on the currently selected item. palette index Reset button action What the PS2 reset button does while SMS is running. cycles actions (incl. exit) Exit to Where Exit SMS sends you — the target program SMS returns to on exit. cycles exit targets ℹ Palette / colour items The colour options ( Active border color , Text color , Selection bar color , and the subtitle colours in the Player menu) pick from SMS's 16-entry palette. Cycle the value to preview each colour live in the panel it affects, so you can match your skin. See Themes & Skins for the palette itself."},{"title":"Player settings — playback, subtitles & audio","cat":"Customize","url":"settings.html#player-settings","text":"The biggest sub-menu. It covers subtitles, audio/SPDIF, volume, the scroll bar, colour resolution, an auto power-off timer, and an MP3 settings sub-menu for audio playback. Toggles are on/off; value items cycle with Left/Right; colour items pick a palette index."},{"title":"Subtitles","cat":"Customize","url":"settings.html#player-subtitles","text":"SMS shows external subtitle files over video. These options control loading, look and placement. Some (offset, font width/height) show a live Sample while you adjust them. See the dedicated Subtitles page for the full walkthrough. Option What it does Values Autoload subtitles Automatically load a matching subtitle file when a video starts. on / off Opaque subtitles Draw subtitles on a solid backing rather than transparent, for readability over busy scenes. on / off Subtitle color Palette colour of normal subtitle text. palette index Subtitle bold color Palette colour used for bold subtitle text. palette index Subtitle italic color Palette colour used for italic subtitle text. palette index Subtitle underline color Palette colour used for underlined subtitle text. palette index Subtitle alignment Horizontal placement of the subtitle text. left / center / right / center-right Subtitle offset Vertical position of subtitles on screen (shows a live Sample). value in points Subtitle font width Horizontal size of the subtitle font (shows a live Sample). value Subtitle font height Vertical size of the subtitle font (shows a live Sample). value Subtitle MBCS Multi-byte character-set handling for subtitles (for languages that need it). on / off"},{"title":"Audio & video output","cat":"Customize","url":"settings.html#player-audio","text":"Option What it does Values S/PDIF - Dolby Digital/DTS Passes AC3/DTS out over the optical S/PDIF port as a bitstream to an external decoder, instead of decoding on the PS2. on / off Video auto 2:2 pulldown Applies 2:2 pulldown handling for correct playback of certain video cadences. on / off Default volume The starting playback volume level. value Color resolution Framebuffer colour depth used during playback / GUI. Higher looks better; lower saves video memory. 16 bit / 32 bit"},{"title":"On-screen extras & scroll bar","cat":"Customize","url":"settings.html#player-ui","text":"Option What it does Values Display scroll bar time Whether the elapsed/remaining time is shown on the player's scroll (progress) bar. on / off Auto power-off Automatically powers the console off after a period of inactivity. off, or a number of minutes Scroll bar length The on-screen length of the player's scroll bar. value Scroll bar position Where the scroll bar sits on screen. top / bottom / off Scroll bar color Palette colour of the scroll bar. palette index Volume bar color Palette colour of the on-screen volume bar. palette index"},{"title":"MP3 settings (audio sub-menu)","cat":"Customize","url":"settings.html#mp3-settings","text":"Opened from the Player settings menu via MP3 settings… , this covers audio and playlist behaviour, including the order of video folders played with Play all → Video . Option What it does Values Audio animation display Whether a visual animation is shown while audio-only files play. See Audio & Playlists . on / off Randomize playlist Shuffle playlists and Play all folders, including Play all → Video . Turn it off to keep the normal play order. on / off Repeat mode Repeat the current playlist/track. on / off Audio spectrum display Show a spectrum-analyser visual during audio playback. on / off Autodetection parameter A tuning value for MP3 format auto-detection. value"},{"title":"Saving your settings — and where they go","cat":"Customize","url":"settings.html#saving","text":"Nothing you change in the menus is permanent until you run Save settings from the top level of the SMS menu. Until then you can change anything and simply not save. There is also a button shortcut on some builds ( Select + Square ) that saves settings from the browser."},{"title":"Make your changes","cat":"Customize","url":"settings.html#make-your-changes","text":"Set your Autostart toggles, colours, subtitle look, and so on in the sub-menus. Try devices with Start … support first if you like."},{"title":"Run Save settings","cat":"Customize","url":"settings.html#run-save-settings","text":"Back out to the top of the SMS menu (Triangle), select Save settings , and confirm."},{"title":"Reboot to confirm","cat":"Customize","url":"settings.html#reboot-to-confirm","text":"Your Autostart choices and preferences come back on the next boot. Configuration location Use Save settings after changes. On a remountable launch source, SMS.cfg normally lives beside the ELF even when a memory card is fitted; SMS.smb follows its resolved directory. See Config Files & Paths for card-slot, network-file and fallback rules. IPCONFIG.DAT has separate card/boot-folder rules. Back up the active config before renaming it to test clean defaults."},{"title":"See also","cat":"Customize","url":"settings.html#see-also","text":"Devices Overview Which device is which, and the bus rules Network Setup On-screen IP, SMB & UDPFS Subtitles Loading, styling & placement Themes & Skins Skins, palette & artwork MX4SIO Enable & play from microSD Troubleshooting When a device or file won't behave Why is there both \"HDD APA\" and \"HDD BDM\" — aren't they the same drive? They are the same physical internal HDD, but reached two different ways. HDD APA uses the PS2's classic APA/PFS filesystem and reads media from the PFS partitions on hdd0: — the traditional SMS route. HDD BDM reads the drive as a modern exFAT volume over ATA, appearing as a massN: device like USB or MX4SIO — the path this fork adds so you can use a normally-formatted drive. They share the internal ATA bus, so only one can be active; whichever starts first owns it. Choose the one that matches how your drive is formatted. See Internal HDD . I enabled Autostart MX4SIO but MMCE stopped working (or vice-versa) That's expected. MX4SIO and MMCE both drive the SIO2 / memory-card-slot bus, so SMS can only run one of them at a time. Turn off the Autostart for the one you're not using, enable the one you are, and save . If you need to switch after one has claimed SIO2, save the intended choice and relaunch SMS. Disabling Autostart does not unload an already running driver. See Devices Overview . Boot-source exception: optional Autostart flags default off, but SMS may start/remount the device holding its ELF/config during startup. A source already visible after first boot does not mean another Autostart choice was saved. See configuration discovery ."},{"title":"Config Files & Paths","cat":"Customize","url":"configuration.html","text":"Where SMS.cfg, SMS.smb, IPCONFIG.DAT, language, skin, palette and font files live for each launch/fallback path."},{"title":"Where SMS.cfg and SMS.smb live","cat":"Customize","url":"configuration.html#boot-folder","text":"Launch source Normal configuration location Memory card, USB/MX4SIO/i.LINK/ATA mass volume, MMCE, PFS/HDD, writable UDPFS SMS.cfg beside the launched ELF. SMS.smb follows the same resolved config directory. SMB, developer host: , optical launch, or a boot mass device that SMS cannot remount after IOP reset Fallback resolution: an available memory card can retain the card config; otherwise SMS searches writable USB/MMCE roots for an existing SMS.cfg , then prefers a USB target, then MMCE. The card path is the final fallback. No usable launch directory The configured memory-card default, normally mc0:/SMS/SMS.cfg (the selected card slot can differ). Examples: mass0:/APPS/SMS/SMS.elf normally uses mass0:/APPS/SMS/SMS.cfg ; mc1:/APPS/SMS.elf normally uses mc1:/APPS/SMS.cfg . BDM mass numbering can change across boots. A UDPFS server must permit writes for config saved there; an unreachable/read-only boot source can cause fallback. SMS remounts filesystem boot devices before its second settings load. That is why a saved skin or display setting may load later than the first boot screen. The October repaint fix addresses that sequence; the reported restart case still needs acceptance ."},{"title":"IPCONFIG.DAT is separate","cat":"Customize","url":"configuration.html#network-files","text":"The network editor saves the PS2's static address/mask/gateway to mc?:/SYS-CONF/IPCONFIG.DAT when the configured memory card is usable. Without a card it uses the ELF's folder. Startup probes the boot-folder copy and the card fallback; a stale boot-folder copy can therefore matter. This file does not simply follow SMS.cfg into every fallback directory. SMS.smb stores SMB server settings, including credentials, beside the resolved SMS.cfg . Keep your personal configuration out of public bug-report attachments or remove credentials before sharing it."},{"title":"Language, skin, font and palette assets","cat":"Customize","url":"configuration.html#assets","text":"Asset Lookup SMS.lng Boot-folder copy first, then mc?:/SMS/SMS.lng . Enable the external language after installing a pack matching the build. Skins/Name.smi Boot-folder Skins/ first, then mc?:/SMS/Skins/ . Names are selected and saved in config. SMS.pal Boot-folder copy first, then the configured memory-card SMS directory. .mbf fonts Scanned in the SMS card/boot locations; select the desired font in the relevant menu. See languages and subtitles ."},{"title":"Save, compare and reset","cat":"Customize","url":"configuration.html#save-reset","text":"Use Save settings after changing autostart, language, skin or playback preferences. If a setting reverts, check the exact launch directory and file timestamp; do not assume another copy on mc0: is active. Before resetting, back up the active SMS.cfg and SMS.smb . Rename the config and relaunch to test defaults. A fresh config disables optional device autostart. When comparing builds, keep a separate config copy for each and record which file each used. Source: main.c , SMS_Config.c , SMS_IOP.c and SMS_GUIDesktop.c . The active target depends on successful remount/probe results."},{"title":"Themes & Skins","cat":"Customize","url":"themes.html","text":"SMS SMI skins and artwork, boot-folder/card lookup, saved-skin testing and deliberate HD gradient behavior."},{"title":"The skin engine","cat":"Customize","url":"themes.html#skin-engine","text":"SMS has always had a lightweight skin engine. The one thing it lets you replace is the desktop background , by pointing SMS at a .smi skin file: File Extension What it changes Desktop skin .smi The full-screen background behind the device menu, file browser and status line — the \"wallpaper\". A .smi is an IPU-encoded image — a compact picture format the PlayStation 2's IPU hardware decodes directly, the same colour pipeline SMS uses to draw video. When a desktop skin is set and loads successfully, its picture is stretched to fill the screen and replaces the default background ; if no skin is set (or a skin file fails to load), SMS falls back to its built-in desktop. ℹ A note on .mbf files Alongside the skin setting you may see a separate .mbf option. Despite living near the skin settings, .mbf is not a background image — it is an optional custom font used to draw text, most useful for subtitle scripts that need characters the built-in font doesn't cover. It changes how text looks, not the wallpaper. Subtitle rendering is covered on the Subtitles page. ℹ Where skins are chosen The desktop skin name is an ordinary SMS setting. You pick it from the on-screen menus alongside the other player options, and — like every other setting — it is written to your saved configuration so it persists across reboots. See Settings Reference for how the settings menu is laid out and how configuration is saved."},{"title":"This fork's look","cat":"Customize","url":"themes.html#this-fork","text":"This build ships with a refreshed desktop and a full set of device icons on top of the classic skin engine. The default artwork is separate from skin loading; the October work also adds skin-input validation and a late-config repaint fix. Compared with older SMS builds you get: A redrawn full-screen desktop background and a matching boot splash shown while SMS starts up. A complete, consistent set of device icons — including a dedicated MX4SIO microSD icon (older builds reused another device's icon for it), plus icons for the internal HDD, MMCE, i.LINK and SMB. Redrawn file and status icons used throughout the browser and menus. These are baked into the program itself, so they are the same on every device you boot from. Applying a custom .smi skin overrides the desktop background on top of them; the icons stay as the built-in set."},{"title":"Applying a skin","cat":"Customize","url":"themes.html#applying","text":"SMS looks for skin files in a Skins folder, and the location depends on how SMS was started:"},{"title":"Use the launch folder first","cat":"Customize","url":"themes.html#use-the-launch-folder-first","text":"On a remountable launch source, including a memory-card boot, SMS first tries Skins/MyTheme.smi beside the ELF. For mc1:/APPS/SMS.elf , that is mc1:/APPS/Skins/MyTheme.smi ."},{"title":"Check the card fallback","cat":"Customize","url":"themes.html#check-the-card-fallback","text":"If the launch-folder skin cannot be read, SMS tries mc?:/SMS/Skins/MyTheme.smi on the configured card slot. See asset lookup order rather than assuming the presence of a memory card changes the primary path."},{"title":"Select the skin, then save","cat":"Customize","url":"themes.html#select-the-skin-then-save","text":"Put the .smi file in the appropriate Skins folder, choose it from the on-screen menu, and save your settings so it is remembered. On the next redraw the desktop background changes to your skin. If a skin doesn't appear Make sure the file has the exact .smi extension and sits directly in the Skins folder SMS is looking in (memory card or boot device, per above), and that the name you selected matches the file name. If a skin file can't be read or isn't a valid IPU image, SMS quietly falls back to its built-in desktop rather than showing an error — so a missing background usually means a wrong path, name or file format."},{"title":"Making a skin","cat":"Customize","url":"themes.html#making-a-skin","text":"Because .smi is an IPU-encoded image and not a plain PNG or JPEG, you don't just rename a picture — the image has to be converted into the IPU format with a converter tool first. The workflow, conceptually, is:"},{"title":"Start from a full-screen image","cat":"Customize","url":"themes.html#start-from-a-full-screen-image","text":"Design your wallpaper at a normal screen resolution. SMS stretches the skin to fill the display, so a 4:3 image sized for standard-definition output gives the most predictable result."},{"title":"Convert it to .smi","cat":"Customize","url":"themes.html#convert-it-to-smi","text":"Run the image through an IPU converter to produce the .smi file. This is the step that turns an ordinary picture into something SMS's IPU decoder can load."},{"title":"Drop it in Skins and select it","cat":"Customize","url":"themes.html#drop-it-in-skins-and-select-it","text":"Copy the finished .smi into the Skins folder as in Applying a skin , pick it on-screen, and save. Why isn't a skin just a PNG or JPEG? The desktop skin loads through the PS2's IPU hardware, which decodes a specific image format rather than PNG/JPEG. Converting your picture to .smi up front means SMS can load and display the background quickly using the same hardware path it uses for video, without doing a general-purpose image decode every time it draws the desktop. The device and file icons in this fork are handled by a separate artwork pipeline and are built into the program, so they aren't part of the .smi skin."},{"title":"Device icons","cat":"Customize","url":"themes.html#device-icons","text":"Every storage source in SMS has its own icon in the top device menu, so you can tell at a glance which device is selected. This fork ships a distinct icon for each: Device Icon shows USB USB mass-storage drive MX4SIO (microSD) A dedicated microSD icon — new in this fork; older builds reused another device's icon Internal HDD Hard-disk drive i.LINK i.LINK / IEEE 1394 storage MMCE (SD2PSX / MemCard PRO2) Memory-card-slot storage device SMB network share Network share CD / DVD Optical disc — data CD or DVD Audio CD (CDDA) Audio disc The disc icon reflects the kind of disc in the drive — a data CD, a DVD and an audio CD are shown differently — so the device menu stays informative even when the same optical drive holds different media. See Devices Overview for how the device menu itself works."},{"title":"HD output deliberately uses a gradient","cat":"Customize","url":"themes.html#hd-known-issue","text":"720p / 1080i background behavior In 720p and 1080i modes SMS uses a plain gradient instead of loading full-screen desktop artwork, because the HD framebuffer consumes the VRAM needed by that texture. Use an SD mode when checking a wallpaper. This deliberate fallback is separate from #27's skin-after-restart report . On a remountable launch path, skins are tried in Skins/ beside the ELF first, even on memory-card boots; the configured card's SMS/Skins/ is a fallback. See asset lookup order . Test skin persistence after saving and relaunching."},{"title":"See also","cat":"Customize","url":"themes.html#see-also","text":"Settings Reference Where skin names live & how config saves Devices Overview The device menu & icons in context Troubleshooting When something looks wrong Credits & Sources Artwork & contributors"},{"title":"Languages & Fonts","cat":"Customize","url":"languages.html","text":"Install and translate SMS language packs: 311 strings, matching downloads, code pages, Spanish and font limits."},{"title":"Install an external language","cat":"Customize","url":"languages.html#install","text":"Download the matching language ZIP . It contains 19 named variants (including an English variant), the base English template and translator guide. Choose a variant such as SMS.lng_ES and rename it SMS.lng . Copy it beside the ELF, or to mc?:/SMS/SMS.lng . A boot-folder copy has priority. Alternatively highlight a language file in the browser and use its import/update action. Enable the external language with Browser settings → Language ; choosing English uses the built-in strings. Select the matching Character set , save settings and check the UI after relaunch. Spanish was updated with Nacheras's submitted strings in the October work. Other variants can retain English placeholders for newer entries. Integrated strings and host checks do not establish that every glyph or long label fits on a console."},{"title":"Encoding matters","cat":"Customize","url":"languages.html#encoding","text":"Menu language files use the player's single-byte code pages, not general UTF-8 text rendering. Use the relevant WinLatin1, WinLatin2, Cyrillic or Greek setting. An external .mbf font is a separate mechanism, particularly for multibyte subtitles. Subtitle encoding is different: the subtitle reader recognizes UTF-8 with a BOM and has its own MBCS/font selection. Do not apply menu-language encoding rules to every SRT. See Subtitles ."},{"title":"Translate safely","cat":"Customize","url":"languages.html#translate","text":"Start with the current English template . Keep all 311 entries in order and do not leave blank entries. Preserve every printf placeholder, including its type, order, width and precision. The loader does not validate all formatting mistakes for you. Keep zero-based entries 166 and 167 as boot paths. Device/protocol names such as MX4SIO, MMCE and UDPFS should remain recognizable. Keep labels short enough for the fixed menu boxes. Check real glyph rendering and clipping after import. The full translator reference explains the fixed positions. If the language does not activate, check file location, pack/build pairing, blank lines and character set before assuming the toggle itself is broken."},{"title":"Report a language issue","cat":"Customize","url":"languages.html#report","text":"Include the build manifest, variant, active file path, character set, affected menu and a photograph of clipping or incorrect glyphs. If sharing an edited pack, retain its original byte encoding."},{"title":"Wiki & Guide Index","cat":"Reference","url":"wiki.html","text":"Complete SMS guide index with the searchable manual, native GitHub wiki and versioned Markdown mirror."},{"title":"Get a file playing","cat":"Reference","url":"wiki.html#start","text":"Getting Started — first file and the three-part browser. Install & Update and Downloads & Releases — standard/packed/DIAG, rolling/stable and matching language packs. Controls — browser, playback, pictures and website shortcuts."},{"title":"Media and conversion","cat":"Reference","url":"wiki.html#media","text":"Video , Audio & Playlists , Pictures and Subtitles . Media Converter — presets, multiple audio tracks, text burn-in, SD dimensions and validation results."},{"title":"Storage and networking","cat":"Reference","url":"wiki.html#storage","text":"Devices Overview — mount names and mutually exclusive driver paths. USB , MX4SIO , Internal HDD , i.LINK , MMCE and CD / DVD / CDDA . Network & UDPFS and SMB Shares . SMS's folder-served UDPFS is distinct from UDPBD and POPStarter BDMA drivers."},{"title":"Configure and customize","cat":"Reference","url":"wiki.html#customize","text":"Settings Reference , Config Files & Paths , Themes & Skins and Languages & Fonts ."},{"title":"Troubleshoot, contribute and preserve history","cat":"Reference","url":"wiki.html#reference","text":"Known Issues & Testing and Troubleshooting . Building from Source and Contributing & Architecture . Credits & Sources and Historical Manual . Historical encoding instructions and downloads are preserved with their context."},{"title":"Wiki sources and maintenance","cat":"Reference","url":"wiki.html#wiki-mirror","text":"The native GitHub wiki and versioned repository mirror are generated from these same article sources, with a sidebar and footer. This searchable site is the primary manual; the Markdown mirror makes the same guides available through GitHub's wiki interface. Documentation maintenance explains generation, link/search validation and native-wiki synchronization. Improve the shared source so fixes reach both formats."},{"title":"Known Issues & Testing","cat":"Reference","url":"known-issues.html","text":"Dated SMS issue status: reproduced fixes, unconfirmed console regressions, converter acceptance and the poster-grid request."},{"title":"MPEG-2/AC3 opening freeze — #29","cat":"Reference","url":"known-issues.html#mpeg","text":"The reporter's MPG freezes at container opening on an SCPH-77004/USB setup and worked in 2.9rev4. Probing, cursor restoration, short-read handling, metadata retries and progress exits have fixes with host regressions. Retest the unchanged reporter file on the same console/source using standard and DIAG builds. Record the last label: MPEG probing is E48 probe mpg ; include its text because diagnostic numbers can overlap other phases."},{"title":"High-bitrate USB stutter — #24","cat":"Reference","url":"known-issues.html#usb","text":"USB stutter remained in the reporter's later test; MX4SIO reduced it and 2.9rev4 played the same USB video smoothly. Allocation/recovery fixes and bounded 32 KiB synchronous refills are implemented. A smaller batch is not asynchronous read-ahead and does not establish a throughput fix. Compare the identical file, source and settings while measuring read time, queue starvation and decode time before selecting another I/O change."},{"title":"Green or red video color — #20 / #23","cat":"Reference","url":"known-issues.html#colors","text":"Green color and red fluctuation have newer paired recordings and converter logs. Conversion success does not establish correct console output. The exact failing MPG bytes/hash are still needed. The MPEG RGB ownership fix removes a reproduced held-buffer overwrite in controlled host/emulator tests. Synthetic raw decoder controls also pass at 512×480 and 640×480 in 16/32-bit modes. These controls exclude the affected console's full display path. Test the unchanged failing MPG, pause/resume, seek, stop and sequence changes on the affected setup. No blanket BT.601/range transform is supplied."},{"title":"MP4 late start and stall — #10","cat":"Reference","url":"known-issues.html#mp4","text":"The reported 0:28 start and 0:35 video/audio-loop stall remain unconfirmed. MOV packet selection, offsets, timing tables, co64/extended atoms, allocation ownership and failed sample reads have host-tested fixes. Obtain the exact original and converted files and test both ordinary and fast-start layouts through the full player. Faster opening alone does not demonstrate that the stall is fixed."},{"title":"Saved skin after restart / Spanish — #27","cat":"Reference","url":"known-issues.html#skin","text":"Skin persistence still needs the reporter's USB save/restart test. The late-configuration repaint and skin bounds/allocation paths were fixed, and the Spanish strings were integrated. Test default PAL/NTSC, a non-default output mode and card/USB launch paths; check Spanish glyphs and label clipping separately. HD gradient behavior is not the same issue."},{"title":"Folder shuffle — #25","cat":"Reference","url":"known-issues.html#shuffle","text":"Folder shuffle is implemented, with failed-open and playlist-lifetime regressions covered on the host. Enable Randomize playlist under Player settings → MP3 settings , then use Play all → Video . Check every eligible item appears once, Stop/next behavior, missing/unreadable files and a large folder on console."},{"title":"Converter tracks and subtitles — #22","cat":"Reference","url":"known-issues.html#converter","text":"Multi-audio, text burn-in, source-SD sizing and the high-quality Xvid/PCM preset are implemented and host-tested. SMS audio switching, SD aspect/display and high-bitrate playback on suitable storage remain acceptance work. Switchable embedded text, sidecar export and bitmap conversion are separate from the implemented burn-in control."},{"title":"Poster / cover-art grid — #26","cat":"Reference","url":"known-issues.html#posters","text":"Poster-grid browsing is unimplemented. A future feature needs artwork naming, fallback/navigation rules, a bounded JPEG cache, lifecycle cleanup, and memory/VRAM and slow-storage testing. The current picture viewer is not a poster browser."},{"title":"How to read validation claims","cat":"Reference","url":"known-issues.html#evidence","text":"Evidence What it establishes Host tests / sanitizers Specific C/Python behavior with fixtures and controlled interfaces; not PS2 scheduling, bus throughput or display output. PS2 target build / ABI checks Compilation, linking and checked module imports; not actual device operation. Controlled PCSX2 probes Only the exercised decoder, buffer, IPU and scheduling paths; not a full console playback result. Real-console comparison The recorded console, source, media bytes, build and settings. Do not generalize a passing profile to every encode or transport. For the detailed reproductions and next-agent work, read the engineering handoff and color-control report . Use the reporting checklist for a new result."},{"title":"Troubleshooting","cat":"Reference","url":"troubleshooting.html","text":"SMS playback, storage, network and config troubleshooting, diagnostic labels and reproducible bug reports."},{"title":"A video stutters, drops frames, or won't play","cat":"Reference","url":"troubleshooting.html#video-wont-play","text":"SMS decodes video in software on the Emotion Engine and uses the PS2's IPU for colour conversion. That is a fixed budget — a file that is too demanding for the console will stutter no matter how fast your storage is. Work through these in order."},{"title":"Confirm the format is supported","cat":"Reference","url":"troubleshooting.html#confirm-the-format-is-supported","text":"SMS plays a specific set of containers and codecs. If a file uses anything outside this list, it will fail to open or may play only its supported audio track. Supported video is AVI (DivX / Xvid / MPEG-4 Part 2), MP4 ( mp4v , MPEG-4 Part 2), and MPEG program streams (MPEG-1/2, .mpg / .mpeg ). H.264/AVC, H.265/HEVC, VP9, AV1 and WMV video are not supported — re-encode those to MPEG-4 Part 2 first. See Video Playback for the full format table."},{"title":"Lower the bitrate and resolution","cat":"Reference","url":"troubleshooting.html#lower-the-bitrate-and-resolution","text":"SMS rejects either video dimension above 1024 pixels , but passing that check does not guarantee real-time playback. Resolution, frame rate, B-frames, QPel/GMC, motion complexity, audio work, bitrate and storage speed all matter. Start from the previously reported 640×480, 30 fps, 1.5 Mbit/s MPEG-4 Part 2 profile and reduce resolution, frame rate or bitrate if needed. If the same file plays from USB but stutters over the network or from MX4SIO, storage throughput or fragmentation is the likely difference."},{"title":"Rule out a damaged or fragmented file","cat":"Reference","url":"troubleshooting.html#rule-out-a-damaged-or-fragmented-file","text":"A partial download, a bad burn, or a heavily fragmented file can cause hitching or a mid-file stop. Re-copy the file to the device (ideally to a freshly formatted or defragmented card/drive), or re-download it, and try again. A PC passing the file does not distinguish a PS2 parser/decoder regression from a demanding encode or storage problem. Keep an unchanged copy and its hash before converting or reorganizing it."},{"title":"Check the encode features","cat":"Reference","url":"troubleshooting.html#check-the-encode-features","text":"Interlaced MPEG-4 and MPEG-2 decode paths exist, but the current hardware fixture baseline is progressive. B-frames, QPel, GMC and interlacing can add work or expose compatibility differences. If a supported file is marginal, re-encode it progressive with no B-frames using the known-good command on the Video Playback page. Audio-only files that won't play The same \"is it a supported format\" rule applies to audio. SMS handles MP3, AAC, AC3, FLAC, OGG Vorbis and WMA . A file with an unusual codec inside a familiar-looking container (for example a lossless track the decoder doesn't recognise) can fail to open — re-encode to plain MP3 to confirm the file, then work back from there."},{"title":"A storage device doesn't appear","cat":"Reference","url":"troubleshooting.html#device-missing","text":"If a device you plugged in isn't showing in the top-row device menu, it's almost always one of three things: it isn't started, it isn't formatted the way SMS expects, or it's blocked by a mutual exclusion."},{"title":"Start the device (or turn on its Autostart)","cat":"Reference","url":"troubleshooting.html#start-the-device-or-turn-on-its-autostart","text":"On a clean install optional Autostart choices are off — this is by design (see below). Open the settings menu and either flip on that device's Autostart toggle (Autostart USB, Autostart MX4SIO, Autostart HDD APA, Autostart HDD flip on that device's Autostart toggle (Autostart USB, Autostart i.LINK, Autostart MX4SIO, Autostart MMCE, Autostart HDD APA, Autostart HDD BDM for ATA/exFAT, then the three mutually exclusive network rows — Autostart HOST ( ps2dev ) , Autostart SMB and Autostart UDPFS , where picking one clears the other two), or use the matching Start … item that appears when a device isn't running yet. Starting it on demand does not need a reboot."},{"title":"Check the filesystem format","cat":"Reference","url":"troubleshooting.html#check-the-filesystem-format","text":"BDM mass devices (USB, MX4SIO, ATA/exFAT) expect a FAT32 or exFAT filesystem. A drive formatted NTFS, HFS+ or ext4 will not mount. Re-format the card or drive as FAT32 (or exFAT for large volumes) and copy your media back. The internal-HDD APA/PFS path is different — that uses a PlayStation 2 PFS partition rather than a PC filesystem; see Internal HDD ."},{"title":"Rule out a mutual exclusion","cat":"Reference","url":"troubleshooting.html#rule-out-a-mutual-exclusion","text":"Three resource pairs cannot both be active in one boot: MX4SIO and MMCE both use the SIO2 / memory-card-slot bus. If one is running, the other won't appear — turn off the one you don't need. The internal HDD APA/PFS path and the ATA/exFAT (BDM) path both own the internal ATA bus. Whichever starts first owns it; pick one. SMB/host and UDPFS load different stacks for the single Ethernet controller. Reboot, then start only the network path you intend to use. ℹ How devices are named BDM mass devices (USB, MX4SIO, ATA/exFAT, i.LINK) all share the massN: namespace, with N counting up from 0. MMCE devices are mmce0: / mmce1: . The internal APA/PFS HDD is hdd0: , and the UDPFS folder is udpfs: . If a device is present but numbered differently than you expect, that's usually just the mount order — check the path shown on the status line at the bottom of the screen."},{"title":"An SMB share won't connect","cat":"Reference","url":"troubleshooting.html#smb-wont-connect","text":"SMS speaks the older SMB1 / CIFS network-share protocol. By far the most common reason a share won't connect is that the server no longer accepts SMB1. Top cause: SMB1 is disabled on the server Modern Windows and Samba ship with SMB1 turned off by default for security. SMS needs it enabled to connect. This is a server-side setting, not something SMS can work around from the PS2. How do I re-enable SMB1 on the server? On Windows: open Turn Windows features on or off and enable SMB 1.0/CIFS File Sharing Support (specifically the client/server sub-features), then reboot. Be aware SMB1 is legacy and considered insecure — only enable it on a trusted local network, and consider a dedicated share for the PS2. On a Samba (Linux/NAS) server: allow the older protocol in the server config (for example by setting the minimum protocol to NT1 ) and restart the service. Consult your NAS or Samba documentation for the exact option, as it varies by version."},{"title":"Verify the address, share name and credentials","cat":"Reference","url":"troubleshooting.html#verify-the-address-share-name-and-credentials","text":"Once SMB1 is on, re-check the server's IP address, the exact share name, and the username/password — all entered on-screen with the built-in keyboard (see Network Setup ). A typo in any of these looks identical to \"server refused\". Case can matter for share names and usernames."},{"title":"Check the PS2's own network settings","cat":"Reference","url":"troubleshooting.html#check-the-ps2-s-own-network-settings","text":"Make sure the PS2 has a valid static IP on the same subnet as the server, saved through Device settings → Network settings → Edit IPCONFIG.DAT , and that the network device is started. If the console can't reach the network at all, no share will connect."},{"title":"Check firewalls and the server's file sharing","cat":"Reference","url":"troubleshooting.html#check-firewalls-and-the-server-s-file-sharing","text":"A firewall on the server (or router) can block the PS2's connection even with everything else correct. Confirm file sharing is enabled on the server and that its firewall allows connections from the PS2's IP on your local network. SMB1 is a legacy protocol The SMB client in SMS is the legacy SMB1 stack, and modernising it to SMB2 is separate work beyond this build. If you can't or won't enable SMB1 on your server, use the UDPFS network drive or a directly-attached device instead."},{"title":"The UDPFS network drive won't appear","cat":"Reference","url":"troubleshooting.html#udpfs-wont-connect","text":"Save the PS2's own static IP through Edit IPCONFIG.DAT ; a NOIP error means this step is missing. Run PS2-Servers UDPFSD on a PC on the same LAN, select the folder to serve, and keep Modulo mode off . Allow UDP port 0xF5F6 (62966) through the PC firewall on the trusted LAN. If SMB/host was already started, reboot before choosing Start UDPFS network drive . The two stacks cannot share the NIC in one boot."},{"title":"Settings don't persist across reboots","cat":"Reference","url":"troubleshooting.html#settings-dont-save","text":"If your Autostart toggles, network details or playback preferences reset every time you power on, the config isn't being saved — or isn't being found on the next boot."},{"title":"Find the active configuration","cat":"Reference","url":"troubleshooting.html#find-the-active-configuration","text":"Use Save settings after changes. On a remountable launch source, SMS.cfg normally lives beside the ELF even when a memory card is fitted; SMS.smb follows its resolved directory. See Config Files & Paths for card-slot, network-file and fallback rules."},{"title":"This fork can save to the boot device instead","cat":"Reference","url":"troubleshooting.html#this-fork-can-save-to-the-boot-device-instead","text":"When SMS remounts its boot device, this fork saves the config next to the SMS ELF on that boot device instead. So a memory-card-less setup can still keep its settings — as long as the boot device is writable and present at each boot."},{"title":"Save after changing settings","cat":"Reference","url":"troubleshooting.html#save-after-changing-settings","text":"Make your changes, then save through the settings menu so they're written out. If a setting only ever holds for the current session, it wasn't committed to storage. ℹ Resetting to a clean state Deleting the config file returns SMS to clean defaults — which means optional Autostart choices are off and every Autostart toggle is off. If SMS is misbehaving after a lot of tweaking, clearing the config is a reliable way to get back to a known-good baseline; you then turn back on only the devices you use."},{"title":"Nothing starts up on the first boot","cat":"Reference","url":"troubleshooting.html#nothing-autostarts","text":"ℹ This is by design On a clean install, optional Autostart flags start off . SMS boots to the desktop with the device menu waiting, and you enable what you need. This is deliberate — it avoids forcing a device (such as an internal HDD) that you may not have, and it keeps the first boot fast and predictable. To bring a device online, either turn on its Autostart toggle in settings (so it comes up automatically on future boots) or use the matching Start \u003cdevice> now item to start it immediately without rebooting. Once you save your settings, your chosen devices will auto-start from then on. This is expected behaviour, not a fault."},{"title":"The screen looks corrupted in HD video modes","cat":"Reference","url":"troubleshooting.html#hd-corruption","text":"Known issue in HD output modes In the two high-definition modes (720p and 1080i) SMS does not draw the desktop wallpaper at all — it paints a plain gradient background instead. This is deliberate: the HD framebuffer leaves no room in VRAM for the full-screen artwork, and forcing it there used to comb the text and leave black bars. Everything else works normally; if you want the artwork, use a standard-definition mode (480i/480p/576p), all of which are unaffected. HD modes use a much larger framebuffer than standard definition, which leaves little room for the full-screen desktop artwork; the result can be a corrupted or garbled background on the desktop. This is a known, open issue in the current theme at HD resolutions. Standard-definition output (480i) is not affected and is the recommended mode until this is resolved. If you do use an HD mode and the desktop looks wrong, switch the video/output mode back to 480i. ℹ Still stuck? If none of the above matches your symptom, note exactly what you did, which device and file were involved, and what you saw on screen (a photo of the TV helps). That detail is what makes a problem fixable — vague \"it doesn't work\" reports are the hardest to act on."},{"title":"Reporting a freeze","cat":"Reference","url":"troubleshooting.html#reporting","text":"If SMS stops responding during startup and a power cycle is the only way out, there is a build that can tell us exactly where it stopped. Simple-Media-System-DIAG.elf , published alongside every release, is the same code with one addition: before each step of startup it prints a short code such as E44 in the status bar, naming the step it is about to perform. If that step never finishes, the code stays on screen . So the last code visible is the thing that hung. It needs no network and no memory card — the codes are drawn directly by the console's graphics hardware, so they still appear when almost nothing else is working. That is the whole point: it reports faults that a log file could not, because nothing was left running to write one."},{"title":"How to use it","cat":"Reference","url":"troubleshooting.html#how-to-use-it","text":"SMS playback, storage, network and config troubleshooting, diagnostic labels and reproducible bug reports."},{"title":"Run it","cat":"Reference","url":"troubleshooting.html#run-it","text":"Boot Simple-Media-System-DIAG.elf the same way you boot the normal ELF."},{"title":"Watch the status bar","cat":"Reference","url":"troubleshooting.html#watch-the-status-bar","text":"Codes appear at the bottom of the screen as startup progresses. On a healthy console they flick past quickly and you end up at the file browser."},{"title":"Note where it stops","cat":"Reference","url":"troubleshooting.html#note-where-it-stops","text":"If it freezes, write down or photograph the last code and its text label shown. A recording is even better — it captures the order they appeared in."},{"title":"Report it","cat":"Reference","url":"troubleshooting.html#report-it","text":"Open an issue with that code, your console model, and what was connected. Include the label as well as the number; numeric phase values can overlap. Use the standard ELF for normal playback — the diagnostic build is not faster or better, it simply talks more."},{"title":"Include a reproducible comparison","cat":"Reference","url":"troubleshooting.html#reproduction-details","text":"The bundle's build-info.json , standard/packed/DIAG choice and exact release/run. Console model, boot method, storage device/format, video mode, cable/display and relevant settings. The unchanged original and converted file, or a short failing sample where you can share one; include a SHA-256, codecs, dimensions, frame rate, bitrate, audio tracks and timestamps. The exact trigger and time of failure. Record pause/seek/stop separately. Compare with 2.9rev4 where the issue reports that version working. Converter settings and logs when applicable. Successful encoding is a host result, not proof of console output. Remove SMB credentials from shared configuration/logs. For a color fault, compare the same bytes and fixed display settings before applying any matrix/range transform. For stutter, compare sources and record timing before assuming fragmentation or bitrate is the cause. Current fixes and unconfirmed reports show which evidence is still missing."},{"title":"Quick reference","cat":"Reference","url":"troubleshooting.html#quick-reference","text":"Symptom Most likely cause Fix Video stutters / drops frames Bitrate or resolution too high for the console (worse on slow devices) Re-encode lower; use a faster device Video won't open at all Unsupported codec, or damaged / fragmented file Re-encode to MPEG-4/XviD; re-copy the file Device not in the menu Not started, wrong format, or mutual exclusion Autostart / Start-now; format FAT32/exFAT; free the shared bus MX4SIO and MMCE clash Both use the SIO2 slot bus Run only one at a time HDD APA and ATA/exFAT clash Both own the internal ATA bus Pick one; first to start owns the bus SMB share won't connect SMB1 disabled on the server Re-enable SMB1; then check IP / credentials / firewall UDPFS drive won't appear No static PS2 IP, no server response, firewall, or SMB already owns the NIC Save IPCONFIG; run UDPFSD with Modulo off; allow UDP 62966; reboot to switch stacks Settings reset each boot Wrong active config, failed write/remount, or unsaved changes Check the actual boot-folder/fallback path , save and compare timestamps Nothing auto-starts Clean-install default Enable Autostart for the devices you use No wallpaper in 720p/1080i Deliberate VRAM-saving gradient Use an SD mode for skin checks; report actual corruption separately"},{"title":"Building from Source","cat":"Reference","url":"building.html","text":"Build SMS with its existing GCC/Docker toolchain, separate target variants and embedded IOP module requirements."},{"title":"Prerequisites","cat":"Reference","url":"building.html#prerequisites","text":"Docker running on your host and a source checkout, including irx/ , artwork and fonts. The known-working ps2dev/ps2dev:v1.0 image for the application, using EE GCC 3.2.3. Do not silently replace it with a current SDK or LLVM build. A separate checkout/build directory when comparing variants. Changing make flags without cleaning can reuse objects built for a different variant."},{"title":"The pinned application toolchain","cat":"Reference","url":"building.html#toolchain","text":"The old Alpine image can compile SMS, but its musl runtime cannot run modern GitHub Actions JavaScript steps. CI checks out and uploads on the host and runs only make inside Docker. A tag selects the expected toolchain; verify actual output hashes rather than assuming a tag alone guarantees every future build is identical."},{"title":"Standard and packed builds","cat":"Reference","url":"building.html#build","text":"Build SMS with its existing GCC/Docker toolchain, separate target variants and embedded IOP module requirements."},{"title":"Linux / macOS / Git Bash","cat":"Reference","url":"building.html#posix","text":"git clone https://github.com/NathanNeurotic/Simple-Media-System.git cd Simple-Media-System docker run --rm -v \"$PWD\":/src -w /src ps2dev/ps2dev:v1.0 \\ sh -c 'apk add --no-cache build-base git zip && make all pack'"},{"title":"Windows PowerShell","cat":"Reference","url":"building.html#powershell","text":"docker run --rm -v \"${PWD}:/src\" -w /src ps2dev/ps2dev:v1.0 sh -c 'apk add --no-cache build-base git zip && make all pack' Run the PowerShell command from the repository root. Shell quoting and line continuation differ from POSIX/cmd.exe; do not copy the backslash-continued command into cmd.exe unchanged. Outputs are bin/SMS.elf and bin/SMS-packed.elf . BDM defaults to enabled; that is the distributed full-device player."},{"title":"Diagnostic and compatibility variants","cat":"Reference","url":"building.html#variants","text":"In a disposable build checkout, clean between variants, then use the same container setup with: make clean make SMS_DIAG=1 all # Save/copy bin/SMS.elf before cleaning again. make clean make BDM=0 all SMS_DIAG=1 adds on-screen diagnostic labels. BDM=0 verifies the legacy build still links; it is not the standard rolling download. Cleaning removes that checkout's build outputs, so keep any binaries or symbols you need before running it."},{"title":"Embedded IOP modules","cat":"Reference","url":"building.html#iop","text":"The Makefile converts committed irx/*.irx.xz into embedded arrays. SMS decompresses and starts them on the IOP. Normal installation/building does not require a separate IRX build or copying loose modules next to the player. The modules include iomanX/fileXio, BDM/FAT/exFAT, USB, MX4SIO, ATA, i.LINK, MMCE, card/pad support and network stacks. Consult the actual Makefile for the selected objects; the enabled stack depends on the build flags and runtime choices."},{"title":"Rebuilding the MX4SIO driver","cat":"Reference","url":"building.html#mx4sio-driver","text":"The embedded MX4SIO module carries a local driver patch . Preserve its watchdog, bounded SIO2 waits, batch limit and partial-read progress handling. Patch notes describe the rebuild; the historical investigation records the original reproducer and earlier read strategy. An IOP module's compiler/SDK requirements can differ from the EE application. In particular, the UDPFS module build uses its own script/toolchain and must retain SMS's dev9 import ordinals. See IOP and network development . Do not replace compressed modules with arbitrary SDK builds."},{"title":"Build and publication workflow","cat":"Reference","url":"building.html#ci","text":"Trigger Result Push / PR Host regressions, ABI checks, standard/packed/DIAG and separate legacy builds; downloadable per-run artifacts. Successful master push Updates the rolling player assets. Separate converter jobs update Windows, Win7 and Linux assets on the same release. v* tag Versioned release publication. Master test-media workflow Regenerates synthetic media. This does not execute console playback. Documentation changes Checks shared generation, local links/anchors, search targets and browser-script syntax. Pages serves master:/docs . See host test prerequisites and the workflow sources . A successful compile is not a device test. Keep the build manifest, symbols and exact media when reporting results."},{"title":"Next steps","cat":"Reference","url":"building.html#next","text":"Architecture and contributing · Known issues and acceptance · Build identity and downloads ."},{"title":"Contributing & Architecture","cat":"Reference","url":"developers.html","text":"SMS repository architecture, host tests, IOP ABI constraints, converter development, docs generation and wiki maintenance."},{"title":"Repository map","cat":"Reference","url":"developers.html#layout","text":"Path Responsibility src/ , include/ EE player, UI, config, container parsers, codecs, file contexts and rendering. iop/ , irx/ IOP module sources and embedded/compressed modules. Their build tools and import ABIs can differ from the EE application's toolchain. images/ , theme/ , fonts/ Embedded artwork and fonts. A user .smi skin does not replace every icon. tools/test_*.py Actual-source host regressions and controlled failure tests. tools/mpeg_color_probe/ Explicit decoder/frame-pool controls with documented scope. tools/sms_converter/ Python converter, Qt compatibility layer, output validator and FFmpeg tests. docs-site/ , docs/ , wiki/ Maintained article sources, published static site and generated native-wiki mirror."},{"title":"Media and ownership boundaries","cat":"Reference","url":"developers.html#pipeline","text":"The file context supplies bytes to a container probe/parser. Supported streams feed packet rings; decoder threads publish frame pointers or audio data to the renderer. MPEG-1/2 reuses RGB buffers only after renderer completion; size changes wait before replacing allocated frames. Shutdown distinguishes allocated pointer slots from notification counts and respects subtitle ownership transfers. When changing this path, test failure cleanup, partial reads, stalled input, queue reservations, seek/reset, pause/resume, sequence changes and final stop. Preserve packet alignment and limits. A test that replaces storage or display with a stub does not validate the replaced subsystem."},{"title":"Build and test","cat":"Reference","url":"developers.html#build-tests","text":"Use the existing GCC build instructions . The application uses ps2dev/ps2dev:v1.0 , EE GCC 3.2.3; this documentation update does not migrate it. Standard, packed, DIAG and BDM=0 compatibility builds have different purposes. python3 tools/test_mpeg_ps_probe.py python3 tools/test_mpeg12_frame_lifetime.py python3 tools/test_player_video_drain.py python3 tools/test_mov_parser.py python3 tools/irx_imports.py --assert dev9=1.1 irx/udpfs_smap.irx.xz irx/ata_bd.irx.xz Host C harnesses need a supported host compiler and sanitizer runtime; the MOV parser fixture test also needs FFmpeg/FFprobe. The compilation workflow lists all 17 current host regressions. Do not submit a physical-console program merely to check whether the console is online."},{"title":"IOP and network changes","cat":"Reference","url":"developers.html#drivers","text":"SMS embeds a dev9 v1.1 provider. IOP imports bind by library/major and ordinal, so an apparently successful module load can still bind the wrong functions. Run the ABI gate on the compressed bytes actually embedded. Keep local changes in UDPFS attribution/build notes and SMB module notes . SMB/HOST and UDPFS own different Ethernet stacks. Changing modes requires the explicit IOP restart/relaunch boundary; restarting can invalidate storage mounts. Avoid changing global fileXio async mode without a demonstrated ownership and cancellation design."},{"title":"Converter development","cat":"Reference","url":"developers.html#converter","text":"Keep modern PySide6 and Win7 PySide2 paths consistent. Preserve Python 3.8 compatibility in shared code and retain the Win7 bootloader pins. The Windows FFmpeg setup action uses a retained, SHA-checked fallback archive; refresh that pin before September 2028. Do not turn an encoder/host-package fault into a PS2 SDK defect. Run unit, real-FFmpeg multitrack/worker and preset tests appropriate to a converter change. PASS/WARN/FAIL describe inspected output constraints, not playback certification. Bounded packet scanning must disclose incomplete inspection."},{"title":"Documentation and wiki workflow","cat":"Reference","url":"developers.html#documentation","text":"Edit docs-site/content/*.html , the manifest and shared assets, then generate both the static site and the Markdown mirror: python docs-site/build.py python docs-site/build.py --check python -m http.server 8000 --directory docs GitHub Pages continues publishing master:/docs ; a new hosting service is unnecessary. Search also loads from a local script, allowing the generated site to work without a search service. Review the maintainer workflow before adding routes, changing shared navigation or syncing the native wiki."},{"title":"Useful contributions","cat":"Reference","url":"developers.html#contribute","text":"Start with the open issue map . Include the trigger, exact media identity, before/after result, test limits and relevant logs. Preserve unrelated dirty work and historical manuals. For format or performance claims, identify the codec settings and transport, not just the extension or filename."},{"title":"Credits & Sources","cat":"Reference","url":"credits.html","text":"SMS authors, fork contributors, component provenance, documentation theme sources and evidence references."},{"title":"Built on twenty years of shared work","cat":"Reference","url":"credits.html#built-on-twenty-years-of-shared-work","text":"SMS began as one developer's quest to play good-looking video on an unmodified PlayStation 2, and became a community effort spanning USB, HDD, network, a GUI, a skin engine and a long list of formats. This fork adds storage breadth — MX4SIO playback, exFAT, i.LINK, MMCE, SMB and UDPFS — plus DualShock 3/4 support. None of it would exist in isolation."},{"title":"The foundation","cat":"Reference","url":"credits.html#foundation","text":"The heart of SMS is Eugene Plotnikov 's original media player and the years of ps2homebrew community work that modernised it. This fork's contribution is a focused one — making MX4SIO microSD playback work reliably and adding several more storage devices — but it rests entirely on that shared foundation. Eugene Plotnikov The original author of Simple Media System (2001–2005). He built the PS2-optimised, FFmpeg-derived video/audio player that uses the console's IPU for colour conversion, and devised CDDAFS — the technique for storing data on an audio CD so an unmodified PS2 accepts a burned disc. Everything SMS is began here. The ps2homebrew community The maintainers and contributors who adopted and modernised SMS over many years — adding USB, internal-HDD, network (SMB) and playlist support, a GUI file browser, the .smi skin engine, and support for many more container and codec formats. The PS2SDK, the IOP module ecosystem, and the toolchain this fork builds with all come from that same open, community-maintained work. KrahJohlito Provided the BDM + MX4SIO integration foundation this fork builds on: loading the block-device (BDM) module stack, the memory-card/pad rework that a shared SIO2 bus requires, and the device-menu wiring that lets MX4SIO appear as a first-class device. This groundwork is what made the playback fix possible to land. El_isra, Ripto & Berion Additional work and testing around the MX4SIO effort. Real-hardware testing and feedback are what turn a build that merely compiles into one that actually plays — and this fork's MX4SIO support was shaped by that hands-on QA. bbsan2k Author of the MMCE (Memory Card Mass Storage) protocol — the protocol behind loading files from an SD2PSX or MemCard PRO2 through the PS2's memory-card slot. This fork's mmce0: / mmce1: device support builds directly on that work. See MMCE . rickgaiser, Maximus32 & the neutrino contributors The UDPFS / UDPRDMA , ministack and SMAP foundation vendored from neutrino . The working load sequence was cross-checked against wLaunchELF-R3Z . SMS carries documented local integration changes while preserving upstream attribution. The Open PS2 Loader contributors The DS3/DS4 USB and B"},{"title":"What this fork contributes","cat":"Reference","url":"credits.html#what-this-fork-adds","text":"To be precise about which parts are new here versus inherited: this fork's own work is the storage-breadth layer and the playback fix that headlines it. At a user level, that means: MX4SIO microSD playback — video and audio now play smoothly from a microSD in an MX4SIO adapter, with a hardened driver that keeps playback reliable under a media player's load. See MX4SIO . exFAT internal HDD over ATA — modern exFAT drives read alongside the classic APA/PFS APA/PFS partitions. See Internal HDD . i.LINK (IEEE 1394) storage — a FireWire drive as a media source. See i.LINK . MMCE devices — browsing media from an SD2PSX or MemCard PRO2 in the memory-card slot. See MMCE . UDPFS network drive — a folder-served network filesystem at udpfs: , distinct from UDPBD. See Network Setup . On-screen network & SMB setup — configure your IP and connect to a share entirely from the pad, no hand-edited config files. See Network Setup . DualShock 3 / 4 support — wired USB and Bluetooth input, with controller pairing inside SMS. MPEG-4 Part 2 MP4 playback — mp4v video with AAC audio, covered by the hardware test pack. Settings that save anywhere — configuration persists to the memory card or to the device SMS booted from. See Settings . A refreshed theme — new desktop artwork and device icons, on the existing skin engine. See Themes & Skins ."},{"title":"Testing & diagnosis","cat":"Reference","url":"credits.html#testing","text":"Some of the most valuable work on this fork was not writing code. Homebrew runs on hardware nobody has all of, and a fault nobody can reproduce is a fault nobody can fix. These people supplied the missing half. Nadwiślański (Berion) — the fork's graphics and interface work, and the sustained visual feedback behind the current theme, icons, splash and menu layout. GhostTownUS — systematic hardware testing on PS2 and PSX (DESR), with recorded sessions rather than descriptions. Traced the memory-card-less IP problem, and the screen capture that localised the PSX boot hang to a single step. Also spotted that wLaunchELF R3Z keeps its boot driver loaded where SMS resets the IOP — which is why config beside the ELF had to be read at a different point in boot. israpps — root-caused the PSX boot failure from a DESR‑5000 ROM dump: the DESR BIOS has no rom0:LIBSD , only the XMB and testmode variants. That single observation turned an unreproducible hang into a one-line diagnosis, and is why SMS now ships its own sound driver instead of relying on the console's. TnA — encoder expertise behind the Media Converter: multi-pass encoding, the distinction between B‑frames and the keyframe interval, and the push that led to real Xvid being used where the preset claimed it."},{"title":"From the original SMS thanks","cat":"Reference","url":"credits.html#from-the-original-thanks","text":"The original SMS README records the people and projects Eugene Plotnikov thanked during the project's early development. A few relevant to what SMS is today: Credited For Marcus R. Brown The early exploit work that made PS2 homebrew possible in the first place The PS2Reality team Inspiration and timer code ffmpeg contributors The decoder heritage SMS's player is derived from Chris \"Neovanglist\" Gilbert GS and DMA code gawd The audsrv audio server SMS was created by Eugene Plotnikov (copyright 2001–2005, ps2dev) and is documented by the ps2homebrew community. See the original project's SMS documentation for its full history."},{"title":"A note on trademarks & affiliation","cat":"Reference","url":"credits.html#trademarks","text":"ℹ A community project, unaffiliated with Sony Simple Media System is a free, open-source, community project. PlayStation 2 is a trademark of Sony Interactive Entertainment; this project and this fork are unaffiliated with Sony and are not official Sony software. Device and product names such as SD2PSX , MemCard PRO2 and MX4SIO belong to their respective owners and are referenced only to describe the hardware SMS works with."},{"title":"Sister documentation","cat":"Reference","url":"credits.html#sister-sites","text":"This site is one of a small family of PlayStation 2 homebrew documentation projects. If you are here for SMS you may also want: POPStarter docs ↗ PS1 / POPSTARTER reference RiptOPL docs ↗ Open PS2 Loader fork reference Why is this a fork, and where does the canonical SMS live? The community-maintained SMS project lives with the ps2homebrew organisation, and its documentation is at ps2homebrew.github.io/SMS . This documentation covers a downstream fork focused on storage breadth — it makes MX4SIO microSD playback work and adds exFAT/ATA HDD, i.LINK, MMCE and UDPFS devices, moves network and SMB setup fully on-screen, adds DualShock 3/4 support, and refreshes the look. It is a labour of love built on the work of everyone named above; the long-term home of SMS remains the community project."},{"title":"October continuation and documentation","cat":"Reference","url":"credits.html#october-contributors","text":"eggcatroll — the parser, stream, skin, playlist and regression continuation incorporated before PR #30 merged. Nacheras — updated Spanish strings and skin/language feedback. tktagmedia — the reported high-resolution MPEG-2/MX4SIO conversion profile, retained with its settings and limits. Issue reporters supplied videos, logs, hardware comparisons and reproductions that remain essential to acceptance. The documentation theme shares the black/cyan PS2 design of the Launchstation , POPSLoader and RiptOPL sites. Existing SMS artwork is reused; the original manual/XML, screenshots, historical downloads and original README/changelog sections are preserved."},{"title":"Source and evidence map","cat":"Reference","url":"credits.html#source-audit","text":"Use Known Issues & Testing for the dated report status, developer notes for code/test boundaries and the documentation audit record for the page-by-page coverage. A previous documentation claim of hardware success is retained as a historical report unless this audit has direct repeat evidence."},{"title":"Legacy Documentation Archive","cat":"Reference","url":"legacy-manual.html","text":"Preserved SMS DocBook and MPEG-2 encoding material, clearly distinguished from the maintained manual."},{"title":"Preserved encoding guide","cat":"Reference","url":"legacy-manual.html#preserved-guide","text":"The one complete legacy guide retained in this repository is pitrz's high-resolution MPEG-2 encoding guide . Treat its encoder instructions as historical; use the current Video Playback page for SMS's actual playback limits."}];
